B.aBY=s(["Counts are selectable model parts, including segments, groups and alternative references. They are not counts of unique whole organs.","Neither body reference is an exhaustive anatomy atlas. The female reference has partial musculoskeletal coverage.","Lymphatic references do not include the complete distributed lymph-vessel network.","Sensory detail includes a combined labyrinth, not separately segmented cochlear microanatomy.","Skin is modeled at body and organ scale, without complete follicle or sweat-gland microanatomy.","Detail scenes keep separate source coordinates and are not superimposed on the body references.","Per-structure teaching review metadata is missing. Automated/source checks do not establish medical accuracy or human subject-matter approval. Unknown anatomical IDs remain unreviewed."],t.s)
B.aEO=s(["Represent rational quantities as fractions.","Operate with signed fractions.","Check signs and magnitude."],t.s)
B.b4n={"\u22121/2 + 3/4":0,"\u22122/3 \xd7 3/4":1,"\u22123/5 \xf7 9/10":2}
B.aTT=new A.au(B.b4n,["\u22122/4 + 3/4 = 1/4","\u22126/12 = \u22121/2","\u22123/5 \xd7 10/9 = \u22122/3"],t.w)
B.aAm=s(["\u22121/2 + 3/4 equals what?|1/4|\u22121/4;1/2;5/4|Change negative one half to negative two fourths.","\u22122/3 \xd7 3/4 equals what?|\u22121/2|1/2;\u22122/7;\u22123/2|The product is negative six twelfths.","\u22123/5 \xf7 9/10 equals what?|\u22122/3|\u221227/50;2/3;\u22123/2|Multiply by ten ninths and simplify.","Two negative nonzero factors produce what sign?|Positive|Negative;Zero;Undefined always|The two sign reversals produce a positive product.","0.125 equals which fraction?|1/8|1/4;1/5;1/125|125 thousandths simplifies to one eighth.","The infinitely repeating decimal 0.333... equals what?|1/3|3/10;33/100;1/30|A repeating third is rational, unlike a finite truncation.","2/7 + (\u22122/7) equals what?|0|4/7;2/7;\u22124/7|Opposite numbers add to zero.","Which denominator is forbidden for a rational-number representation?|0|1;2;\u22123|Division by zero is undefined.","\u22121/4 + 5/8 equals what?|3/8|1/2;\u22123/8;7/8|Add negative two eighths to five eighths.","(\u22122/3)(\u22123/5) equals what?|2/5|\u22122/5;1/5;\u22121/5|Two negatives multiply to positive six fifteenths.","Which is larger: \u22123/4 or \u22121/4?|\u22121/4|\u22123/4;They are equal;Neither is rational|Negative one fourth lies closer to zero and farther right."],t.s)
B.a7Q=new A.cu("Rational Numbers",B.aEO,"A rational number is a ratio of integers with a nonzero denominator.","Fractions such as \u22123/4, integers such as five, and terminating or repeating decimals are rational. Use common denominators for addition: \u22121/2 + 3/4 = \u22122/4 + 3/4 = 1/4. Multiply numerator products and denominator products; one negative factor makes a negative product, while two make a positive product. Dividing by a nonzero fraction multiplies by its reciprocal. A zero divisor remains undefined.",B.aTT,"Compute \u22123/5 \xf7 9/10. Step 1: verify the divisor is nonzero. Step 2: multiply by reciprocal 10/9. Step 3: obtain \u221230/45. Step 4: simplify to \u22122/3 and check its negative sign.","Find \u22121/4 + 5/8 and (\u22122/3)(\u22123/5).","The sum is \u22122/8 + 5/8 = 3/8. The product is 6/15 = 2/5, positive because both factors are negative.","Keep signs while finding common denominators. Flip the divisor for division, not the dividend. A fraction denominator cannot be zero.","Rational-number operations combine fraction rules with sign rules and nonzero-divisor restrictions.",B.aAm)
B.aCX=s(["Solve equivalent-ratio equations.","Recognize a constant rate.","Distinguish proportional relationships from offsets."],t.s)
B.b6v={"5 notebooks \u2192 80 credits":0,"8 notebooks \u2192 128 credits":1,"10 notebooks \u2192 160 credits":2,"0 notebooks \u2192 0 credits":3}
B.aVE=new A.au(B.b6v,["80/5 = 16 credits per notebook.","128/8 = 16 credits per notebook.","160/10 = 16 credits per notebook.","No fixed fee: C = 16n passes through the origin."],t.w)
B.aJv=s(["2/3 = x/12 gives x what?|8|6;4;18|Scale both terms by four.","x/4 = 6/8 gives x what?|3|6;12;2|Six eighths equals three fourths.","Which rule is proportional?|y = 3x|y = 3x + 2;y = x\xb2;y = 3 + x|The ratio y to x stays three.","For y = 4x, constant of proportionality is what?|4|x;0;1/4|Four is the multiplier of x.","A direct-proportion graph must include what point?|(0, 0)|(1, 0);(0, 1);(4, 1)|Zero input gives zero output.","Why is y = 2x + 1 not directly proportional?|It includes a nonzero fixed offset|Its slope is positive;It uses numbers;It is a line|The extra one changes y divided by x.","Three items cost forty-two at a fixed rate. One costs what?|14|39;45;126|Divide the total by three equal items.","Which checks 2/3 = 8/12?|2 \xd7 12 = 3 \xd7 8|2 + 12 = 3 + 8;2 \xd7 3 = 8 \xd7 12;2 \u2212 3 = 8 \u2212 12|Equal cross-products verify valid ratios.","Five notebooks cost eighty at a fixed rate. Eight cost what?|128|83;100;160|Each costs sixteen, so eight cost 128.","If y = 5x and x = 7, y is what?|35|12;2;57|Multiply seven by the constant rate five.","A shop charges 16 credits per notebook plus a fixed 20-credit order fee. For eight notebooks, which total and relationship are correct?|148 credits; not directly proportional|128 credits, directly proportional;148 credits, directly proportional;288 credits, not directly proportional|C = 20 + 16 \xd7 8 = 148 credits. At zero notebooks the rule still gives a 20-credit fee, so total cost is not directly proportional to count."],t.s)
B.a88=new A.cu("Proportions",B.aCX,"A proportion states that two ratios are equal.","Start here: can you simplify 6/8 to 3/4 and divide 80 into five equal groups? These fraction and division skills help us find a constant rate.\n\nImagine a shop selling identical notebooks. Five cost 80 credits and ten cost 160 credits. Does buying three more always add just three credits? A ratio compares quantities by multiplication, not by adding the same number to both.\n\nIn 2/3 = x/12, the denominator scales by four, so x = 8. Cross-products provide another check: 2 \xd7 12 = 3x. Denominators must be nonzero. A proportional relationship has form y = kx with constant k, and its graph passes through the origin. The rule y = 2x + 1 is not proportional because its ratio y/x changes and it gives y = 1 at x = 0.\n\nFor a fixed price with no extra fee, C = kn: total cost C in credits equals the rate k in credits per notebook times the number n of notebooks. The formula applies because every additional notebook has the same price. A discount or fixed order fee would require checking the model again. The table below compares matching units; graph the pairs (0, 0), (5, 80), (8, 128) and (10, 160) with notebook count horizontal and cost vertical. They lie on one straight line through the origin.",B.aVE,"Solve x/4 = 6/8. Step 1: simplify 6/8 to 3/4. Step 2: compare matching denominators. Step 3: x = 3. Step 4: check 3 \xd7 8 = 4 \xd7 6.\n\nApply the same idea to a hypothetical shop: five notebooks cost 80 credits at a fixed price with no fee. Find the cost C of eight notebooks.\n\nStep 1 \u2014 name the quantities. Known: 5 notebooks cost 80 credits; wanted: C credits for 8 notebooks. We assume identical notebooks at one unchanged unit price.\n\nStep 2 \u2014 choose the relationship. Equal unit prices give C/8 = 80/5, with both sides in credits per notebook. Multiply both sides by the nonzero count 8: C = (80/5) \xd7 8. Equality is preserved because both sides receive the same operation.\n\nStep 3 \u2014 calculate with units. k = 80 credits / 5 notebooks = 16 credits per notebook. Then C = (16 credits/notebook) \xd7 8 notebooks = 128 credits; notebook units cancel.\n\nStep 4 \u2014 check. 128/8 = 16 and 80/5 = 16, so the rates agree. Eight notebooks are more than five but fewer than ten: 128 is sensibly between 80 and 160 credits. Scaling both counts and costs by 8/5 gives the same result.","If five identical notebooks cost eighty credits at a fixed unit price, what do eight cost? State the proportionality constant.\n\nHint 1: first find the cost of one notebook. Hint 2: keep credits per notebook on both sides of your proportion. Hint 3: multiply the unit price by the requested count, then check against the table.\n\nTry independently before revealing: a different hypothetical shop uses the same 16-credit price but adds a fixed 20-credit fee to every order. Write its total cost rule and find the total for eight notebooks. Is total cost directly proportional to count? Explain using the total at zero notebooks in this mathematical model.","The unit price is sixteen credits per notebook. Eight cost 128; k = 16 in cost = 16 \xd7 count.\n\nIndependent transfer: C = 20 + 16n, so eight notebooks cost 20 + 16 \xd7 8 = 148 credits. The 20-credit fee is added once per order, not once per notebook. At zero notebooks the rule gives 20 credits instead of zero, so total cost is not directly proportional to notebook count. For five and eight notebooks the total-cost ratios are 100/5 = 20 and 148/8 = 18.5 credits per notebook; they are not equal. The notebook-only part still has a constant rate of 16.\n\nFor written solutions, 128, 128.0 or an equivalent fraction represent the same numerical cost when labeled credits; 16 credits per notebook describes the rate, not the total. In the app, select the complete supplied answer. A complete explanation names the fixed-price assumption, shows the rate or a valid proportion, includes units and checks the result.","An equal difference alone does not prove a proportion. Cross-multiplication requires valid nonzero denominators, and a graph through the origin alone must also follow a constant straight-line rate.\n\nDiagnose this claim: 'Five notebooks cost 80, so eight cost 83 because both numbers increase by three.' The error is adding equal amounts rather than keeping the unit price fixed. Check: 83/8 = 10.375 credits per notebook, not 16. Correct it by finding the rate first. Also do not write credits/notebook on one side and notebooks/credit on the other.","Equivalent ratios and constant rates define direct proportions; added fixed amounts do not.\n\nReview tomorrow without the table: explain why C = 16n is proportional and C = 20 + 16n is not. Rebuild the one-notebook rate from 80 credits for five notebooks, then check your explanation against this lesson.",B.aJv)
B.aA3=s(["Combine matching variable powers.","Distribute positive and negative factors.","Evaluate a simplified expression."],t.s)
B.b4u={"2(3x \u2212 4) + 5x":0,"\u2212(x \u2212 3)":1,"3x\xb2 + 2x\xb2":2}
B.aUh=new A.au(B.b4u,["6x \u2212 8 + 5x = 11x \u2212 8","\u2212x + 3","5x\xb2, not 5x\u2074"],t.w)
B.aFc=s(["3x\xb2 + 2x\xb2 simplifies to what?|5x\xb2|5x\u2074;6x\xb2;5x|Combine coefficients of matching squared terms.","2(3x \u2212 4) expands to what?|6x \u2212 8|6x \u2212 4;5x \u2212 8;6x + 8|Double both terms.","\u2212(x \u2212 3) equals what?|\u2212x + 3|\u2212x \u2212 3;x + 3;x \u2212 3|Multiply both terms by negative one.","Coefficient of x in \u22123x + 7?|\u22123|3;7;x|The coefficient includes its negative sign.","Constant term of 5x \u2212 9?|\u22129|5;9;x|The constant has no variable and retains its sign.","3x + 2x\xb2 can be combined to one like term?|No|Yes, 5x\xb3;Yes, 5x\xb2;Yes, 6x\xb3|The variable powers differ.","3(x + 2) \u2212 x simplifies to what?|2x + 6|2x + 2;4x + 6;3x + 5|Distribute then combine three x minus one x.","At x = 4, 2x + 6 equals what?|14|10;18;8|Two times four plus six gives fourteen.","2(3x \u2212 4) + 5x simplifies to what?|11x \u2212 8|11x \u2212 4;6x \u2212 3;10x \u2212 8|Distribute and join the six x and five x terms.","At x = 2, 11x \u2212 8 equals what?|14|6;30;22|Twenty-two minus eight equals fourteen.","4a \u2212 7a simplifies to what?|\u22123a|3a;11a;\u221228a|Subtract coefficients while preserving the variable."],t.s)
B.a8l=new A.cu("Algebraic Expressions",B.aA3,"An algebraic expression records operations involving variable quantities.","Like terms have the same variable part including exponents. Thus 3x\xb2 + 2x\xb2 = 5x\xb2, but 3x and 2x\xb2 stay distinct. A coefficient is the numerical multiplier; a constant has no variable. Distribution gives 2(3x \u2212 4) = 6x \u2212 8. A minus before parentheses multiplies every term by negative one, so \u2212(x \u2212 3) = \u2212x + 3. Equivalent expressions have the same value for all permitted inputs.",B.aUh,"Simplify 3(x + 2) \u2212 x. Step 1: distribute to get 3x + 6 \u2212 x. Step 2: combine three x minus one x. Step 3: write 2x + 6. Step 4: at x = 4, both forms give fourteen.","Simplify 2(3x \u2212 4) + 5x and evaluate at x = 2.","The result is 11x \u2212 8. At x = 2, it gives twenty-two minus eight, or fourteen.","Adding powers does not add their exponents; multiplication is different. Distribute a negative sign to every grouped term, not only the first.","Combine matching variable parts, distribute to all terms, and check equivalence with substitution.",B.aFc)
B.aJP=s(["Use inverse operations on both sides.","Represent an inequality solution set.","Reverse inequality direction when dividing by a negative."],t.s)
B.b4p={"2x + 5 = 17":0,"x + 3 < 7":1,"\u22122x > 8":2}
B.aUl=new A.au(B.b4p,["2x = 12 \u2192 x = 6","x < 4; four itself is excluded.","x < \u22124 after division by negative two."],t.w)
B.atw=s(["2x + 5 = 17 gives x what?|6|11;12;5|Subtract five and divide twelve by two.","x + 3 < 7 simplifies to what?|x < 4|x > 4;x < 10;x = 4|Subtract three on both sides without reversing.","\u22122x > 8 simplifies to what?|x < \u22124|x > \u22124;x < 4;x = \u22124|Division by a negative reverses direction.","Which satisfies x < 4?|3|4;5;6|A strict bound excludes four and larger values.","3(x \u2212 2) = 12 gives x what?|6|2;4;14|Divide by three then add two.","Why use an operation on both equation sides?|To preserve equality|To remove all variables;To force zero;To change the solution|Equal quantities remain equal under the same valid operation.","Does x \u2264 5 include five?|Yes|No;Only with negative x;Only if x is integer|The equality part includes the boundary.","When is an inequality direction reversed here?|Multiplying or dividing by a negative|Adding any positive;Subtracting any positive;Dividing by a positive|A negative factor reverses numerical order.","\u22123x \u2264 12 simplifies to what?|x \u2265 \u22124|x \u2264 \u22124;x \u2265 4;x = 4|Divide by negative three and reverse the sign.","5x \u2212 4 = 21 gives x what?|5|17;25;4|Add four then divide twenty-five by five.","Which checks x = 6 in 2x + 5 = 17?|2 \xd7 6 + 5 = 17|2 + 6 + 5 = 17;2 \xd7 5 + 6 = 17;6 + 5 = 17|Substitute into the original equation."],t.s)
B.a7V=new A.cu("Equations & Inequalities",B.aJP,"Equations identify equal values; inequalities describe ordered values.","To solve 2x + 5 = 17, subtract five on both sides and divide both by two, giving x = 6. Doing the same valid operation on both sides preserves equality. For x + 3 < 7, subtract three to get x < 4, a set of values rather than one number. Multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative reverses its direction: \u22122x > 8 becomes x < \u22124. Check a value in the original statement.",B.aUl,"Solve 3(x \u2212 2) = 12. Step 1: divide both sides by three. Step 2: obtain x \u2212 2 = 4. Step 3: add two, giving x = 6. Step 4: check 3(6 \u2212 2) = 12.","Solve \u22123x \u2264 12 and name a value satisfying the original inequality.","Divide by negative three and reverse direction: x \u2265 \u22124. For x = 0, zero is at most twelve, so it satisfies.","Do not operate on only one side. Dividing by a negative reverses inequality direction. A strict inequality excludes its boundary value.","Balance both sides, track inequality signs, and verify solutions in the original relation.",B.atw)
B.aNe=s(["Find triangle area from perpendicular height.","Use angle sums.","Calculate probabilities from equally likely outcomes."],t.s)
B.b4w={"Triangle: base 8, height 5":0,"Angles 50\xb0, 60\xb0, ?":1,"Fair die: even faces 2, 4, 6":2}
B.aTr=new A.au(B.b4w,["Area = 8 \xd7 5 \xf7 2 = 20","Missing angle = 70\xb0","Probability 3/6 = 1/2"],t.w)
B.aQJ=s(["Triangle base eight, height five. Area?|20 square units|40 square units;13 square units;26 square units|Take half of the base-height product.","Triangle angles fifty and sixty degrees. Third angle?|70\xb0|110\xb0;130\xb0;90\xb0|Subtract their sum from one hundred eighty.","Fair six-sided die gives probability of an even number what?|1/2|1/6;1/3;2/3|Three of six equally likely faces are even.","Probability of rolling a seven on a standard six-sided die?|0|1;1/6;7/6|Seven is not a possible face.","What height is used in triangle area?|Perpendicular distance to the base|Any slanted side;Perimeter;Angle measure|The area formula uses perpendicular height.","Triangle base ten, height six. Area?|30 square units|60 square units;16 square units;32 square units|Half of sixty equals thirty.","Triangle angles forty and sixty-five. Third angle?|75\xb0|105\xb0;85\xb0;65\xb0|The known sum 105 leaves seventy-five.","When does favorable divided by total apply directly?|Equally likely outcomes|All outcomes have different probabilities;No outcomes exist;Only the largest outcome counts|Equal likelihood makes outcome counts proportional to chances.","Fair die probability of a number greater than four?|1/3|1/2;1/6;2/3|Two of six faces qualify.","Fair coin probability of heads?|1/2|0;1;2|One of two equally likely outcomes is heads.","Triangle base twelve, perpendicular height three. Area?|18 square units|36 square units;15 square units;30 square units|Half of twelve times three is eighteen."],t.s)
B.a8e=new A.cu("Geometry & Probability",B.aNe,"Geometry measures shapes; probability compares favorable and possible outcomes.","A triangle area is one half of base times perpendicular height. A base of eight and height five gives twenty square units. Triangle interior angles total one hundred eighty degrees. For a fair six-sided die, each face has probability 1/6; three even faces give 3/6 = 1/2. Counting favorable outcomes over total works when outcomes are equally likely. Do not use slanted side length as height without checking perpendicularity.",B.aTr,"Find a triangle third angle when the others are forty and sixty-five degrees. Step 1: use total one hundred eighty. Step 2: add known angles to 105. Step 3: subtract to obtain seventy-five. Step 4: check 40 + 65 + 75 = 180.","Find triangle area with base ten and height six. For a fair die, find probability of a number greater than four.","Area is thirty square units. Two faces, five and six, exceed four, so probability is 2/6 = 1/3.","Height must be perpendicular to the chosen base. Probability counts need a justified equal-likelihood assumption; a biased die may not match simple counts.","Use geometric definitions and explicit probability assumptions rather than visual guesses.",B.aQJ)
