B.aHI=s(["Match m, s and t to familiar word beginnings.","Find the first and last letters of a word.","Blend the letters in map."],t.s)
B.anX=s(["m \u2192 map","s \u2192 sun","t \u2192 top"],t.s)
B.apJ=s(["Map starts with m.","Sun starts with s.","Top starts with t."],t.s)
B.atq=s(["Which letter starts map?|m|s|t|p|Map begins with the letter m, before a and p.","Which word starts with s?|sun|map|top|mat|Sun begins with s; the other words do not.","Which letter ends mat?|t|m|a|s|In m-a-t, the last letter is t.","Which small letter matches M?|m|n|s|t|Capital M and small m are the same letter.","Put m, a, p in order. Which word is made?|map|pam|amp|mat|Reading m-a-p in order makes the word map.","Which two words start with t?|top and tap|sun and sit|map and mat|sun and map|Top and tap both begin with the letter t.","Which letter is in the middle of map?|a|m|p|t|The order is m-a-p, so a is in the middle.","Which letter ends sun?|n|s|u|m|Sun is spelled s-u-n and ends with n.","Which word starts like mat?|map|sun|top|sit|Mat and map both begin with the m sound.","Which capital letter matches t?|T|S|M|P|Capital T and small t represent the same letter.","Read all the letters in s-i-t. Which word is made?|sit|sun|sat|top|The letters s, i and t in this order spell sit."],t.s)
B.a6Q=new A.ce("g1","Letters & Sounds","Letters help us write sounds. Say map slowly: m, the short a sound in map, then p. Put the sounds together: map. The letter m starts map and mat. The letter s starts sun and sit. The letter t starts top and tap. A capital M and a small m are two ways to write the same letter. A letter name is not always the sound it makes inside a word. Ask a helper to say these example words with you.","Read mat. 1. Point to m: it starts the word. 2. Point to a: hear the middle sound in mat. 3. Point to t: it ends the word. 4. Join the sounds to say mat.","Look at sun. Which letter starts it? Which letter ends it? Say sun with a helper.","Sun starts with s and ends with n. The letters stay in the order s-u-n when we blend the word.","Do not guess from only one letter. Read from the first letter to the last. Capital letters and small letters can represent the same letter.","Look at each letter. Say its sound in the example word. Blend the sounds in order.",1,B.aHI,B.anX,B.apJ,B.atq)
B.apF=s(["Recognize I, the and my in short sentences.","Read a repeated word in a new sentence.","Choose a word that fits a simple sentence."],t.s)
B.aDu=s(["I see my cat.","The cat is small.","We see the cat."],t.s)
B.aOb=s(["I names the speaker; my shows belonging.","The points to cat; is links cat with small.","We includes the speaker and others."],t.s)
B.aCT=s(["Which word means the speaker in I see a cat?|I|the|my|is|I names the person speaking in this sentence.","Which word is repeated in my bag and my hat?|my|bag|hat|the|My appears before both bag and hat.","Read The dog is big. Which word comes before dog?|The|big|is|my|The is the written word immediately before dog.","Which sentence uses my correctly?|I see my cat.|My see I cat.|Cat my see I.|See cat my I.|My belongs before the thing owned: my cat.","Which word includes the speaker and a friend?|we|I|the|is|We refers to the speaker together with another person.","Which word completes The cat __ small?|is|my|we|the|Is links the cat with the description small.","Which word is in both the sun and the moon?|the|sun|moon|my|The is the common word before both nouns.","How is the pronoun I for the speaker written?|capital I|small i|capital T|small w|The English pronoun I is written as a capital.","Which word shows belonging in my red bag?|my|red|bag|is|My tells that the bag belongs to the speaker.","Which word completes __ see a dog, for two friends speaking?|We|My|Is|The|We can name two friends who are speaking together.","Find is in The sun is hot. What comes after it?|hot|The|sun|my|Reading in order shows that hot follows is."],t.s)
B.a7C=new A.ce("g1","Sight Words","Some little words appear often when we read. Practise I, the, my, we and is in whole sentences. Read: I see my cat. The cat is on my mat. I tells who is speaking. My tells that something belongs to the speaker. The points to a thing we are talking about. We means the speaker and someone else. Look closely at every letter, even in a word you know. Sight reading grows from connecting the written word with its sounds and meaning; it is not guessing from a picture.","Read I see my dog. 1. Find I: the speaker. 2. Find my before dog: the dog belongs to the speaker. 3. Reread the whole sentence smoothly.","Read We see the sun. Find we and the. Which word tells that more than one person is looking?","We tells that the speaker and another person are looking. The is before sun and points to the sun in the sentence.","Do not replace my with we just because both are short. I is written as a capital when it means the speaker.","Recognize each little word, check its letters, then read it in the whole sentence.",2,B.apF,B.aDu,B.aOb,B.aCT)
B.auI=s(["Name a person, place, animal or thing.","Find a naming word in a short sentence.","Sort familiar nouns by what they name."],t.s)
B.axd=s(["Person: teacher","Place: park","Animal: cat","Thing: bag"],t.s)
B.aqT=s(["A teacher is someone.","A park is somewhere.","A cat is an animal.","A bag is something."],t.s)
B.aBr=s(["Which word names an animal?|cat|runs|small|quickly|Cat names an animal; the others tell action or description.","Which word names a person?|teacher|park|bag|red|Teacher names a person who teaches.","Which noun names a place?|park|cup|dog|girl|Park names a place people can visit.","Which word names an object you can hold?|bag|jump|happy|slowly|Bag names an object, so it is a noun.","Find the noun in The bird sings.|bird|sings|The|loudly|Bird is the naming word for the animal.","In The girl holds a cup, which two words are nouns?|girl and cup|holds and a|The and holds|a and The|Girl names a person and cup names a thing.","Which names more than one cup?|cups|cup|runs|small|The ending s makes cups name more than one.","Which special name needs a capital first letter?|Mina|chair|park|cat|Mina is a particular person name and begins with a capital.","In The small dog runs, what is the noun?|dog|small|runs|The|Dog names the animal in the sentence.","Which pair contains two place nouns?|school and park|cat and dog|bag and cup|boy and girl|School and park both name places.","In The boy reads a book, what names a thing?|book|reads|boy|The|Book names the thing; boy names the person."],t.s)
B.a74=new A.ce("g1","Nouns","A noun is a naming word. Teacher names a person. Park names a place. Cat names an animal. Bag names a thing. Read: The girl holds a cup. Girl and cup are nouns. We can name more than one thing: one cup, two cups. A special name, such as Mina, begins with a capital letter. Other words can tell what a noun does or what it is like: in The small cat runs, cat is the naming word, small describes it, and runs tells its action.","Read The boy sees a dog. 1. Ask who: boy names a person. 2. Ask what animal: dog. 3. These naming words are nouns; sees tells an action.","In The cat sits on a chair, find the animal noun and the thing noun.","Cat names the animal. Chair names a thing. Sits is the action, so it is not the naming word here.","An action such as jump is not a noun when it tells what someone does. A noun can name a place even when we cannot hold it.","Nouns name people, places, animals and things. Use the sentence to find what each word names.",3,B.auI,B.axd,B.aqT,B.aBr)
B.aP9=s(["Recognize words that tell an action.","Match an action to a familiar sentence.","Find who does the action."],t.s)
B.arw=s(["The dog runs.","The bird sings.","The girl claps."],t.s)
B.aLm=s(["Runs is the dog action.","Sings is the bird action.","Claps is the girl action."],t.s)
B.aFA=s(["What is the action word in The dog runs?|runs|dog|The|small|Runs tells what the dog does.","Which word tells the action in We clap?|clap|chair|red|park|Clap tells something a person can do.","In The bird sings, who does the action?|the bird|the song|the chair|the park|The bird is the one that sings.","Which word completes The fish __ in water?|swims|bag|blue|park|Swims tells an action a fish can do.","Find the action in The girl kicks a ball.|kicks|girl|ball|a|Kicks tells what the girl does to the ball.","Which pair names two actions you do with your legs?|run and jump|cup and bag|red and big|cat and bird|Run and jump both name actions.","In I read a book, which word tells the action?|read|I|book|a|Read tells what the speaker does.","Which action is in The cat drinks water?|drinks|cat|water|The|Drinks tells what the cat does with water.","Which sentence tells a waving action?|Mina waves.|Mina has a bag.|The bag is red.|The cup is small.|Waves tells the action in Mina waves.","What does the boy do in The boy sits?|sits|boy|The|chair|Sits is the action stated in the sentence.","Which word is an action in We wash our hands?|wash|hands|our|We|Wash tells what we do to our hands."],t.s)
B.a7x=new A.ce("g1","Action Words","An action word tells what someone or something does. Read: The dog runs. Runs tells what the dog does. The bird sings. Sings tells what the bird does. The girl kicks a ball. Kicks tells her action. We can act out safe actions such as clap, wave and sit. The word for the person or animal is a noun; the action word is a verb. Not every verb names a movement, but these examples use easy actions we can notice.","Read The boy jumps. 1. Ask who: the boy. 2. Ask what he does: jumps. 3. Jumps is the action word. Say and safely act out jump.","In The cat drinks water, which word tells the action? Who does it?","Drinks tells the action. The cat does the drinking. Water names what the cat drinks.","Do not choose dog just because a dog can move. Choose the word that tells what it does in this sentence.","Ask what the person or animal does. That word tells the action in these sentences.",4,B.aP9,B.arw,B.aLm,B.aFA)
B.aJX=s(["Recognize a sentence that tells a whole idea.","Begin a sentence with a capital letter.","End a telling sentence with a full stop."],t.s)
B.aFY=s(["The cat","The cat sleeps.","Capital \u2192 words \u2192 full stop"],t.s)
B.asP=s(["This names the cat but leaves the idea unfinished.","This tells who and what happens.","These written clues help us read a whole sentence."],t.s)
B.aQN=s(["Which tells a whole idea?|The dog runs.|The dog|A red bag|My little cat|The dog runs tells who and what the dog does.","Which sentence begins with a capital letter?|The cat sleeps.|a dog runs.|my bird sings.|we sit.|The first letter of a sentence is a capital; only The starts with one here.","What ends a telling sentence?|a full stop|a space|a capital M|a number|A full stop marks the end of a telling sentence.","Which sentence has spaces between words?|The bird sings.|Thebirdsings.|Thebird sings.|The birdsings.|Spaces separate The, bird and sings.","Finish The fish __ to tell an action.|swims.|blue|small|and|Swims gives a predicate and completes the idea.","Which telling sentence is written correctly?|My dog sits.|my cat sleeps.|My dog sits|The small dog|My dog sits begins with a capital, tells a whole idea and ends with a full stop.","Who sleeps in The cat sleeps?|the cat|the dog|the bird|the cup|The words the cat name who is sleeping.","What is missing from The bird sings, a telling sentence?|a full stop|a noun|an action|all spaces|The sentence has a subject and action but needs its end mark.","Which correctly writes the telling sentence we clap?|We clap.|we run|Weclap.|The happy children|We clap has a capital, spaces, a complete idea and a full stop.","Which is unfinished?|The small dog|The dog sits.|I wave.|We run.|The small dog does not tell what happens or what the dog is.","Which word completes Mina __ a cup.|holds|red|small|park|Holds provides the action and completes the sentence."],t.s)
B.a7s=new A.ce("g1","Simple Sentences","A sentence tells a whole idea. The cat sleeps. tells who and what happens. The cat by itself does not tell what happens. Start a sentence with a capital letter. Put spaces between words. End a telling sentence with a full stop: The dog runs. Read the sentence aloud to check that it makes sense. A short sentence can tell a complete idea. Adding many words does not help if the idea is still unfinished.","Fix the bird sings. 1. The words tell who and what happens. 2. Change the first t to capital T. 3. Add a full stop: The bird sings.","Write this telling sentence correctly: my cat sleeps","My cat sleeps. begins with capital M, has spaces between words, and ends with a full stop. It tells a whole idea.","A capital letter alone does not make a whole sentence. The big dog still needs an action or another complete predicate.","A telling sentence gives a whole idea, starts with a capital, and ends with a full stop.",5,B.aJX,B.aFY,B.asP,B.aQN)
B.aHc=s(["Blend sh and ch as consonant teams.","Compare cap with cape.","Recognize the long a pattern in rain."],t.s)
B.azh=s(["sh \u2192 ship","ch \u2192 chip","cap \u2192 cape","ai \u2192 rain"],t.s)
B.aLn=s(["Sh works as a consonant team.","Ch is a different consonant team.","Final e marks long a in cape.","Ai represents long a in this word."],t.s)
B.aJC=s(["Which letters make a team at the start of ship?|sh|hi|ip|sp|Sh represents the first consonant sound in ship.","Which word begins with ch?|chip|ship|cap|rain|Chip begins with the consonant team ch.","Which word uses the long a pattern taught here?|cape|cap|cat|map|Cape has long a and a final e that is not separately sounded.","Which vowel team is in rain?|ai|ra|in|an|The adjacent letters a and i form the vowel team ai.","What changes when cap becomes cape?|a final e is added|c is removed|p becomes t|a becomes i|Adding final e changes this word to cape.","Which word shares the vowel team in rain?|paid|ship|cap|chip|Paid and rain both contain the vowel team ai.","Which first team changes ship to chip?|sh changes to ch|ip changes to ai|h changes to p|s changes to r|The first team changes from sh to ch while ip stays.","Is the final e in cape a separate spoken syllable?|No|Yes, always|Only after p|Only at night|Cape is one syllable in its common reading.","Which word is an exception to the long-vowel final-e pattern?|have|cape|tape|made|Have does not use the long a heard in cape.","Which pair shares the sh team?|ship and shop|chip and cap|rain and paid|cape and cap|Ship and shop both begin with sh.","Which spelling makes the word for water falling from clouds?|rain|ran|ring|rang|Rain is spelled r-ai-n in this familiar example."],t.s)
B.a7h=new A.ce("g2","Phonics Patterns","Letters can work in teams. In ship, sh represents one consonant sound; in chip, ch represents another. Compare cap and cape: cap has the short a heard in cap, while cape has the long a heard in the letter name. The final e is not pronounced separately in cape. The vowel team ai also represents long a in rain and paid. These are word examples, not a rule for every English word: have does not follow the long-a cape pattern. Read the whole word and check its meaning.","Read rain. 1. Find the team ai. 2. Say the long a heard in rain. 3. Blend r, ai and n. 4. Compare paid: the same vowel team appears.","Compare ship and chip. Which letters change? Read cap and cape with a helper.","Ship begins with sh; chip begins with ch. Cap and cape have different a sounds in these common readings; the final e helps mark the long-a pattern in cape.","Two letters can represent one sound. Do not pronounce the final e in cape as a separate syllable. Patterns have exceptions.","Notice consonant and vowel teams, blend the word, and check the particular example.",1,B.aHc,B.azh,B.aLn,B.aJC)
B.asg=s(["Replace a repeated name with a clear pronoun.","Use I and we for speakers.","Use it and they for things or groups."],t.s)
B.aLb=s(["Mina \u2192 she in this example","a ball \u2192 it","the children \u2192 they"],t.s)
B.aBa=s(["The passage tells us Mina uses she.","It refers back to the thing named.","They refers back to the group."],t.s)
B.aGm=s(["Mina uses she. Replace Mina in Mina waves.|She|It|We|I|She is the pronoun specified for Mina in this example.","The bag is red. __ is small.|It|We|I|He|It stands for the thing named the bag.","The children laugh. __ are happy.|They|It|I|She|They replaces the group the children.","Which pronoun names the speaker?|I|it|they|she|I is used by a person referring to themself.","Which pronoun includes the speaker and a friend?|we|it|he|she|We includes the speaker as part of a group.","In I see a dog. It runs, what is it?|the dog|the speaker|the sun|a bag|It refers back to the dog just mentioned.","The two cups are clean. Replace the two cups.|They|It|She|I|They can replace the plural noun phrase the two cups.","Leo uses he. Choose the matching pronoun.|he|she|it|we|The example explicitly identifies he as Leo pronoun.","Mina puts the book in the bag. It is wet. What is unclear?|Whether it means book or bag|Who Mina is speaking to|Whether puts is an action|How book is spelled|Two nearby things could be the meaning of it.","Which revision makes a wet bag clear?|The bag is wet.|It is wet.|They wet.|She is bag.|Repeating the bag removes the uncertain pronoun reference.","In We sing together, is the speaker included?|Yes|No|Only a bag is included|Only one bird is included|We includes the speaker with at least one other person."],t.s)
B.a7b=new A.ce("g2","Pronouns","A pronoun can stand in for a noun. Mina has a ball. She kicks it. She stands for Mina; it stands for the ball. I means the speaker. We means the speaker and others. They can refer to a group: The children wave. They smile. Make it clear what each pronoun means. A person may use he, she or they; follow the person wording given in the example rather than guessing from a name. In our sentence, Mina uses she. With two possible nouns, repeat the noun if it is needed for clarity.","Read The dogs play. They run. 1. Find the group: dogs. 2. Match they to that group. 3. Replace they with the dogs to check the meaning.","Read I carry a bag. It is blue. What does it stand for? What pronoun includes you and a friend?","It stands for the bag. We includes the speaker and a friend. Both pronouns need a clear meaning in their sentence.","Do not use it for every person. Do not leave a reader guessing between two possible nouns.","Pronouns replace nouns. Use the surrounding sentence to make their meaning clear.",2,B.asg,B.aLb,B.aBa,B.aGm)
B.aMo=s(["Use present verbs for usual or current actions.","Use regular past verbs ending in ed.","Recognize went and saw as past forms."],t.s)
B.aJl=s(["Today: play \u2192 Yesterday: played","Today: go \u2192 Yesterday: went","Today: see \u2192 Yesterday: saw"],t.s)
B.aHJ=s(["Play adds ed in the past.","Go has an irregular past form.","See also has an irregular past form."],t.s)
B.ayh=s(["Yesterday I __ with my friend.|played|play|plays|playing|Played is the simple past form that fits yesterday.","Which is the past form of go?|went|goed|goes|going|Go has the irregular simple past form went.","Which is the past form of see?|saw|seed|sees|seeing|Saw is the irregular simple past form of see.","Today she __ after school, as a habit.|plays|played|playing|playeds|Plays is simple present with the singular subject she.","Change I jump to simple past.|I jumped.|I jumps.|I jumping.|I jumpeds.|Jump is regular and adds ed to become jumped.","Which sentence tells a finished past action?|We walked yesterday.|We walk each day.|We will walk tomorrow.|We are walking now.|Walked and yesterday identify a finished past action.","Which time word best fits I visited the park?|yesterday|tomorrow|next week|soon in the future|Visited is past, so yesterday matches its time.","Which verb has the regular ed past?|walk \u2192 walked|go \u2192 went|see \u2192 saw|eat \u2192 ate|Walk follows the regular ed past pattern.","Fix Yesterday we go home.|Yesterday we went home.|Yesterday we goes home.|Yesterday we going home.|Yesterday we goed home.|Went gives the correct past form of go.","Choose the present habitual sentence.|I play on Fridays.|I played last Friday.|I went yesterday.|I saw it last week.|Play with on Fridays tells a repeated habit.","Which verb completes Last night I __ a bird?|saw|see|sees|seeing|Saw is the past form matching last night."],t.s)
B.a7H=new A.ce("g2","Present & Past Verbs","Verb forms help tell when an action happens. Today I play. Yesterday I played. Played is the past form of play; it ends in ed. Some past forms change in a different way: I go becomes I went; I see becomes I saw. A time word can help choose a tense, but the verb must also fit. Today she plays has s for one person; yesterday she played does not add another s. These examples use the simple present for habits and the simple past for finished actions.","Change Yesterday I walk to school. 1. Yesterday tells us past time. 2. Walk is regular: add ed. 3. Write Yesterday I walked to school.","Choose the past form for Yesterday we go to the park. Then change I jump to past time.","Yesterday we went to the park uses the irregular past went. I jumped uses the regular ed ending.","Went is not goed. Do not combine yesterday with a present verb when you mean a finished past action.","Choose the verb form for the time: play/played, go/went, see/saw.",3,B.aMo,B.aJl,B.aHJ,B.ayh)
B.aF6=s(["Find words that describe nouns.","Use size and color words to add detail.","Distinguish a description from an action."],t.s)
B.as4=s(["red bag","small dog","The tree is tall."],t.s)
B.aqD=s(["Red describes the bag color.","Small describes the dog size.","Tall describes the tree after is."],t.s)
B.aNs=s(["Which word describes the bag in a red bag?|red|bag|a|runs|Red tells the color of the noun bag.","Which adjective tells size?|small|dog|jump|park|Small describes how large a noun is.","Find the adjective in The tall tree sways.|tall|tree|sways|The|Tall describes the tree rather than its action.","What does blue describe in a blue cup?|cup|a|water|runs|Blue is placed before cup and describes its color.","Which adds a color description to ball?|green ball|ball rolls|a ball|ball and bat|Green is an adjective naming the ball color.","Which word is an action in The small dog runs?|runs|small|dog|The|Runs tells what the dog does, not what it is like.","Which sentence uses an adjective after is?|The bag is red.|The bag runs.|The bag holds a book.|I carry the bag.|Red describes the bag after the linking verb is.","Choose a description for a soft toy.|soft|sings|table|read|Soft is a quality an adjective can describe.","In a small red bag, how many adjectives describe bag?|two|one|three|none|Small and red both describe the noun bag.","Which word describes the box in The box is heavy?|heavy|box|The|is|Heavy describes the box weight.","Which pair contains two adjectives?|bright and quiet|cat and dog|run and jump|park and school|Bright and quiet can describe qualities of nouns."],t.s)
B.a7w=new A.ce("g2","Adjectives","An adjective adds information about a noun. In a red bag, red tells the color of the bag. In a small dog, small tells its size. Adjectives can appear before a noun or after is: The bag is red. In The small dog runs, small describes the dog and runs tells the action. Choose a description that fits what you mean. The same noun can have more than one detail: a small red bag. A describing word does not replace the noun it describes.","Read The blue cup is small. 1. Find the noun cup. 2. Blue tells its color. 3. Small tells its size after is. Both describe the cup.","Find the adjective in The tall tree sways. What noun does it describe?","Tall describes the tree size. Tree is the noun; sways is its action. The adjective does not tell the action.","Runs tells an action here; it is not an adjective. Ask which noun a describing word belongs to.","Adjectives tell what a noun is like, including its color, size or other quality.",4,B.aF6,B.as4,B.aqD,B.aNs)
B.any=s(["Identify what happens first, next and last.","Use before and after to connect events.","Choose a retelling that keeps story order."],t.s)
B.auM=s(["First: add soil","Next: plant seed","Then: water","Later: shoot appears"],t.s)
B.aL9=s(["Mina prepares the pot.","She puts the seed in the soil.","She adds water after planting.","The story moves forward by days."],t.s)
B.aES=s(["What happens first in the planting story?|Mina adds soil.|A shoot appears.|Mina waters.|Mina plants a seed.|The first stated event is putting soil in a pot.","What happens after planting and before the shoot?|watering|adding soil|buying a book|packing a bag|Mina waters the planted seed before the shoot appears.","Which event is last in the story?|A shoot appears.|Soil is added.|The seed is planted.|Water is added.|Days later places the shoot after the other events.","Which word shows an event that follows another?|next|because|red|quiet|Next is a time clue for the following event.","What comes before watering in this story?|planting the seed|the shoot appearing|a storm|opening a book|The text says planting comes before watering.","Arun packs, walks, opens. What is the middle event?|walking|packing|opening|watering|Walking occurs between packing and opening.","Which retelling keeps the planting order?|soil, seed, water, shoot|shoot, soil, water, seed|water, shoot, seed, soil|seed, shoot, soil, water|That sequence matches all four stated story events.","What does Days later tell the reader?|Time has passed.|The story starts again.|Nothing grew.|Mina lost the pot.|The time phrase moves the story forward by several days.","Which event is not stated in the planting story?|A storm breaks the pot.|Mina adds soil.|Mina plants a seed.|A shoot appears.|The passage never mentions a storm or a broken pot.","Which word could introduce the final retelling event?|finally|first|before everything|at the beginning|Finally commonly signals the last event in a sequence.","If Arun opens his book after walking, which comes earlier?|walking|opening|both at once|neither event|After means that opening follows walking."],t.s)
B.a71=new A.ce("g2","Story Sequence","Events happen in an order. Read this original story: Mina put soil in a pot. Next she planted a seed. Then she watered it. Days later a shoot appeared. First, next, then and later help show the sequence. Planting comes before watering in this story; the shoot appears after both. A retelling keeps the important events in order. It does not add events the story never tells. Sometimes a story changes time, so use the actual time clues instead of guessing from the order of sentences.","Retell the planting story. 1. Find the first event: soil in a pot. 2. Put planting before watering. 3. End with the later shoot. 4. Keep only events the text states.","An original story says: Arun packed his bag, walked to school, and opened his book. What happened between packing and opening?","Arun walked to school between packing his bag and opening his book. The sentence gives the events in that order.","Do not say a shoot appeared before the seed was planted in this story. A retelling should not invent a lost seed or a storm.","Read time clues and keep the important events in their stated order.",5,B.any,B.auM,B.aL9,B.aES)
B.aEt=s(["Classify nouns, verbs and adjectives in sentences.","Explain how a word functions in its sentence.","Recognize a pronoun replacing a noun."],t.s)
B.aQH=s(["bird: noun","sings: verb","small: adjective","softly: adverb"],t.s)
B.aK4=s(["Names an animal.","States its action.","Describes the bird.","Describes how it sings."],t.s)
B.aBe=s(["What is bird in The bird sings?|noun|verb|adverb|pronoun|Bird names an animal, which is a noun job.","What is sings in The bird sings?|verb|noun|adjective|pronoun|Sings tells the action performed by the bird.","What is small in a small bird?|adjective|verb|pronoun|noun|Small describes the noun bird.","What does softly describe in It sings softly?|how it sings|the bird color|the word it spelling|a place|Softly is an adverb describing the manner of singing.","What is it in It sings?|pronoun|adjective|noun naming a place|preposition|It stands in for a previously named thing or animal.","What is paint in I paint the fence?|verb|noun|adjective|pronoun|Paint tells an action in this sentence.","What is paint in The paint is blue?|noun|verb|adverb|pronoun|Paint names a material here, rather than an action.","Which word is an adjective in The quiet child reads?|quiet|child|reads|The|Quiet describes the noun child.","Which word is an adverb in Mina walks slowly?|slowly|Mina|walks|none|Slowly describes how Mina walks.","Which pronoun can replace the kite in The kite flies?|it|red|flies|quick|It can stand in for the singular thing the kite.","Why is friendly an adjective in a friendly teacher?|It describes teacher.|It always means an action.|All ly words are adverbs.|It names a place.|Friendly describes a noun despite ending in ly."],t.s)
B.a7d=new A.ce("g3","Parts of Speech","Words do different jobs. In The small bird sings, bird is a noun naming an animal, sings is a verb telling an action, and small is an adjective describing bird. In It sings softly, it replaces bird as a pronoun and softly describes how it sings as an adverb. Classify a word from its job, not only its spelling. In I paint, paint is a verb. In The paint is blue, paint is a noun naming a material. A word can have different jobs in different sentences.","Analyze The quiet child reads slowly. 1. Child names a person: noun. 2. Reads states an action: verb. 3. Quiet describes child: adjective. 4. Slowly describes reads: adverb.","In The red kite flies, identify the noun, verb and adjective. Replace the kite with a pronoun.","Kite is a noun; flies is a verb; red is an adjective. It flies uses it in place of the kite.","Not every word ending in ly is an adverb: friendly can describe a person. Check the sentence job.","Identify what a word names, does, replaces or describes in its sentence.",1,B.aEt,B.aQH,B.aK4,B.aBe)
B.aPc=s(["Find who or what a sentence is about.","Find what the sentence says about its subject.","Separate complete subjects from complete predicates."],t.s)
B.asx=s(["The young birds","sing loudly","birds / sing"],t.s)
B.aPY=s(["Complete subject: who the sentence is about.","Complete predicate: what is said about them.","Simple subject and simple predicate."],t.s)
B.aRf=s(["What is the subject in Mina reads?|Mina|reads|the book|reading|Mina names who performs the action reads.","What is the predicate in Mina reads?|reads|Mina|the girl|a noun|Reads tells what Mina does.","Find the complete subject: The young birds sing loudly.|The young birds|young birds sing|sing loudly|The|The whole noun phrase before sing names the subject.","Find the complete predicate: The young birds sing loudly.|sing loudly|The young birds|birds|The young|Sing loudly tells what the subject does and how.","What is the simple subject in The red bus stops?|bus|red|The|stops|Bus is the main noun in the complete subject.","What is the simple predicate in The red bus stops near the gate?|stops|bus|gate|near|Stops is the verb at the center of the predicate.","Which split is correct: The dog sleeps under the table?|The dog / sleeps under the table|The / dog sleeps under the table|The dog sleeps / under the table|The dog sleeps under / the table|The dog is the subject; the rest is its predicate.","In Mina is tired, what is the predicate?|is tired|Mina|tired Mina|is Mina|Is tired says something about the subject Mina.","In Arun carries a bag, is bag the subject?|No, Arun is the subject.|Yes, bag performs carries.|Both words are verbs.|There is no subject.|Arun performs carries; bag is what he carries.","Choose a predicate for The children.|play in the park|the happy children|a red bag|three children|Play in the park says what the children do.","Which complete subject belongs with rings loudly?|The school bell|rings|loudly|near the gate|The school bell names what rings loudly."],t.s)
B.a7y=new A.ce("g3","Subject & Predicate","The subject tells who or what a sentence is about. The predicate says something about that subject and contains the verb. In The young birds sing loudly, the complete subject is The young birds and the complete predicate is sing loudly. The simple subject is birds; the simple predicate is sing. A complete subject can include describing words. A predicate can include the action and other information. In Mina is tired, is tired is the predicate even though it does not describe a moving action.","Split The red bus stops near the gate. 1. Find the verb stops. 2. Ask what stops: The red bus. 3. The remaining stops near the gate is the complete predicate.","Split The tired dog sleeps under the table into a complete subject and complete predicate.","The tired dog is the complete subject. Sleeps under the table is the complete predicate; it tells what the dog does and where.","Do not include the verb in the complete subject. A noun after the verb may be an object, not the subject.","Find the verb, ask who or what does or is that, and separate the two sentence parts.",2,B.aPc,B.asx,B.aPY,B.aRf)
B.anN=s(["Distinguish simple present, past and future.","Use irregular past forms accurately.","Keep time consistent within a short account."],t.s)
B.aKB=s(["Present: I walk each day.","Past: I walked yesterday.","Future: I will walk tomorrow."],t.s)
B.aL4=s(["The action is a habit.","The action is finished in the past.","Will plus the base verb expresses future time."],t.s)
B.apR=s(["Which sentence is simple past?|I walked yesterday.|I walk each day.|I will walk tomorrow.|I am walking now.|Walked is the simple past form of walk.","Which verb completes Tomorrow I __?|will eat|ate|eats|eaten|Will plus the base form eat expresses future time.","What is the past form of take?|took|taked|taken by will|takes|Take has the irregular simple past form took.","Which verb completes Yesterday I __ an apple?|ate|eat|eats|will eat|Ate matches the finished past time yesterday.","Which sentence describes a habit?|We walk every morning.|We walked last night.|We will walk next week.|We have just walked.|Simple present walk with every morning expresses a habit.","Fix Yesterday we visited and watch birds.|Yesterday we visited and watched birds.|Yesterday we visit and watched birds.|Yesterday we visited and will watch birds.|Yesterday we visiting and watch birds.|Both finished actions use simple past forms.","Which future form is correct?|will take|will took|will takes|will taken|Will is followed by the base form take.","What tense is Arun plays on Saturdays?|simple present|simple past|simple future|past perfect|Plays with on Saturdays states a regular habit.","Which change of tense has a clear time reason?|Yesterday we walked; tomorrow we will run.|Yesterday we walked and run.|Last night we eat and slept.|Tomorrow we went and sit.|Different time clues explain the past-to-future change.","Choose the past pair: We __ the bus and __ the zoo.|took; visited|take; visits|will took; visit|takes; visited|Took and visited both fit a finished past account.","Which word is the base verb in will watch?|watch|will|watched|watching|Watch is the unchanged base form following will."],t.s)
B.a7g=new A.ce("g3","Verb Tenses","Tense connects verbs with time. I walk each morning uses simple present for a habit. I walked yesterday uses simple past for a finished action. I will walk tomorrow uses will with the base verb to express future time. Some past forms are irregular: eat becomes ate and take becomes took. Do not change tense without a reason. Yesterday we visited the pond and watched birds keeps both actions in the past. A different time clue can justify a change: Yesterday we visited; tomorrow we will return.","Revise Yesterday we take the bus and visit the zoo. 1. Both actions happened yesterday. 2. Take has past took. 3. Visit becomes visited. 4. Write Yesterday we took the bus and visited the zoo.","Choose verbs for Tomorrow I __ a sandwich, and Yesterday I __ an apple, using eat.","Tomorrow I will eat a sandwich expresses future time. Yesterday I ate an apple uses the irregular past form ate.","Do not write will ate: will takes the base form eat. A past time word needs a compatible verb form.","Match each verb to its time, use correct forms, and explain any change of tense.",3,B.anN,B.aKB,B.aL4,B.apR)
B.awc=s(["Use nearby examples to infer a word meaning.","Check a guessed meaning by rereading.","Distinguish a clue from an unsupported guess."],t.s)
B.aHY=s(["fragile","carried gently","easily broken"],t.s)
B.ax8=s(["The unfamiliar word describes the cup.","Careful handling supplies a clue.","This meaning fits the whole sentence."],t.s)
B.aNy=s(["The fragile cup was carried gently. Fragile probably means what?|easily broken|very loud|full of food|always hot|Gentle handling suggests the cup can break easily.","A nocturnal animal is active at night. Nocturnal means what?|active at night|active only at noon|unable to move|without a home|The sentence directly defines nocturnal.","The room was dim, so we lit a lamp. Dim means what?|not bright|very noisy|very large|full of people|Adding a lamp suggests that the room needs more light.","Which clue supports exhausted meaning very tired?|needed to rest|a red shirt|a short name|the word Mina|Needing rest after a run supports the tired meaning.","Which sentence gives an example clue for orchard?|The orchard had fruit trees such as mango trees.|The orchard was quiet.|Arun saw an orchard.|The orchard was nearby.|Fruit-tree examples help establish what an orchard contains.","How can you check an inferred meaning?|Substitute it and reread.|Count the letters only.|Choose the longest option.|Ignore the sentence.|A fitting substitute should preserve the sentence meaning.","The path was narrow; two people could not walk side by side. Narrow means what?|not wide|very sunny|very muddy|very long|The limited side-by-side space suggests little width.","Mina was delighted and smiled broadly. Delighted means what?|very pleased|very sleepy|very frightened|very hungry|Smiling broadly supports a pleased feeling.","Which conclusion is supported by dim room and lamp?|The room needs more light.|The room is certainly tiny.|A storm destroyed the roof.|The people dislike reading.|The lamp addresses limited light; other details are not given.","What should you do if two meanings both fit?|Seek more context or a dictionary.|Declare either meaning certain.|Ignore every later sentence.|Guess from spelling alone.|More evidence can resolve an uncertain inference.","The timid puppy hid when strangers arrived. Timid likely means what?|shy or easily frightened|very noisy|fully grown|brightly colored|Hiding from strangers supports a shy or fearful meaning."],t.s)
B.a6Z=new A.ce("g3","Context Clues","Context is the language around a word. Read: The cup was fragile, so Arun carried it gently. Gently suggests that the cup could break easily. Fragile likely means easily broken. Another sentence might define a word directly: A nocturnal animal is active at night. Examples can help too: The orchard had fruit trees, such as mango and orange trees. Orchard refers to a place with fruit trees. Substitute your meaning and reread. If several meanings still fit, ask for more context or check a dictionary.","Read Mina was exhausted after the long run and needed to rest. 1. Notice the long run. 2. Notice needed to rest. 3. Infer very tired. 4. Substitute: Mina was very tired. The sentence still makes sense.","The room was dim, so we turned on a lamp. What does dim probably mean? Which action is the clue?","Dim probably means not bright. Turning on a lamp supplies more light, supporting that meaning. It does not mean noisy.","One familiar word is not enough if its meaning does not fit the sentence. A context clue suggests meaning but may not give an exact dictionary definition.","Notice nearby evidence, propose a meaning, and test it in the full sentence.",4,B.awc,B.aHY,B.ax8,B.aNy)
B.aHH=s(["Identify a paragraph main idea.","Select details that support a topic sentence.","Remove an unrelated sentence."],t.s)
B.aP7=s(["Main idea: garden care","Detail: water the beds","Detail: pull weeds","Detail: check plants"],t.s)
B.aIm=s(["Covers the whole paragraph.","Shows one care task.","Shows another care task.","Adds a third related example."],t.s)
B.aMk=s(["What is the garden paragraph main idea?|The class cares for a garden.|The class has blue shoes.|Friday is always sunny.|Only weeds grow.|Watering, weeding and checking all support garden care.","Which is a supporting garden detail?|We water the beds.|My shoes are blue.|I lost a toy.|The bus is loud.|Watering is an example of caring for the garden.","Which sentence does not fit a paragraph about garden care?|My shoes are blue.|We pull weeds.|We water plants.|We check leaves.|Shoe color does not support the garden-care idea.","What does a topic sentence usually do?|States the paragraph main idea.|Lists every letter.|Ends every story.|Names only punctuation.|A topic sentence introduces the idea developed by details.","Which ending fits the garden paragraph?|We all help the garden grow.|My bicycle is red.|The lake is deep.|I dislike shoes.|This ending connects all the care tasks to garden growth.","Which detail supports A pet needs daily care?|Give it clean water.|Count the roof tiles.|Paint the bus.|Choose a new school.|Clean water is a daily need of a pet.","Which pair fits a library-reading paragraph?|quiet voices and many books|loud engines and car wheels|waves and seashells|soil and weeds|Quiet voices and books both support library reading.","How many main ideas should this short paragraph develop?|one main idea|eleven unrelated ideas|only a title|no idea|The lesson paragraph keeps its sentences focused on one idea.","Which is broader than the watering detail?|The class cares for a garden.|We water at eight.|One can is blue.|A bed is two steps wide.|Garden care covers watering and other related tasks.","What should happen to an unrelated sentence?|Remove it or explain a relevant connection.|Keep it without a reason.|Move it to every paragraph.|Treat it as proof of the main idea.|A paragraph needs a clear relationship among its sentences.","Choose a topic for details about feeding and watering a pet.|Daily pet care|Buying bus tickets|Painting a fence|School uniforms|Feeding and watering are both forms of pet care."],t.s)
B.a7j=new A.ce("g3","Paragraph Basics","A paragraph groups sentences about one main idea. Read this original paragraph: Our class cares for a garden. We water the beds each morning. We pull weeds on Fridays. We check the young plants for damage. The first sentence states the main idea; the others give examples of garden care. A paragraph can end with a sentence that brings the idea together. We all help the garden grow would fit. My shoes are blue would not fit this main idea, even if it is true.","Build a paragraph about caring for a pet. 1. State the topic: A pet needs daily care. 2. Add details about food and clean water. 3. End by connecting those details to the pet health.","Choose two details for The library is a quiet place to read: soft voices, many books, and a broken bicycle.","Soft voices supports quiet; many books supports reading. A broken bicycle does not support this library main idea without another connection.","A topic sentence should cover the paragraph, not only one tiny detail. Do not add an unrelated sentence just because it is interesting.","Keep one main idea, support it with related details, and connect the ending to that idea.",5,B.aHH,B.aP7,B.aIm,B.aMk)
B.aE7=s(["Distinguish statements, questions, commands and exclamations.","Choose suitable end punctuation.","Recognize an implied subject in a command."],t.s)
B.aPU=s(["The gate is open.","Is the gate open?","Close the gate.","What a tall tree!"],t.s)
B.aOM=s(["Statement gives information.","Question asks for information.","Command directs an action.","Exclamation expresses strong feeling."],t.s)
B.atx=s(["What kind is The bus is late.?|statement|question|command|exclamation|It gives information about the bus.","What kind is Where is the bus?|question|statement|command|fragment|It asks for information about the bus location.","What kind is Please open the door.?|command|question|statement of past fact|noun phrase|It politely directs the listener to act.","Which end mark suits When will Mina arrive?|question mark|full stop only|comma|colon|The sentence asks a direct question.","What is the understood subject of Sit down.?|you|the chair|Mina and Arun|nobody|Commands commonly address an implied you.","Which expresses strong feeling?|What a wonderful view!|The view is visible.|Is there a view?|Look at the map.|The wording and exclamation mark express strong feeling.","Which is a statement rather than a command?|You closed the gate.|Close the gate.|Please close it.|Close it now!|You closed the gate reports a past event.","Which command has ordinary end punctuation?|Please wait here.|Please wait here?|Please wait here,|Please wait here:|A non-urgent command normally ends with a full stop.","Which revision makes a direct question?|Did you bring the book?|You brought the book.|Bring the book.|What a book!|Did plus the subject forms a direct question.","Can an urgent command use an exclamation mark?|Yes: Stop!|No command can do so.|Only nouns can do so.|Only a past tense can do so.|An exclamation mark can mark urgency in a command.","Which sentence requests an action?|Please check the window.|The window is open.|Is the window open?|What a wide window!|Please check directs the listener to perform an action."],t.s)
B.a7p=new A.ce("g4","Sentence Types","A statement gives information: The gate is open. A question asks for information: Is the gate open? A command requests or directs an action: Close the gate. An exclamation expresses strong feeling: What a beautiful garden! End marks help show the purpose. Commands usually end with a full stop; an urgent command can use an exclamation mark. The subject you is often understood in a command. Do not classify a sentence by only its first word: You closed the gate. is a statement, not a command.","Classify Please bring the book. 1. It asks the listener to act. 2. The understood subject is you. 3. It is a polite command, with a full stop.","Classify Where is the book? and The book is on the desk. Explain their end marks.","Where is the book? asks a question and uses a question mark. The book is on the desk. gives information and uses a full stop.","An exclamation mark does not turn every sentence into the same grammatical structure. Judge its purpose and wording as well.","Connect sentence purpose with wording and suitable punctuation.",1,B.aE7,B.aPU,B.aOM,B.atx)
B.aN4=s(["Match singular subjects with present-tense verbs.","Match plural subjects with present-tense verbs.","Ignore intervening phrases when finding agreement."],t.s)
B.aRk=s(["one bird \u2192 sings","many birds \u2192 sing","box of pencils \u2192 is"],t.s)
B.aEM=s(["Singular third-person subject takes sings.","Plural subject takes the base form sing.","Box controls agreement, not pencils."],t.s)
B.aKA=s(["Choose The bird __ each morning.|sings|sing|singing|have sing|The singular third-person subject bird takes sings.","Choose The birds __ each morning.|sing|sings|is sing|singed|The plural subject birds takes sing.","Choose I __ ready.|am|is|are|be|The present form of be with I is am.","Choose They __ ready.|are|is|am|was tomorrow|They is plural and takes are in the present.","Choose The box of pencils __ heavy.|is|are|am|be always|The main subject box is singular.","Choose Mina and Arun __ ready.|are|is|am|was today|Two subjects joined by and normally form a plural subject.","Choose You __ very clearly.|speak|speaks|is speaking by speak|speakeds|You takes the base present form speak.","What controls the verb in The basket of apples is full?|basket|apples|full|of|Basket is the main subject; of apples is a modifying phrase.","Choose The girl with two books __ here.|is|are|am|be|Girl remains singular despite the plural books in the phrase.","Which pair agrees?|The dogs run.|The dogs runs.|The dog run.|The dogs is running.|Dogs is plural, so run is the agreeing present form.","Choose The two lamps __ bright.|are|is|am|was now|The plural subject lamps takes are."],t.s)
B.a7n=new A.ce("g4","Subject\u2013Verb Agreement","A present-tense verb must agree with its subject. The bird sings uses a singular subject and sings. The birds sing uses a plural subject and sing. I sing and you sing do not add s. Forms of be also change: I am, she is, they are. Find the main subject, not the nearest noun. In The box of pencils is heavy, box is singular; of pencils does not turn it into a plural subject. Two subjects joined by and normally take a plural verb: Mina and Arun are ready.","Choose The basket of apples is/are full. 1. Find the main noun basket. 2. It is singular. 3. Of apples describes the basket. 4. Use is.","Choose verbs for The girls sing/sings and The girl with two books is/are here.","Girls is plural, so use sing. Girl is singular even with the phrase with two books, so use is.","Do not add s to a verb because its subject is plural. In ordinary simple present, the extra s usually goes with one third-person subject.","Find the main subject first, then choose its agreeing verb form.",2,B.aN4,B.aRk,B.aEM,B.aKA)
B.aPw=s(["Identify adverbs describing how, when or where.","Distinguish adjectives from adverbs in context.","Use adverbs without changing the core action."],t.s)
B.aP4=s(["walks carefully","arrived yesterday","played outside","very quiet"],t.s)
B.azZ=s(["Carefully describes how.","Yesterday describes when.","Outside describes where.","Very describes the degree of quiet."],t.s)
B.aPu=s(["Which word tells how in Mina walks carefully?|carefully|Mina|walks|none|Carefully describes the manner of walking.","Which word tells when in We arrived yesterday?|yesterday|We|arrived|none|Yesterday locates the arrival in time.","Which word tells where in They played outside?|outside|They|played|none|Outside identifies where the playing happened.","Which is an adjective in a careful reader?|careful|reader|a|reads|Careful describes the noun reader.","Which is an adverb in She reads carefully?|carefully|She|reads|book|Carefully describes the verb reads.","Does fast need ly to be an adverb in Arun runs fast?|No|Yes, fastly is required.|It must become fasterly.|It is always a noun.|Fast is an adverb describing runs without an ly ending.","What does very describe in very quiet?|the adjective quiet|a named person|the verb arrived|a plural noun|Very indicates the degree of the adjective quiet.","Which pair tells manner and time?|politely and yesterday|boy and book|red and blue|walk and jump|Politely tells how; yesterday tells when.","Which word is an adjective in a friendly child?|friendly|child|a|none|Friendly describes child even though it ends in ly.","Which adverb completes Speak __ so others can hear?|clearly|clear|child|school|Clearly appropriately describes the manner of speaking.","In We waited outside quietly, what does quietly tell?|how we waited|who waited|what object was carried|how many people waited|Quietly describes the manner of the waiting action."],t.s)
B.a7t=new A.ce("g4","Adverbs","An adverb can describe a verb: Mina walks carefully tells how she walks. We arrived yesterday tells when. The children played outside tells where. Some adverbs describe adjectives or other adverbs: very quiet; very slowly. Many manner adverbs end in ly, but not all adverbs do: fast, soon and here do not. An adjective describes a noun; an adverb often describes an action. Compare a careful reader with reads carefully. The added detail should fit the intended meaning.","Analyze The boy answered politely yesterday. 1. Answered is the action. 2. Politely tells how he answered. 3. Yesterday tells when. Both words are adverbs here.","Find the adverbs in We waited outside quietly. What question does each answer?","Outside answers where we waited. Quietly answers how we waited. Waited is the verb both help describe.","Do not assume every ly word is an adverb: a friendly child uses an adjective. Fast can be an adverb without ly.","Find the word being described, then ask how, when, where or to what degree.",3,B.aPw,B.aP4,B.azZ,B.aPu)
B.aKa=s(["State an idea covering the whole paragraph.","Use details as evidence for that main idea.","Distinguish a topic from a complete main-idea statement."],t.s)
B.atE=s(["Main idea: several ways to move pollen","Bees carry pollen","Birds can carry pollen","Wind carries pollen"],t.s)
B.aL7=s(["Covers the three examples.","One supporting detail.","A second supporting detail.","A third supporting detail."],t.s)
B.aPp=s(["What is the pollen passage main idea?|Pollen can move in several ways.|Only bees move pollen.|All flowers grow in wind.|Birds never feed.|The three carriers support several ways of moving pollen.","Which is a supporting detail in that passage?|Wind carries pollen for many grasses.|All plants need birds.|Bees never visit flowers.|Every seed flies.|The statement about wind is directly stated as an example.","Which is a topic rather than a complete main idea?|pollen movement|Pollen moves in several ways.|Wind carries pollen.|Bees carry pollen.|Pollen movement names the topic without making a complete claim.","Why is Bees carry pollen too narrow as the main idea?|It omits birds and wind.|It is always false.|It names no animal.|It is too long.|The paragraph also presents two other carriers.","Which library main idea covers stories, facts and poems?|The library has several kinds of books.|The library has only stories.|Poetry is missing.|All books are red.|Several kinds includes the three categories in the passage.","Which claim exceeds the pollen evidence?|Every flower depends only on bees.|Some birds can move pollen.|Wind carries pollen for grasses.|Bees carry pollen.|The passage does not establish every flower or only bees.","What should support a main idea?|Relevant details from the text.|An unrelated favorite fact.|Only the longest word.|The reader name.|Textual details explain and support the central statement.","Original: Mina cycles, walks and rides a bus to school. Main idea?|Mina uses different ways to reach school.|Mina never walks.|Mina owns three buses.|School is always closed.|The three travel methods share the idea of reaching school.","Which detail supports that Mina main idea?|She sometimes walks to school.|Her bag is blue.|She likes mangoes.|Her cup is small.|Walking to school is one stated travel method.","Which strategy helps find a main idea?|Connect the important repeated ideas.|Choose the first noun regardless of meaning.|Ignore the last two sentences.|Invent another paragraph.|Connections among key details reveal the broader main idea.","Original: The club paints signs and repairs benches. Main idea?|The club improves shared spaces.|The club only reads poems.|Every bench is new.|No signs exist.|Both tasks support improving the shared surroundings."],t.s)
B.a6L=new A.ce("g4","Main Idea & Details","A topic names what a paragraph is about; a main idea says what the paragraph communicates about that topic. In this original passage, Bees carry pollen between flowers. Some birds also move pollen while feeding. Wind carries pollen for many grasses. The topic is pollen movement. The main idea is Pollen can move in several ways. Bees carry pollen is one supporting detail, not the full main idea. A good main idea covers the important details without claiming more than the passage establishes.","Analyze the pollen passage. 1. Notice the repeated idea pollen. 2. List bees, birds and wind. 3. Find their shared role: moving pollen. 4. State Pollen moves in several ways.","Original passage: The class library has storybooks, fact books and poetry books. Which main idea covers all three?","The class library has different kinds of books covers the whole list. It has storybooks is only one supporting detail.","A single vivid detail is not always the main idea. Avoid adding all flowers need bees, which this passage does not say.","Use the main idea to connect important details, and keep it within the text evidence.",4,B.aKa,B.atE,B.aL7,B.aPp)
B.aty=s(["Arrange sentences in a logical sequence.","Use transitions that match the relationship.","Revise a paragraph for a consistent focus."],t.s)
B.axb=s(["First: fill the pot","Next: plant the seed","Then: water it","Finally: place it safely"],t.s)
B.arv=s(["Prepare before planting.","The seed needs prepared soil.","Water follows planting here.","The last instruction completes the process."],t.s)
B.aMw=s(["Which organization suits planting instructions?|time order|random order|alphabetical character names|opposite conclusions only|Instructions follow the sequence needed to complete the process.","Which transition introduces the next step?|next|however|despite|although only|Next marks the following step in a sequence.","Which transition signals contrast?|however|because|therefore|first|However introduces a difference or contrast between ideas.","Which transition signals a reason?|because|finally|meanwhile|similarly|Because introduces why an event or claim holds.","Which planting order is logical?|soil, seed, water|water, shoot, seed|seed, empty pot, soil|shoot, water, soil|Preparing soil comes before planting and watering.","Which pair can be linked with however?|The room is small; it has much light.|She is late; the bus broke down.|Water it; then plant it correctly.|He slept; therefore he slept.|Small size and plentiful light form a meaningful contrast.","Which sentence fits a paragraph describing a classroom?|The reading corner is beside the window.|My uncle repaired a boat elsewhere.|A whale lives far away.|Last year a different town flooded.|A reading-corner location supports the classroom description.","What does therefore mean in a logical paragraph?|as a result|at the beginning|in the same place|for an unrelated example|Therefore signals a consequence that should follow from the previous idea.","Which transition connects two similar points?|similarly|however|despite|instead|Similarly introduces a related likeness.","What should you fix before adding transition words?|The logical order and relationships.|The author shoe size.|Every noun length.|The page color only.|Transitions should reflect a sensible structure already present.","Which ending keeps a planting paragraph focused?|These steps help prepare the seed pot.|My bicycle needs repair.|A poem has rhymes.|The bus leaves at noon.|The ending returns to the planting task rather than an unrelated topic."],t.s)
B.a7l=new A.ce("g4","Paragraph Organization","Organization helps a reader follow an idea. A process paragraph uses time order: First fill the pot with soil. Next plant the seed. Then water it. A description may group details by place: near the door, beside the window. A comparison groups the same features of two things. Transitions should express the actual relationship. Because signals a reason, while however signals contrast. Keep the topic sentence and supporting details focused. A paragraph need not always begin with its main idea, but a clear topic-first structure is useful when learning to write.","Arrange planting instructions. 1. State the task. 2. Put soil before planting. 3. Put watering after planting. 4. Add first, next and then to mark time order.","Connect The room is small and It has plenty of light with a transition showing contrast.","The room is small; however, it has plenty of light shows contrast. Because would incorrectly present the light as a reason for the small size.","Transitions cannot repair an illogical order. Do not use therefore unless the second idea follows as a result.","Choose a clear pattern, order related details, and use transitions that express real connections.",5,B.aty,B.axb,B.arv,B.aMw)
B.aOT=s(["Match subjects and verbs in present-tense sentences.","Use pronouns with clear antecedents.","Distinguish complete sentences from fragments."],t.s)
B.apO=s(["The girl carries her book.","The girls carry their books.","Because the bus was late \u2192 We walked because it was late."],t.s)
B.apq=s(["Singular agreement and reference.","Plural agreement and reference.","A dependent reason attaches to an independent idea."],t.s)
B.asf=s(["Choose The girls __ their books.|carry|carries|is carrying as carries|carrys|Plural girls takes the simple present form carry.","Choose The girl __ her book.|carries|carry|carrys|are carry|Singular third-person girl takes carries.","What is the antecedent of their in The girls carry their books?|the girls|the books|carry|the classroom|Their refers to the group named the girls.","Which is a complete sentence?|We walked because the bus was late.|Because the bus was late|Near the old gate|The two bright lamps|We walked provides an independent subject and predicate.","Choose The boxes near the door __ heavy.|are|is|am|was now|Boxes is plural; the phrase near the door does not change it.","Which sentence has clear pronoun reference?|Mina put the book in the bag. The bag was wet.|Mina put the book in the bag. It was wet.|It put Mina in it.|They was book.|Repeating the bag removes uncertainty between book and bag.","Which word is an adjective in The careful driver stopped?|careful|driver|stopped|The|Careful describes the noun driver.","Which word is an adverb in The driver stopped safely?|safely|driver|stopped|The|Safely describes how the driver stopped.","Fix The girls brings her books.|The girls bring their books.|The girls brings their books.|The girls bring her books, meaning every girl.|The girls is bringing their books.|Bring agrees with girls and their refers to the whole group.","Why is Because the bus was late incomplete alone?|Because makes the clause dependent.|Bus is not a noun.|Late is always a verb.|It has no letters.|A dependent reason clause needs an independent clause.","Which revision makes an independent sentence?|The two lamps shine brightly.|The two lamps near the gate|Because the two lamps shine|Under the two lamps|Shine supplies a predicate for the subject the two lamps."],t.s)
B.a6U=new A.ce("g5","Grammar Review","Grammar connects meaning with sentence structure. In The girls carry their books, girls is a plural subject, carry agrees with it, and their refers to the girls. In The girl carries her book, the singular subject takes carries. A complete sentence has a subject and predicate that express an independent idea. Because the bus was late is unfinished by itself: because makes the reason dependent. We walked home because the bus was late completes the idea. Revision starts with meaning, then checks agreement, reference and sentence boundaries.","Revise The boxes near the door is heavy. 1. Identify boxes as the main subject. 2. Near the door is a modifying phrase. 3. Choose plural are. 4. Write The boxes near the door are heavy.","Revise The girls brings her books. The intended meaning is that all the girls bring books belonging to them.","The girls bring their books uses bring with plural girls and their to refer clearly to that group.","Do not agree with the nearest noun inside a phrase. A clause beginning because may need a main clause to make a whole sentence.","Check complete meaning, subject\u2013verb agreement and clear pronoun reference together.",1,B.aOT,B.apO,B.apq,B.asf)
B.aR_=s(["Identify independent and dependent clauses.","Join related ideas with a suitable subordinating conjunction.","Punctuate an introductory dependent clause."],t.s)
B.awJ=s(["We stayed inside.","because the path flooded","Because the path flooded, we stayed inside."],t.s)
B.aLP=s(["Independent clause.","Dependent reason clause.","Complex sentence with an introductory-clause comma."],t.s)
B.aAB=s(["Which clause can stand alone?|We stayed inside.|because it rained|although it was sunny|when the bell rang|We stayed inside expresses an independent complete idea.","Which word introduces a dependent reason clause?|because|and|or|but by itself|Because introduces a reason that attaches to a main clause.","Which is a complex sentence?|We stayed inside because it rained.|We stayed inside.|We stayed and rested.|The rain and wind.|It has an independent clause and a dependent because clause.","Which conjunction best shows contrast?|although|because|since for reason|when for time|Although marks an unexpected contrast between the ideas.","Punctuate Because it rained we stayed inside.|Because it rained, we stayed inside.|Because, it rained we stayed inside.|Because it, rained we stayed inside.|Because it rained we, stayed inside.|A comma follows the introductory dependent clause.","Which dependent clause tells time?|when the bell rang|because the path flooded|although it was costly|if the book is missing|When introduces the time of an event.","What does a dependent clause have?|A subject and verb but dependent meaning.|Only an adjective.|No possible verb.|Always two independent ideas.|A dependent clause is a clause but needs a main clause in this use.","Which sentence expresses a reason?|We hurried because the bus was leaving.|We hurried although the bus waited.|We hurried when we arrived.|We hurried and waved.|Because directly connects hurrying with its reason.","Choose the main clause in Although it rained, we walked.|we walked|Although it rained|although|rained|We walked can stand alone as the independent clause.","Which revision finishes When the bell rang?|When the bell rang, we lined up.|When the bell rang.|When the bell.|The bell when.|We lined up supplies the needed independent main clause.","Does and alone make two independent clauses a complex sentence?|No, that makes a compound sentence.|Yes, every time.|Only in past tense.|Only with three nouns.|Complex sentences require a dependent clause, not only coordination."],t.s)
B.a7i=new A.ce("g5","Complex Sentences","An independent clause can stand alone: We stayed inside. A dependent clause has a subject and verb but cannot stand alone in this use: because rain flooded the path. A complex sentence contains an independent clause and at least one dependent clause. We stayed inside because rain flooded the path gives a reason. Although it rained, we walked gives a contrast. When a dependent clause comes first, usually put a comma after it: Because the path flooded, we stayed inside. Choose the conjunction for the meaning; time, reason and contrast are different relationships.","Combine The path flooded and We stayed inside. 1. Decide that flooding explains staying inside. 2. Use because. 3. Write Because the path flooded, we stayed inside. 4. Put a comma after the introductory clause.","Combine We played outside and The sky was cloudy using although. Put the dependent clause first.","Although the sky was cloudy, we played outside. Although marks a contrast, and a comma closes the introductory dependent clause.","Do not leave although the sky was cloudy as a complete sentence. Do not use because when you mean contrast.","A complex sentence joins a complete main idea with a dependent reason, time or contrast clause.",2,B.aR_,B.awJ,B.aLP,B.aAB)
B.aFp=s(["Use roots and affixes to propose meanings.","Test a meaning against sentence context.","Choose a dictionary sense matching the use."],t.s)
B.ayX=s(["re + read \u2192 reread","help + ful \u2192 helpful","river bank / financial bank"],t.s)
B.aQ3=s(["The prefix suggests doing the reading again.","The suffix forms a describing word.","Context selects one of several meanings."],t.s)
B.at2=s(["What does reread mean?|read again|write again|read backward only|stop reading|The prefix re adds the meaning again to read.","What does unhappy mean in The unhappy child frowned?|not happy|very noisy|very helpful|always sleepy|Un indicates not, and frowned supports that meaning.","What does reusable mean?|able to be used again|never used|made only of paper|impossible to carry|Re and able combine with use to suggest use again.","In They sat on the river bank, bank means what?|land beside a river|a money institution|a classroom desk|a word ending|River and sat identify the geographic meaning of bank.","Which suffix makes help into an adjective meaning giving help?|ful|ed|ing as a verb|s as plural|Helpful is an adjective formed with ful.","What should confirm a word-part guess?|The surrounding sentence meaning.|Only the number of letters.|The page color.|The longest dictionary entry.|Context tests whether the guessed meaning fits this use.","Which word most clearly uses re to mean again?|rewrite|red|real|rest|Rewrite means write again and has the meaningful prefix re.","A dictionary lists two senses. Which should you choose?|The sense fitting this sentence.|Always the first one.|Always the shortest one.|Every sense at once.|Dictionary senses must be matched to the actual context.","What does careless most likely mean?|without enough care|full of carefulness|a person who repairs cars|able to care again|Less commonly indicates absence, making careless lacking care.","Which clue supports spotless meaning without marks?|The cloth had no stains.|The cloth was folded.|The cloth was large.|The cloth was near Mina.|No stains directly supports the meaning without spots or marks.","Which explanation uses two strategies?|Unhappy means not happy, and frowned confirms it.|It means unhappy because it is long.|Choose any familiar meaning.|Ignore the prefix and sentence.|This combines a prefix clue with contextual evidence."],t.s)
B.a7k=new A.ce("g5","Vocabulary Strategies","Use more than one clue when meeting an unfamiliar word. A prefix comes before a base: reread means read again because re often means again. A suffix can change a word job: helpful is an adjective meaning giving help. Context confirms the likely meaning. Mina reread the note to check the address supports reading again, not rewriting it. Some words have several meanings: bank can mean a financial institution or the land beside a river. Choose the dictionary meaning that fits the sentence. Word parts are useful clues, but not every visible letter group is a meaningful prefix.","Interpret reusable bottle. 1. Find use as the base. 2. Re suggests again. 3. Able suggests possible. 4. A reusable bottle can be used again. 5. Check that this fits the context.","Explain unhappy in The unhappy child frowned. Then choose the sense of bank in They sat on the river bank.","Unhappy means not happy, supported by frowned. River bank means the land beside a river, not a financial institution.","Do not assume every word beginning re means again: real is not rereading plus a prefix. Check word formation and context.","Combine word parts, context and the appropriate dictionary sense instead of relying on one guess.",3,B.aFp,B.ayX,B.aQ3,B.at2)
B.ayj=s(["Identify sequence, comparison and cause-and-effect structures.","Use signal words with the actual relationships.","Select a summary matching the structure."],t.s)
B.aPx=s(["Sequence: soil \u2192 seed \u2192 water","Comparison: bus / bicycle capacity","Cause \u2192 effect: rain \u2192 flooded path","Problem \u2192 solution: leak \u2192 repair"],t.s)
B.axx=s(["Events are ordered.","The same feature is compared.","A reason explains a result.","A response addresses a difficulty."],t.s)
B.auE=s(["First wash, then dry, then store. What structure is used?|sequence|comparison|claim and counterclaim|random description|The passage orders actions as steps.","Bus and bicycle passenger capacity is compared. What structure is used?|comparison|time sequence|problem only|unrelated listing|Two things are examined using the same feature.","Rain flooded the path, so we changed entrances. What is the cause?|heavy rain|the new entrance|the class name|the gate color|The rain explains why the path flooded.","What is a result in that rain passage?|using another entrance|rain beginning after entry|a new class name|a dry original path|The entrance change follows the flooding.","A leaking tap and a washer replacement use which structure?|problem and solution|alphabetical order|character description|rhyme|The repair responds to the stated leak problem.","Which signal often marks a result?|therefore|similarly|meanwhile|for contrast only|Therefore indicates a result or conclusion from preceding information.","Which feature should a fair comparison use?|The same feature for both things.|An unrelated feature for each.|Only one thing details.|No stated scope.|Comparing the same feature makes the relationship meaningful.","Can a longer text use several structures?|Yes, different parts can use different structures.|No, never.|Only if it has no paragraphs.|Only poems can do so.|Different paragraphs can organize ideas in different ways.","Which summary fits the leaking-tap passage?|A repair addresses a leaking tap.|All taps are new.|Rain flooded the footpath.|Buses carry more people.|The summary preserves the problem-and-response relationship.","Why should you not rely only on first?|It can introduce a point rather than a time step.|It always means contrast.|It never orders anything.|It is not an English word.|The word alone does not establish the text relationship.","Which sentence clearly uses comparison?|Both routes reach school, but one is shorter.|First open the bag.|The tap leaked.|The bell rang at noon.|Both and but compare two routes on destination and length."],t.s)
B.a7B=new A.ce("g5","Text Structure","Text structure is the way ideas are arranged. Sequence presents steps or events. Comparison examines shared features and differences. Cause and effect connects an event with a result. Problem and solution names a difficulty and a response. This original passage uses cause and effect: Heavy rain flooded the footpath, so the class used a different entrance. Rain is the cause; flooding and the entrance change are results. Signal words help, but determine the relationship from meaning. A text can contain more than one structure, especially across several paragraphs.","Analyze Buses carry many passengers, while ordinary bicycles usually carry one rider. 1. Identify the two things compared. 2. Identify the shared feature passenger capacity. 3. While marks the contrasting values of that feature.","Identify the structure: The tap leaked. The caretaker replaced a worn washer to stop the leak.","This is problem and solution. The leaking tap is the problem; replacing the washer is the proposed response. The passage states its purpose without claiming all leaks have that cause.","A word like first can introduce a point rather than a time step. Check what the ideas actually do.","Match the organization with the relationship among ideas, not with one signal word alone.",4,B.ayj,B.aPx,B.axx,B.auE)
B.aOa=s(["State an opinion as a clear claim.","Support an opinion with relevant reasons and examples.","Acknowledge a reasonable alternative."],t.s)
B.azj=s(["Claim: add shade","Reason: cooler reading place","Example: reading during a break","Concern: cost \u2192 reuse furniture"],t.s)
B.aI3=s(["Names the opinion.","Explains the intended benefit.","Makes the reason concrete.","Responds to a reasonable alternative."],t.s)
B.awO=s(["Which is an opinion claim?|Our class should add a shaded reading corner.|The room has two doors.|The bell rang at noon.|Mina wrote three words.|Should proposes a view that can be discussed.","Which reason supports the shaded-corner proposal?|Shade can make reading more comfortable on hot days.|My shoes are blue.|The moon is distant.|A bag has a zipper.|Comfort in the proposed place is relevant to the claim.","Which is a concrete paper-saving example?|Use the blank back of a draft sheet.|Everyone always agrees.|Paper is a word.|All people are careless.|Reusing a blank side shows a specific action.","Which concern should be addressed fairly?|The cost of furniture.|The writer favorite color only.|A reader name length.|An unrelated sport score.|Cost affects feasibility of the proposed corner.","Which response addresses that cost concern?|Start with existing furniture.|Ignore every question.|Call critics foolish.|Say costs do not exist.|Using existing furniture responds directly to possible expense.","Which support is weak without more explanation?|Because I said so.|Shade may improve comfort.|Use a blank page back for drafts.|Move one existing bench into shade.|The phrase does not provide an independent relevant reason.","Can an opinion paragraph contain factual information?|Yes, relevant facts can support its claim.|No, facts are forbidden.|Only false facts.|Only if all readers agree.|Facts can provide support while the proposal remains an opinion.","Which ending fits a supported recommendation?|Let us trial the corner using one existing bench.|Everyone who disagrees is wrong.|The moon is round.|We have nothing to discuss.|A feasible trial follows from the reasons and cost response.","What should you do with an unavailable survey percentage?|Do not invent it.|Invent a larger number.|Call it scientific anyway.|Hide its source.|Unsupported numbers do not become factual evidence.","Which word can mark a reason?|because|however only|first as a noun|yesterday only|Because connects a claim or event with its reason.","Which treatment of another view is fair?|Explain the concern and answer it respectfully.|Misstate it to make it silly.|Insult its speaker.|Pretend no one could question the proposal.|Fair writing addresses the actual alternative rather than attacking people."],t.s)
B.a7K=new A.ce("g5","Opinion Writing","An opinion states a view someone can reasonably discuss. Our class should add a shaded reading corner is a claim. Because it gives readers a cooler place on hot days is a relevant reason; an example can show how it might help. An opinion paragraph often presents a claim, explains reasons, and ends with a supported recommendation. Facts and opinions can work together, but do not invent facts to sound convincing. Acknowledge an alternative such as cost, then respond honestly. Not every reader must agree, and disagreement does not make a person foolish.","Plan a shaded-corner paragraph. 1. State the proposal. 2. Explain comfort and access to books. 3. Give a concrete example of reading during a break. 4. Address cost by proposing existing furniture. 5. End with a feasible request.","Choose a relevant reason for asking the class to reduce paper waste. Give an example without inventing a survey result.","Using both sides can reduce the number of sheets needed. Reusing an empty side for a draft is a concrete example. No invented survey percentage is needed.","Because I said so is not a developed reason. Do not claim everyone agrees unless reliable evidence supports it.","State a claim, support it with relevant reasons and examples, and respond fairly to alternatives.",5,B.aOa,B.azj,B.aI3,B.awO)
B.aCQ=s(["Distinguish clauses from phrases using subjects and verbs.","Separate independent and dependent clauses.","Use phrases to add precise sentence detail."],t.s)
B.aRa=s(["near the window","the bird sings","because the bird sings","carrying a bag"],t.s)
B.ayQ=s(["Prepositional phrase without a finite verb.","Independent subject\u2013verb clause.","Dependent reason clause.","A participial phrase needing a clear noun to modify."],t.s)
B.axc=s(["Which is a phrase?|near the window|the bird sings|Mina waited|the bell rang|Near the window lacks a subject and finite verb combination.","Which is an independent clause?|The dog sleeps.|while the dog sleeps|under the tree|carrying a bag|The dog sleeps can stand alone with a complete idea.","Which is a dependent clause?|because the path flooded|near the flooded path|the flooded path|Mina walked home|Because introduces dependence despite subject path and verb flooded.","What makes after the rain stopped a clause?|Rain is a subject and stopped is a verb.|It has four words.|It ends with a noun.|It names a place.|The subject\u2013verb combination distinguishes it from a phrase.","What is after the rain?|a phrase|an independent clause|a command|a full paragraph|There is no finite verb in after the rain.","Which sentence gives carrying a bag a clear subject to modify?|Carrying a bag, Mina entered.|Carrying a bag, the door was open.|Carrying a bag, the rain fell.|Carrying a bag, it was noon.|Mina is the person who can carry the bag.","Which part is a phrase in Mina waited under the tree?|under the tree|Mina waited|Mina waits|the tree grew|Under the tree adds place information without a finite verb.","Can a phrase contain a verb form?|Yes, as in carrying a bag.|No form of a verb can occur.|Only if it becomes a paragraph.|Only in future tense.|Participial phrases contain verb forms but are not finite clauses.","Which completes Because the bus was late?|we walked home.|near the door.|the red bus.|very slowly.|An independent clause finishes the dependent reason construction.","Which test distinguishes independence?|Can the clause express a complete idea alone?|Does it contain exactly three words?|Does it name an animal?|Does it start with a capital noun?|A dependent clause needs another clause in this use.","In while the dog sleeps, what is the finite verb?|sleeps|while|dog|the|Sleeps is the verb linked to the subject dog."],t.s)
B.a7G=new A.ce("g6","Clauses & Phrases","A clause contains a subject and a verb linked to that subject. The bird sings is an independent clause. Because the bird sings is a dependent clause in this use. A phrase does not have that subject\u2013finite-verb combination: near the window; the bright blue bag. A phrase can include a verb form without becoming a finite clause: carrying a bag. It adds detail, but by itself it does not establish who carries it in a complete sentence. In Carrying a bag, Mina entered, the opening phrase clearly modifies Mina.","Classify after the rain and after the rain stopped. 1. The first has no finite verb, so it is a phrase. 2. The second has subject rain and verb stopped. 3. After makes that clause dependent.","Classify under the tree, the dog sleeps, and while the dog sleeps. Explain each choice.","Under the tree is a phrase. The dog sleeps is an independent clause. While the dog sleeps contains a subject and verb but is dependent here.","A verb-shaped word alone is not enough. Look for a subject and a finite verb, then test independence.","A phrase adds detail; a clause has a subject\u2013verb structure and may be independent or dependent.",1,B.aCQ,B.aRa,B.ayQ,B.axc)
B.aP3=s(["Match pronouns with clear antecedents.","Maintain consistent person in a sentence.","Use singular they clearly for a person."],t.s)
B.aCU=s(["players \u2192 their bags","a student \u2192 their draft","I plan \u2192 I check"],t.s)
B.aEf=s(["Plural antecedent and reference.","Singular they for an unspecified person.","Consistent first-person viewpoint."],t.s)
B.aJL=s(["Choose The players brought __ bags.|their|his as the whole group|its|my as the whole group|Their refers to the plural group of players.","Is A student can submit their draft acceptable contemporary English?|Yes, singular they can refer to an unspecified person.|No, every person must be called he.|No, every person must be called she.|Only if student means a building.|Singular they avoids assigning an unsupported gender.","Which sentence keeps person consistent?|When I plan, I check the map.|When I plan, you check my map, meaning I check it.|When I plan, it check my map.|When I plan, we is checking.|I is used consistently for the speaker actions.","What is the antecedent of their in The children read their books?|the children|the books|read|the teacher|Their refers to the children who own or use the books.","What is unclear in Mina told Arun that they had won?|Who they refers to.|Whether told is past.|Whether Mina is a name.|Whether won is a verb.|They could mean a particular individual or a group without more context.","Which revision clearly names Arun as winner?|Mina told Arun that Arun had won.|Mina told Arun that they had won.|They told them they won.|It told Arun they won.|Repeating Arun identifies the intended winner.","Treat school as one institution. Choose The school opened __ doors.|its|their|his|my|Its refers to the singular institution the school.","Which form shows possession in the school and its doors?|its|it is|they is|we are|Its is a possessive form, unlike the contraction it is.","For a person using they, which verb is standard?|They are ready.|They is ready.|They am ready.|They be ready in this formal sentence.|They takes are even when it refers to one person.","Which reference is clearest?|The cup hit the bowl. The cup cracked.|The cup hit the bowl. It cracked.|It hit it and it cracked.|They hit it.|Repeating the cup removes ambiguity between two objects.","Why should a writer avoid assigning he from a name alone?|The name does not establish the person pronoun.|Every name means a place.|He is never a pronoun.|Names always require it.|Use stated pronouns or suitable neutral wording rather than assumptions."],t.s)
B.a7F=new A.ce("g6","Pronoun Agreement","A pronoun should have a clear antecedent and appropriate agreement. The players brought their bags links plural players to their. A student can submit their own draft uses singular they appropriately for an unspecified person. This is accepted contemporary English; do not invent a gender from a name. Keep person consistent: When I plan a route, I check the map, rather than switching to you without a reason. Unclear reference is a separate problem: Mina told Arun that they had won can be ambiguous if they could mean one person or a group. Name the intended winner when necessary.","Revise The players packed his bags. 1. Players names a group. 2. The bags belong to that group. 3. Use their: The players packed their bags.","Revise When I write, you check my spelling if the writer also does the checking. Then explain their in Every reader chooses their book.","When I write, I check my spelling keeps the speaker consistent. Their can refer to every reader as an unspecified individual without assigning gender.","Singular they is not automatically an agreement error. Avoid unexplained changes between I, you and they, and make references clear.","Pronouns need clear references and consistent person; singular they can refer to an individual.",2,B.aP3,B.aCU,B.aEf,B.aJL)
B.ayG=s(["Distinguish literal and figurative descriptions.","Explain simile, metaphor and personification.","Support an interpretation with specific words."],t.s)
B.aHM=s(["like a mirror","was a mirror","wind whispered"],t.s)
B.aMN=s(["Simile marks an explicit comparison.","Metaphor makes the comparison directly.","Personification gives wind a human-like action."],t.s)
B.ap5=s(["Which is a simile?|The pond was like a mirror.|The pond was deep.|The pond held fish.|The pond was near school.|Like introduces an explicit comparison of pond and mirror.","Which is a metaphor?|The pond was a mirror.|I like ponds.|The pond has water.|The pond was large.|The statement directly compares the pond to a mirror.","Which personifies wind?|The wind whispered.|The wind was strong.|The wind came from the east.|The wind moved leaves.|Whispered attributes a human-like action to wind.","What does a mirror image suggest about the pond?|A smooth reflecting surface.|Real glass replacing water.|A guaranteed deep bottom.|Many loud machines.|Mirrors suggest stillness and reflection in this context.","Is I like apples a simile?|No, like expresses preference.|Yes, all like sentences are similes.|Yes, apples are people.|Only if apples are red.|No comparison is made; the speaker states a preference.","What does a beehive classroom image suggest?|busy activity and many sounds|real bees necessarily inside|an empty silent room|a classroom under water|The comparison draws on familiar beehive activity.","What is literal in The pond contains water?|A directly stated physical fact.|A metaphor naming glass.|A human voice.|An imaginary school.|The wording states its ordinary physical meaning.","What is personified in The door groaned?|the door|Mina|a tree|the mirror|The door receives a human-like groaning action.","Which explanation goes beyond naming a device?|The simile suggests busy, noisy activity.|It is a simile, so no meaning matters.|There are bees, certainly.|It is long wording.|The explanation connects the comparison with a specific effect.","Why does context matter for imagery?|It guides which qualities the comparison emphasizes.|It makes every metaphor literal.|It removes all possible meaning.|It guarantees the same emotion for everyone.|Surrounding details constrain a reasonable interpretation.","Which sentence uses a direct comparison to suggest worry?|Worry was a heavy backpack.|I carried a backpack.|The backpack was blue.|I like my backpack.|The metaphor represents worry as a burdensome weight."],t.s)
B.a6S=new A.ce("g6","Figurative Language","Figurative language describes through comparison or imaginative wording. The pond was like a mirror is a simile using like; it suggests a smooth, reflecting surface. The pond was a mirror is a metaphor making the comparison directly. The wind whispered through the leaves is personification because whispering is a human action attributed to wind. Explain the effect, not only the device name. These original images describe stillness or soft sound; they do not claim a pond is glass or that wind has a voice. Context may change what an image suggests.","Analyze The classroom buzzed like a beehive. 1. Like marks a simile. 2. A beehive suggests busy activity and many small sounds. 3. The comparison suggests a lively, noisy room, not real bees in the room.","Explain The tired door groaned as Mina opened it. Which human quality is attributed to the door?","The door is given tiredness and a groaning human-like response. Personification makes its creaking sound vivid without making the door a real person.","Not every use of like is a simile: I like apples expresses preference. Do not turn an interpretation into a literal event.","Identify the wording, explain the comparison or human quality, and connect it to the passage effect.",3,B.ayG,B.aHM,B.aMN,B.ap5)
B.atk=s(["Distinguish stated facts from supported inferences.","Combine two clues to justify an interpretation.","Avoid claiming certainty beyond the passage."],t.s)
B.awF=s(["Clock checked twice","Foot tapping","May be impatient","Unknown: exact reason"],t.s)
B.aPz=s(["Stated action.","Second stated action.","Reasonable inference from both clues.","The passage does not identify what Arun awaits."],t.s)
B.ar_=s(["Which is directly stated about Arun?|He checked the clock twice.|He missed a bus.|He failed an exam.|He dislikes doors.|The clock checking is explicitly reported in the passage.","Which inference is supported by clock checking and foot tapping?|He may be impatient.|He certainly owns a bus.|He cannot tell time.|He is asleep.|Those actions commonly support impatience, though alternatives remain.","What is not established about Arun?|Exactly what he is waiting for.|That he checked a clock.|That he tapped a foot.|That a door opened.|No specific awaited person or event is identified.","Mina grins and raises a letter. Which inference fits?|She seems pleased about it.|The letter certainly awards a scholarship.|The letter is blank.|She cannot read.|Her gestures support pleasure but not exact letter contents.","Which two clues support expecting rain?|an umbrella and dark clouds|a blue bag and a cup|a clock and a book|a chair and a door|An umbrella and dark clouds are relevant to possible rain.","Does expecting rain prove rain begins?|No|Yes, always.|Only if the umbrella is red.|Only if there are two clouds.|An expectation is not evidence of the event actually occurring.","Which wording appropriately signals uncertainty?|may be worried|is certainly guilty|must have stolen it|cannot possibly be calm|May acknowledges that the evidence allows alternatives.","Original: Ivo reread the instructions and asked a question. Supported inference?|He may need clarification.|He certainly hates the teacher.|He has never read anything.|He will fail tomorrow.|Rereading and asking support needing clarification without proving motives.","What makes an inference stronger?|Relevant details and explained reasoning.|An invented destination.|A more dramatic story.|The longest possible claim.|Evidence and a clear connection support the interpretation.","New evidence contradicts your inference. What should you do?|Reconsider it.|Ignore the evidence.|Declare the text wrong automatically.|Repeat it more loudly.|Inferences should change when stronger evidence changes the explanation.","Which answer separates fact and inference?|She took an umbrella; she probably expected rain.|It certainly rained because she carried one.|Umbrellas prove storms.|She took a plane, though none is mentioned.|The answer distinguishes a stated action from a probable interpretation."],t.s)
B.a75=new A.ce("g6","Inference","An inference joins text evidence with reasonable knowledge. Original passage: Arun checked the clock twice and tapped his foot. When the door opened, he stood up quickly. The text directly states his actions. He may be impatient or eager is an inference supported by them. He is waiting for a particular bus is not established: no bus is mentioned. Use cautious language where several explanations fit. A strong inference explains which details support it and acknowledges limits. New evidence can change a reasonable initial interpretation.","Infer Mina mood from Mina grinned and held the letter above her head. 1. Grinned suggests pleasure. 2. Raising the letter suggests excitement. 3. She seems pleased about the letter. 4. The text does not state its contents.","Original: Lila took an umbrella and looked at dark clouds. What expectation is supported? What is still unknown?","Lila likely expects rain. The umbrella and clouds both support that expectation. We do not know that rain actually begins or where Lila is going.","A possible story is not automatically a supported inference. Do not invent exact motives, destinations or events without clues.","Use actual clues to infer, explain the reasoning, and state what remains uncertain.",4,B.atk,B.awF,B.aPz,B.ar_)
B.aKr=s(["Develop a focused thesis for a short essay.","Organize paragraphs around supporting points.","Connect evidence and explanation to the thesis."],t.s)
B.att=s(["Thesis: garden learning","Paragraph 1: observations","Paragraph 2: responsibilities","Conclusion: connect both"],t.s)
B.aDO=s(["States the focused two-part idea.","Uses growth records as evidence.","Uses shared watering tasks as evidence.","Draws together the reasoning without a new unrelated claim."],t.s)
B.aJN=s(["Which is a focused thesis?|A garden supports observation and shared responsibility.|Gardens|My essay|Things are interesting.|The sentence identifies a central idea and two supporting directions.","Which example supports observation learning?|Recording plant height each week.|Choosing a shoe color.|Counting unrelated buses.|Naming a favorite singer.|Repeated growth records provide evidence to observe and compare.","Which example supports shared responsibility?|A watering roster.|An unrelated shopping list.|A distant mountain.|A single page number.|A roster distributes a relevant ongoing garden task.","What should a body paragraph develop?|One supporting point connected to the thesis.|An unrelated new essay.|Only a title.|Every possible topic at once.|A focused paragraph contributes one part of the larger explanation.","Why explain an example after presenting it?|To show how it supports the point.|To make it unrelated.|To avoid the thesis.|To repeat only its punctuation.|Evidence needs a reasoning link to the paragraph claim.","What is the introduction role?|Establish the topic and essay direction.|Add only final evidence.|Replace every body paragraph.|List unrelated words.|The introduction orients the reader to the central idea.","What should the conclusion do?|Bring the main reasoning together.|Introduce an unrelated major argument.|Copy every paragraph word for word.|Declare all evidence unnecessary.|A conclusion connects the developed points rather than changing the subject.","Which thesis fits crossing points and visibility?|Crossings and sightlines help compare walking routes.|Every walking route is perfectly safe.|Gardens need water.|No routes have differences.|The thesis covers both proposed paragraph points without overclaiming.","Which is too broad for a short evidence-based essay?|All places everywhere are safe.|This route has two marked crossings.|Clear sightlines help compare these routes.|A watering roster shares tasks.|All places everywhere makes an unsupported universal claim.","What separates an explained argument from a list?|A connection between examples and claims.|Only more items.|A bigger font.|No paragraph divisions.|Reasoning establishes why each example matters.","Which order supports a clear short essay?|introduction, supporting paragraphs, conclusion|conclusion, unrelated list, title|examples with no central idea|one repeated sentence|This order establishes, develops and then connects the central idea."],t.s)
B.a6N=new A.ce("g6","Essay Foundations","A short essay develops a central idea over several paragraphs. A thesis states the focused idea or claim: A school garden can support learning by offering observations and shared responsibilities. One body paragraph can explain observations; another can explain responsibilities. Each paragraph needs a clear point, a relevant example, and an explanation connecting that example to the thesis. The introduction establishes the topic and direction. The conclusion draws the reasoning together instead of adding a completely new argument. A thesis is more useful than a title such as Gardens because it tells the reader what the essay will explain.","Plan the garden essay. 1. State the two-part thesis. 2. Use measuring plant growth as an observation example. 3. Explain how repeated records support comparison. 4. Use a watering roster as a responsibility example. 5. End by connecting both learning benefits.","For an essay explaining safer walking routes, suggest two body-paragraph points and a focused thesis. Avoid claiming every route is safe.","A thesis could state that crossing points and visibility help compare walking routes. One paragraph examines marked crossings; another examines clear sightlines. The scope remains a comparison, not a safety guarantee.","A list of examples is not an essay argument until you explain their relevance. Do not introduce unsupported new claims only in the conclusion.","Use a focused thesis, organized supporting paragraphs, explained evidence and a connected conclusion.",5,B.aKr,B.att,B.aDO,B.aJN)
B.aCS=s(["Distinguish simple, compound and complex sentences.","Combine related ideas without run-ons.","Choose sentence structure for emphasis."],t.s)
B.aCP=s(["Mina checked the map.","Mina checked, and Arun packed.","Before they left, Mina checked."],t.s)
B.aHX=s(["One independent clause.","Two coordinated independent clauses.","A dependent time clause plus an independent clause."],t.s)
B.aNa=s(["Which is a simple sentence?|Mina checked the map.|Mina checked, and Arun packed.|When Mina checked, Arun packed.|Mina checked; Arun packed.|The first sentence has a single independent clause and no dependent clause.","Which is compound?|Mina checked, and Arun packed.|Before Mina checked, Arun packed.|Mina and Arun packed water.|Checking the map, Mina waited.|And coordinates two independent subject\u2013verb clauses.","Which is complex?|Because the path was steep, we slowed.|The path was steep.|We slowed and rested.|We slowed; the path was steep.|The because clause is dependent and attaches to a main clause.","Which boundary fixes a comma splice?|Mina checked; Arun packed.|Mina checked, Arun packed.|Mina checked Arun packed.|Mina checked, packed Arun.|A semicolon can connect closely related independent clauses.","Which conjunction expresses cause?|because|although|while for time|and only|Because explicitly marks a reason for the main clause.","Does a longer sentence always contain more clauses?|No|Yes, length determines clauses.|Only in past tense.|Only with names.|Count subject\u2013verb clause structures, not sentence length.","Which sentence gives sudden closure emphasis?|The bridge was closed.|When they eventually arrived after a long walk, the bridge was closed.|The bridge, which they had expected to cross, was closed.|Although they had planned carefully, they found the bridge closed.|The short independent statement foregrounds the turning point.","Which combination shows simultaneous actions?|While Mina checked, Arun packed.|Because Mina checked, Arun packed.|Although Mina checked, Arun packed.|Mina checked after Arun finished.|While establishes overlap in time.","Which simple sentence has a compound subject?|Mina and Arun packed water.|Mina packed, and Arun checked.|When Mina packed, Arun checked.|Mina packed; Arun checked.|Two nouns share one predicate in one independent clause.","Choose the complete complex sentence.|Before we left, we checked the gate.|Before we left.|We checked, we left.|Before the departure.|The introductory dependent clause is completed by a main clause.","Why vary sentence structure?|To clarify relationships and control emphasis.|To ensure every sentence is longest.|To avoid all punctuation.|To conceal the main point.|Structure communicates how ideas connect and which deserve emphasis."],t.s)
B.a6O=new A.ce("g7","Sentence Variety","A simple sentence has one independent clause: Mina checked the map. A compound sentence coordinates independent clauses: Mina checked the map, and Arun packed water. A complex sentence adds a dependent clause: Before they left, Mina checked the map. Vary structure to show relationships, not just to make a sentence longer. A short sentence can emphasize a turning point: The bridge was closed. A longer sentence can explain circumstances. Two independent clauses need an appropriate boundary; a comma alone makes a comma splice in standard edited English.","Combine The path was steep and We slowed down. 1. Decide that steepness explains slowing. 2. Use because to express the cause. 3. Write We slowed down because the path was steep. 4. Keep The path was steep; we slowed down if a closer balanced connection is intended.","Combine Mina checked the map and Arun filled the bottles as equal independent actions. Then make checking the map a time clause.","Mina checked the map, and Arun filled the bottles coordinates the actions. While Mina checked the map, Arun filled the bottles makes the timing relationship explicit.","A long sentence can still have only one clause. Do not attach independent clauses with a comma alone.","Choose simple, compound or complex structure to express the actual relationship and desired emphasis.",1,B.aCS,B.aCP,B.aHX,B.aNa)
B.aJm=s(["Identify active and passive voice.","Preserve tense and participants when changing voice.","Explain when an agent is omitted."],t.s)
B.atQ=s(["Mina repaired the gate.","The gate was repaired by Mina.","The gate was repaired."],t.s)
B.ayH=s(["Active: agent is subject.","Passive: receiver is subject and agent is retained.","Passive: the agent is omitted."],t.s)
B.avl=s(["Which sentence is active?|Mina repaired the gate.|The gate was repaired by Mina.|The gate was repaired.|The gate is being repaired.|Mina is the agent and the grammatical subject.","Which sentence is passive?|The bench was painted by the club.|The club painted the bench.|The bench looked new.|The club was cheerful.|Was painted presents the receiver as subject.","Who is the agent in The window was cleaned by Arun?|Arun|the window|cleaned|was|By Arun identifies who performed the cleaning.","Change The club painted the bench to passive.|The bench was painted by the club.|The bench paints the club.|The club was a bench.|The bench will paint the club.|Was painted keeps past tense and the same action participants.","Change The window was cleaned by Arun to active.|Arun cleaned the window.|Arun cleans the window.|The window cleaned Arun.|Arun was clean.|The stated agent becomes subject and past tense remains.","Is Mina was tired necessarily passive?|No, it describes a state.|Yes, all was sentences are passive.|Yes, tired is an object.|Only if Mina is plural.|A form of be alone does not establish passive voice.","What is omitted in The gate was repaired?|the agent|the receiving subject|past tense|the repair action|The sentence does not identify who repaired the gate.","Why might a report use passive voice?|The agent is unknown and the result is the focus.|All active sentences are wrong.|Past tense requires passive.|Every sentence must omit responsibility.|Passive can appropriately foreground a result when the agent is unknown.","Which passive preserves present tense in They clean the room?|The room is cleaned by them.|The room was cleaned by them.|The room will be cleaned by them.|They were cleaning the room.|Is cleaned matches the simple present action.","Who receives the action in Mina repaired the gate?|the gate|Mina|the club|no one or nothing|The gate is the thing acted on by repaired.","What concern can arise from agentless passive in a complaint?|Responsibility may be hidden.|The action can never happen.|The tense disappears.|The sentence is automatically false.|Leaving out the agent can obscure who is responsible."],t.s)
B.a7r=new A.ce("g7","Verb Voice","Voice organizes who acts and who receives an action. In Mina repaired the gate, the subject Mina is the agent; this is active voice. In The gate was repaired by Mina, the subject gate receives the action; this is passive voice. Passive voice commonly uses a form of be plus a past participle. The agent may be omitted: The gate was repaired. That omission can be useful if the agent is unknown or unimportant, but it can also hide responsibility. Not every sentence with was is passive: Mina was tired describes a state.","Change The club painted the bench to passive. 1. The bench receives the action. 2. Move it to subject position. 3. Keep past tense with was painted. 4. Retain the agent by the club.","Change The window was cleaned by Arun to active, keeping the same time and participants.","Arun cleaned the window makes the stated agent the subject and preserves the past action and object.","Do not change past to present during a voice change. Was happy is not passive just because it contains was.","Identify the agent, receiver and verb form; use voice to choose a clear sentence focus.",2,B.aJm,B.atQ,B.ayH,B.avl)
B.aO6=s(["Choose a word sense using surrounding evidence.","Distinguish denotation from connotation.","Explain how word choice affects a description."],t.s)
B.avo=s(["current \u2192 moving water","cozy \u2192 comfortable smallness","cramped \u2192 restricted space"],t.s)
B.aRL=s(["Downstream establishes the water sense.","Usually a favorable association here.","Usually an unfavorable association here."],t.s)
B.anv=s(["What does current mean in The current carried leaves downstream?|moving water|present time|electrical wiring necessarily|a school term|Downstream identifies the flowing-water sense.","What does cozy emphasize about a small room?|comfort|danger certainly|exact width|absence of furniture|Cozy typically gives smallness a comfortable association in this context.","What does cramped emphasize?|unpleasantly limited space|comfortable warmth|large size|certain cleanliness|Cramped focuses on restriction rather than comfortable smallness.","What is denotation?|A word basic referential meaning.|Every possible emotion.|The number of syllables.|The author biography.|Denotation concerns the meaning a word directly refers to.","What is connotation?|Associated feeling or judgment.|Only the spelling.|A fixed measurement.|A punctuation rule.|Connotation concerns associations beyond basic reference.","Which pair can share thinness but differ in tone?|slender and skinny|river and mountain|book and chair|open and closed|Slender and skinny can describe thinness with different judgments.","Which word fits a crowd at a narrow exit?|crowded|spacious|deserted|unoccupied|The crowd and limited exit space support crowded.","Can one word establish a room exact size?|No|Yes, cozy means exactly three meters.|Yes, cramped names a fixed area.|Only if repeated.|Descriptive word choice does not supply exact dimensions.","Which sentence establishes charge as a price?|The shop charged five coins.|The horse charged forward.|The wire carries charge.|The court filed a charge.|The shop and coin amount identify the payment sense.","Why should connotation be explained in context?|Audience and nearby language affect interpretation.|Words never have meaning.|Every word is positive.|Every word is negative.|Associations depend partly on use and reader context.","Which choice is more neutral for a factual description?|The room has a floor area of twelve square meters.|The room is horribly cramped.|The room is wonderfully cozy.|The room is a perfect paradise.|The measurement describes size with less evaluative wording."],t.s)
B.a7A=new A.ce("g7","Vocabulary in Context","A word sense depends on context. The current carried leaves downstream uses current for moving water, not present time. Denotation is a word basic referential meaning; connotation is an associated feeling or judgment. Slender and skinny can both describe thinness, but skinny often sounds less favorable. Word choice matters in the original sentence The cottage was cozy: cozy suggests comfortable smallness; cramped would emphasize unpleasant lack of space. These associations vary with context and audience, so support your interpretation with nearby language instead of treating every word as permanently positive or negative.","Interpret The crowd pressed against the narrow exit. 1. Pressed suggests close physical movement. 2. Narrow exit supports limited space. 3. Crowded is more suitable than spacious. 4. Explain why the alternative does not fit.","Compare The room was cozy with The room was cramped. What shared fact may they suggest, and what judgment differs?","Both can suggest a small room. Cozy emphasizes comfort; cramped emphasizes restriction. Neither word alone establishes the room exact measurements.","Do not choose a dictionary first meaning automatically. Connotations are context-sensitive, not exact measurements or universal emotional guarantees.","Use sentence evidence to choose meaning and explain the judgments carried by particular wording.",3,B.aO6,B.avo,B.aRL,B.anv)
B.auB=s(["Identify setting, character and conflict in an original story.","Connect actions to characterization.","Distinguish a theme from a topic or plot summary."],t.s)
B.aRs=s(["Setting: dock after sunset","Conflict: hurry or return bag","Choice: deliver to caretaker","Theme: responsibility can cost convenience"],t.s)
B.aEC=s(["Place and time.","Competing priorities for Lila.","Action supporting characterization.","A supported idea beyond the single event."],t.s)
B.apN=s(["What is the story setting?|The village dock after sunset.|A classroom at noon.|A city airport at dawn.|An unnamed desert.|The first sentence explicitly gives place and time.","Who is the central character?|Lila|the sunset|the dock|the bag itself|Lila makes the central decision in the story.","What conflict does Lila face?|Hurrying home versus returning the bag.|Choosing a bus color.|Winning a race against a bird.|Finding a new school.|The passage presents convenience and responsibility as competing priorities.","Which action supports responsible characterization?|Taking the bag to the caretaker.|Wanting to hurry alone.|Seeing sunset alone.|Standing at a dock alone.|Delivering the lost bag addresses another person need.","Which is a possible theme?|Responsibility may require inconvenience.|Lost bag.|Lila found a bag and returned it.|Village dock.|The statement expresses a broader supported idea.","Which is a topic rather than a full theme?|responsibility|Helping can require sacrifice.|Responsible choices may be inconvenient.|Honesty can matter more than convenience.|Responsibility names a subject without developing a thematic claim.","Which is a plot summary?|Lila finds a bag and delivers it.|Responsibility can cost convenience.|Generosity has value.|A dock after sunset.|It recounts the story events rather than interpreting their broader meaning.","Arun shares his needed pencil. What trait is supported?|generosity|certain lifelong perfection|hatred of pencils|avoidance of school|Sharing a needed resource supports a generous action.","Why qualify a theme as possible?|Another supported interpretation may also fit.|No evidence is needed.|Every theme is equally supported.|The setting is irrelevant.|Themes are reasoned interpretations rather than a single mechanical label.","Which detail names time rather than place?|after sunset|village dock|caretaker office|the traveler bag|After sunset locates the scene in time.","What should a literary interpretation cite?|Specific actions and wording.|Only the reader preference.|A fabricated event.|An unrelated biography.|Text evidence supports the connection between elements and meaning."],t.s)
B.a7f=new A.ce("g7","Literary Elements","Read this original miniature story: At the village dock after sunset, Lila found an unclaimed bag. She wanted to hurry home, but carried it to the caretaker. A traveler returned for it and thanked her. Setting includes place and time: the dock after sunset. Lila is a character. Her conflict is between convenience and responsibility. Her decision helps characterize her as responsible. A possible theme is Choosing responsibility may require a personal inconvenience. Lost bag is a topic; listing the events is a plot summary. Theme is an interpretation supported by the story, not a fact every reader must express identically.","Analyze Lila choice. 1. Identify her desire to hurry. 2. Identify her decision to deliver the bag. 3. Explain the competing priorities. 4. Connect the choice to responsibility and a possible theme.","Original: Arun shares his last pencil with a classmate despite needing it. What action supports characterization? Propose a cautious theme.","Sharing the needed pencil supports generosity. A possible theme is that helping others can involve a personal sacrifice; the passage supports this without describing Arun whole life.","Do not call sunset a character or a lost bag a complete theme. One action supports an interpretation, not a claim about every future action.","Link setting, character choices and conflict to a theme grounded in the actual story.",4,B.auB,B.aRs,B.aEC,B.apN)
B.aK0=s(["State a clear explanatory focus.","Organize a process or explanation logically.","Use examples and precise terms without unsupported claims."],t.s)
B.aBS=s(["Catch rain","Read marked scale","Record at a consistent time","Empty for next interval"],t.s)
B.aG9=s(["Introduces the model purpose.","States what the observer measures.","Keeps the comparison interval clear.","Prevents accumulation from being mistaken for new rainfall."],t.s)
B.atX=s(["What is the main purpose of expository writing?|Explain a subject.|Always persuade a vote.|Always invent a plot.|Hide all procedures.|Expository writing primarily develops information and explanation.","What does scale mean in this rain-collector model?|measurement marks|fish covering|a musical sequence|a climbing action|The marks and reading context establish the measurement sense.","Why empty the collector after recording?|To begin a new measurement interval.|To erase all records.|To guarantee future rain.|To increase yesterday rainfall.|Emptying prevents earlier water from being counted again.","Which organization fits this procedure?|Step-by-step sequence.|Unrelated opinion order.|Alphabetical colors.|A plot twist first.|A procedure should present actions in their required order.","Which explanation is relevant?|Consistent reading times support comparison.|I like blue umbrellas.|All rain is always identical.|Containers never vary.|The statement connects a procedure with its measurement purpose.","Which claim is too strong for this classroom model?|It gives exact regional rainfall everywhere.|It collects water at one location.|Its scale shows water depth.|Its observer records readings.|One improvised local collector cannot establish exact regional measurements.","What does interval mean here?|time between readings|the container color|the observer height|a cloud shape|Interval refers to the measurement period.","Which detail improves an explanation?|Why the observer follows a step.|Only a favorite color.|A made-up result.|An unrelated story ending.|Reasons help a reader understand the procedure rather than memorize it.","Which sentence is mainly opinion, not this explanation?|I think rain is beautiful.|The container catches water.|The observer records the depth.|The collector is emptied.|The sentence states a personal judgment rather than explaining the process.","Why define an unfamiliar term?|To make the explanation understandable.|To make it longer without purpose.|To remove the main idea.|To avoid examples.|A definition clarifies the specific meaning used in the explanation.","Which limit should the writer acknowledge?|Placement and design affect measurements.|The model has no limits.|One collector measures every region.|Records are never needed.|The physical arrangement constrains what the simple model can establish."],t.s)
B.a7I=new A.ce("g7","Expository Writing","Expository writing explains a subject rather than primarily narrating a story or urging a vote. This original model explains a school rain collector: A clean open container catches rain. A marked scale shows the water depth. The observer records the reading at a consistent time and empties the container afterward. A process explanation needs steps in order and reasons for important procedures. An explanatory paragraph can define unfamiliar terms, such as scale meaning measurement marks here. Distinguish this simple model from a professional weather-station instrument; do not claim exact regional rainfall from one improvised classroom container.","Explain the reading procedure. 1. Identify the purpose: compare daily readings. 2. State the consistent reading time. 3. Explain that emptying resets the collector for the next interval. 4. Acknowledge that placement and design affect measurement.","Draft a two-sentence explanation of why a collector is emptied after recording. Use interval to mean the time between readings.","The observer empties the collector after recording so the next reading represents the next interval rather than accumulated water from earlier intervals. Consistent intervals make comparison more meaningful.","Do not replace an explanation with I like rain. Distinguish a classroom model from a precise validated instrument.","Explain a focused subject with logical organization, defined terms, examples and appropriate limits.",5,B.aK0,B.aBS,B.aG9,B.atX)
B.ayl=s(["Correct agreement and pronoun case in context.","Repair misplaced modifiers.","Maintain parallel grammatical structures."],t.s)
B.at9=s(["Mina and I checked.","The teacher spoke to Mina and me.","Reading, drafting and revising"],t.s)
B.arA=s(["Subject pronoun I.","Object pronoun me after to.","Parallel activity forms."],t.s)
B.aLw=s(["In standard edited English, choose __ checked the plan.|Mina and I|Mina and me|Her and me|Us|I is a subject pronoun in the coordinated subject.","Choose The teacher spoke to Mina and __.|me|I|we|she|The preposition to takes the object form me.","Which test helps choose I or me?|Remove the other name and test the role.|Always choose I.|Always choose the longest form.|Count nearby commas.|The remaining clause reveals whether the pronoun is subject or object.","Which modifier clearly describes Arun?|Walking to school, Arun saw the sign.|Walking to school, the sign was damaged.|Walking to school, the bag was blue.|Walking to school, the rain was loud.|Arun is the person who performs the opening walking action.","Which list is parallel?|reading, drafting and revising|read, drafting and revise|reading, to draft and revised|reads, draft and revision|All three items use matching ing activity forms.","Choose The teacher thanked Arun and __.|me|I|we|he|Thanked takes an object, so me is appropriate.","Which sentence agrees with its subject?|The boxes of chalk are heavy.|The boxes of chalk is heavy.|The box of chalk are heavy.|The boxes is heavy.|Plural boxes controls the verb are.","What is wrong with Walking to school, the sign was damaged?|The phrase seems to make the sign the walker.|Sign is not a noun.|Damaged cannot be past.|There is no comma.|The opening modifier must attach to a sensible stated subject.","Which revision keeps Mina as walker?|Walking to school, Mina was soaked by rain.|Walking to school, rain soaked Mina.|Walking to school, Mina bag was wet.|Walking to school, it rained.|Mina is now the subject modified by the walking phrase.","Why is always choosing I unreliable?|Pronoun form depends on grammatical role.|I is never correct.|Me is never correct.|Only names can be subjects.|Subject and object positions require different pronoun forms.","Which sentence has parallel purpose phrases?|We meet to plan, to write and to revise.|We meet to plan, writing and revised.|We meet planning, to write and revision.|We meet planned, write and revising.|The repeated to plus base verb structure presents matching purposes."],t.s)
B.a6K=new A.ce("g8","Grammar & Usage","Edited usage helps make relationships clear. Use subject pronouns for subjects: Mina and I checked the plan. Use object pronouns after a verb or preposition: The teacher spoke to Mina and me. Remove the other name to test the case: spoke to me. A modifier should clearly attach to its intended word. Walking to school, Arun saw the damaged sign makes Arun the walker; Walking to school, the sign was damaged incorrectly suggests that the sign walked. Parallel structures present comparable items in matching forms: reading, drafting and revising.","Revise The teacher thanked Arun and I. 1. Remove Arun and. 2. The teacher thanked I sounds wrong. 3. Use me as the object. 4. Restore The teacher thanked Arun and me.","Revise Walking to school, the rain soaked Mina. Keep Mina as the person walking. Then make read, drafting and revise parallel.","Walking to school, Mina was soaked by the rain attaches the phrase to Mina. Reading, drafting and revising gives three parallel forms.","Do not choose I merely because it sounds formal. Parallelism concerns matching roles and forms, not making every word identical.","Check grammatical role, modifier attachment and matching forms to make sentence relationships clear.",1,B.ayl,B.at9,B.arA,B.aLw)
B.aF5=s(["Analyze how voice shifts sentence focus.","Revise agentless passive when accountability matters.","Retain passive when the receiver is appropriately foregrounded."],t.s)
B.aqq=s(["Council increased fees.","Fees were increased.","Bridge has been repaired."],t.s)
B.avp=s(["Active names the responsible actor.","Agentless passive may obscure responsibility.","Passive can foreground a completed repair result."],t.s)
B.aKy=s(["Which foregrounds the council as actor?|The council repaired the bridge.|The bridge was repaired.|The bridge has been repaired.|The bridge was open.|The council is the active grammatical subject.","Which omits the fee-increase agent?|Fees were increased.|The council increased fees.|The committee voted on fees.|Mina reported the fees.|The passive sentence does not identify who acted.","Which revision restores known accountability?|The council increased fees.|Fees became fees.|Fees were somehow increased.|There was an increase somewhere.|The council is named as the action agent.","Which preserves present perfect when changing The council has repaired the bridge?|The bridge has been repaired by the council.|The bridge was repaired by the council.|The bridge will be repaired.|The bridge is repairing the council.|Has been repaired retains present perfect passive aspect.","When may passive appropriately omit an agent?|When the agent is unknown or irrelevant to the focus.|Whenever a writer wants to hide responsibility.|Only in future tense.|Never.|The receiver or outcome may be the relevant information.","Is every passive sentence poor writing?|No, appropriateness depends on purpose.|Yes, without exception.|Only if it contains a noun.|Only if it has by.|Voice should be evaluated in its communicative context.","What should a responsibility report retain?|A known relevant actor.|Only the receiving object.|Only the shortest sentence.|No stated action.|Naming the responsible agent supports accountability.","Which sentence is a state description rather than passive action?|The council was tired.|The bridge was repaired.|The letter was delivered.|The fees were increased.|Was tired describes a state rather than a passive action.","The actor is unknown. Which is the cautious report?|The window was broken; the actor is unknown.|Mina broke the window, without evidence.|Arun certainly broke it.|The council must have broken it.|The passive reports the event without inventing responsibility.","What changes between active and passive equivalents?|The grammatical subject and information focus.|Necessarily the actual event.|Necessarily the truth value.|Necessarily the date.|Equivalent voice forms can report the same event while shifting focus.","Which passive retains plural present agreement?|The rooms are cleaned each day.|The rooms is cleaned each day.|The room are cleaned each day.|The rooms am cleaned each day.|Plural rooms takes are in the passive verb phrase."],t.s)
B.a77=new A.ce("g8","Active & Passive Voice","Active and passive voice can state the same event with different focus. The council repaired the bridge foregrounds the council action. The bridge was repaired by the council foregrounds the bridge. The bridge was repaired omits the agent. A report on bridge condition may reasonably focus on the bridge; a report investigating responsibility may need the agent. Passive voice is not automatically dishonest or poor style. Check whether omitting a known relevant agent weakens clarity. In present perfect passive, The bridge has been repaired combines has been with the participle repaired; preserve that aspect when rewriting.","Revise Fees were increased when the council is known to be responsible. 1. Identify the hidden action agent. 2. Make council the subject. 3. Use The council increased fees. 4. Explain that responsibility becomes explicit.","Change The council has repaired the bridge to passive without changing its completed-to-present perspective.","The bridge has been repaired by the council preserves present perfect aspect. The bridge was repaired would change the grammatical time perspective.","Not every passive needs replacing. Do not invent an agent if the passage leaves it unknown.","Choose voice for clear focus and accountability while preserving tense, aspect and participants.",2,B.aF5,B.aqq,B.avp,B.aKy)
B.aNz=s(["Identify repetition, rhetorical questions and inclusive language.","Connect a device to an intended audience effect.","Separate persuasive wording from factual proof."],t.s)
B.apk=s(["We need ... We need ...","Will we leave doors closed?","We / together"],t.s)
B.aG0=s(["Repetition emphasizes a pattern of needs.","Rhetorical question invites reflection about a choice.","Inclusive language suggests shared participation."],t.s)
B.aC_=s(["What device is repeated We need?|repetition|a literal measurement|a footnote|a definition only|The same phrase is deliberately repeated across clauses.","What does inclusive we suggest?|Shared membership or responsibility.|Proof that everyone agrees.|An unnamed rival only.|A singular inanimate object.|We places the speaker and audience in a shared frame.","What is the main function of the question about closed doors?|Invite reflection on a council choice.|Report a measured vote.|Define a noun.|Describe a mathematical theorem.|The question presents inaction as a choice to reconsider.","Which statement overclaims the audience effect?|Every listener was certainly persuaded.|The wording may emphasize shared needs.|The question can prompt reflection.|The repetition makes a pattern audible.|The passage reports no unanimous audience response.","What should a device analysis connect?|Exact wording, audience and purpose.|Only a device name.|Only sentence length.|The reader unrelated preference.|A reasoned explanation links technique with the rhetorical situation.","Does repetition prove that a proposal is affordable?|No|Yes, if repeated three times.|Only when it rhymes.|Only with we.|Affordability requires relevant cost evidence, not repetition.","Which example uses a rhetorical question?|Will we ignore this chance to help?|At what time does the bus leave?|What is the measured room length?|Which number is printed here?|The question is intended to prompt reflection on a value-based choice.","Which wording creates a parallel pattern?|We read, we discuss, we discover.|We read, discussing, discover things.|Read, we discussion, discovered.|We reading and to discussed.|The repeated we plus verb structure creates parallel rhythm.","Can emotional language coexist with good evidence?|Yes, assess the evidence separately.|No, emotion makes all claims false.|Only if evidence is hidden.|Only if no claim is made.|Appeals and evidence serve different functions and need separate evaluation.","Who is the specified audience of the original speech?|the school council|an unrelated court|every future child|a sports referee|The lesson explicitly places the speech before a school council.","Which interpretation explains rather than only labels?|Repetition stresses several shared needs for the council.|This is repetition.|This has words.|This is a question mark.|The interpretation connects the pattern to audience and meaning."],t.s)
B.a6X=new A.ce("g8","Rhetorical Devices","Read this original speech to a school council: We need a reading space. We need a quiet place. We need a chance to learn together. Will we leave these doors closed? The repeated We need foregrounds common needs and gives rhythm. We includes the speaker with the audience. The question invites reflection rather than merely asking for a fact. Rhetorical analysis explains how wording serves a purpose for a particular audience. It does not prove all listeners react identically. A vivid device can make a proposal memorable, but it cannot replace evidence about its costs or practical effects.","Analyze We need repeated three times. 1. Quote the repeated phrase. 2. Identify repetition at the starts of clauses. 3. Explain the pattern of shared needs. 4. Link that pattern to the council proposal without claiming guaranteed persuasion.","Explain what the rhetorical question Will we leave these doors closed? asks council members to reconsider.","The question frames continuing closure as a shared choice requiring thought. The inclusive we connects listeners with responsibility; it does not demonstrate that they approve the proposal.","Naming a device is only the first step. Emotional language is not automatically false, and repetition is not factual proof.","Identify wording, explain its intended effect for the audience, and evaluate evidence separately.",3,B.aNz,B.apk,B.aG0,B.aC_)
B.aA5=s(["Separate a claim from evidence and reasoning.","Evaluate whether evidence supports the stated scope.","Identify a counterargument fairly."],t.s)
B.aQp=s(["Claim: trial later opening","Evidence: recorded requests","Reasoning: test demand and cost","Counterargument: staffing"],t.s)
B.awz=s(["A limited proposal, not a universal result.","Shows some interest if records are reliable.","Explains why a trial may be useful.","Raises a relevant practical concern."],t.s)
B.aEG=s(["What is the library proposal claim?|Trial later opening.|Every pupil wants later opening.|Opening is free.|Thirty pupils live elsewhere.|The proposal recommends a limited evening-access trial.","What is its stated evidence?|Recorded requests for later access.|A guaranteed cost saving.|Every pupil vote.|A real published national survey.|The invented request records supply the specific evidence.","What do requests support most directly?|Some expressed interest.|Universal pupil support.|No staffing costs.|Certain permanent success.|Requests indicate interest within the records, not every pupil preference.","Which is a relevant counterargument?|Staffing the later hours may cost more.|The proposer wears blue.|The room name is short.|The school mascot is cute.|Staffing affects the feasibility of the proposed trial.","What is the reasoning for a limited trial?|It can test demand and cost before permanence.|It guarantees every result.|It removes all need for records.|It proves no one objects.|The trial responds to uncertainty about use and feasibility.","Why is ten volunteers prove all pupils weak?|The sample may not represent all pupils.|Ten is never a number.|Volunteers cannot have opinions.|All surveys are always false.|A small self-selected sample cannot justify the universal scope.","What should a reader ask about the request count?|Are requests unique and reliably recorded?|What color is the cover?|Is thirty a long word?|Did the writer use enough exclamation marks?|Record quality affects the evidence strength.","Which conclusion is appropriately scoped?|These requests justify investigating possible demand.|Everyone agrees forever.|No cost can occur.|The trial has already succeeded.|Investigating demand follows more cautiously from the available evidence.","How should a counterargument be represented?|Accurately before evaluating it.|As a deliberately silly version.|Only by insulting its speaker.|Without its main reason.|Fair evaluation addresses the actual competing argument.","Is the count a real-world statistic?|No, it is invented lesson data.|Yes, it describes every school.|Yes, it is a government report.|Only if memorized.|The lesson explicitly labels its proposal data as invented.","What differentiates reasoning from evidence here?|Reasoning links requests to the proposed trial.|Reasoning is the number thirty.|Evidence is only the author opinion.|There is no connection needed.|The inferential link explains why the evidence bears on the claim."],t.s)
B.a7J=new A.ce("g8","Reading Arguments","An argument offers a claim supported by reasons and evidence. Original invented school proposal: The library should trial later opening. Thirty recorded requests this month asked for later access. A one-evening trial could test demand and cost before a permanent change. The claim proposes a trial. Requests are relevant evidence of some interest, not proof that every pupil wants it. The reasoning connects interest with a limited test. A counterargument might question staffing or cost. Evaluate the strength of that concern and the response instead of treating all disagreement as hostility. The request count is invented lesson data, not a real school statistic.","Evaluate the proposal. 1. Identify its limited claim: a trial. 2. Identify requests as evidence of interest. 3. Ask whether requests are unique and representative. 4. Check whether the trial can measure staffing and use.","An invented proposal says Ten volunteers liked the new timetable, so all pupils prefer it. Identify the unsupported jump.","A small volunteer group cannot establish a preference for all pupils. The conclusion exceeds the sample scope and may suffer selection bias.","A number is not automatically reliable or representative. Respond to the actual alternative, not a distorted version.","Identify claim, evidence, reasoning and counterargument, then check relevance, reliability and scope.",4,B.aA5,B.aQp,B.awz,B.aEG)
B.aOd=s(["Develop a qualified and contestable claim.","Support it with evidence and an explicit warrant.","Answer a counterargument without misrepresentation."],t.s)
B.apQ=s(["Claim: trial a second station","Evidence: observed queue","Warrant: access may reduce waiting","Response: monitor maintenance"],t.s)
B.asW=s(["Contestable and limited.","Needs documented real observations in an actual proposal.","Explains the evidence-to-claim connection.","Answers a relevant counterargument."],t.s)
B.axP=s(["Which claim is suitably limited?|Trial a second refill station and monitor use.|Every station fixes every problem.|Nobody ever waits.|All pupils agree.|A monitored trial limits the proposal and its implied certainty.","Which evidence is relevant to waiting time?|Recorded queues at the existing station.|The writer favorite sport.|A distant city name.|A mascot picture.|Queue observations address the proposed access problem.","What is the warrant?|More accessible capacity may reduce waiting.|The proposal title.|Only the queue number.|An insult to an opponent.|The warrant connects evidence about queues with the proposed response.","Which is a fair counterargument?|Maintenance may require staff time.|The writer is foolish.|All stations are evil.|No one can ask a question.|Staff time is a relevant feasibility concern.","Which response genuinely addresses maintenance?|Record cleaning time and repair costs during a trial.|Pretend maintenance never exists.|Repeat the claim loudly.|Insult the caretaker.|The response proposes a way to assess the actual concern.","Which word qualifies an uncertain result?|may|certainly forever|always without exception|guaranteed|May signals a possibility that still requires evidence.","What should a real proposal do with invented lesson data?|Replace it with documented real evidence.|Present it as a measured school result.|Hide that it is invented.|Multiply it by ten.|Illustrative data cannot stand in for real observations.","Which conclusion follows the model argument?|A limited trial can test whether the station helps.|All possible results are already proven.|No costs should ever be recorded.|Opponents must be ignored.|The argument supports investigation rather than a guaranteed outcome.","What is a concession?|Acknowledging a valid point in an opposing view.|Giving up every claim.|Misquoting a critic.|Replacing evidence with a slogan.|Concession recognizes an actual concern before responding.","Which is an attack on a person rather than an argument?|The critic is foolish, so the concern is false.|Maintenance time needs measurement.|Queues suggest access constraints.|A trial would limit initial commitment.|Insulting a critic does not refute the maintenance concern.","What should connect each evidence paragraph to the claim?|Explicit analysis explaining relevance.|Only a decorative heading.|An unrelated story.|No reasoning at all.|Analysis makes the support relationship clear to the reader."],t.s)
B.a7v=new A.ce("g8","Argument Writing","Argument writing defends a position through reasoning. A workable claim is Our school should trial a bottle-refill station near the courtyard. Evidence might include a documented queue at the existing station; the warrant explains that a second accessible point could reduce waiting. This is a plausible connection, not a guaranteed result. A counterargument might identify maintenance cost. Respond with a limited trial and planned cost review rather than pretending cost does not exist. The model uses invented observations for learning; real writing needs real records, clear attribution and appropriate permissions for any data collection.","Draft the core argument. 1. State a limited trial proposal. 2. Present a relevant observed queue. 3. Explain how access may affect waiting. 4. Acknowledge maintenance. 5. Propose monitoring both use and cost.","Write a response to The new station may create maintenance work. Use a concession and a feasible response.","Maintenance is a real concern, so the trial should record cleaning time and repair costs before any permanent decision. This concedes the issue and proposes evidence rather than dismissal.","A warrant is the reasoning connection, not another slogan. Do not invent survey figures or attack the person raising an objection.","Use a focused claim, relevant evidence, explained reasoning, fair counterarguments and qualified conclusions.",5,B.aOd,B.apQ,B.asW,B.axP)
B.aRr=s(["Distinguish restrictive and supplementary clauses.","Control modifier attachment and parallelism.","Explain grammatical changes in meaning."],t.s)
B.aKi=s(["teams that arrived early","teams, which arrived early,","Having reviewed evidence, Mina revised."],t.s)
B.aOL=s(["Restricts the set of teams.","Adds information about an identified set.","Mina is the logical reviewer and reviser."],t.s)
B.aqo=s(["Which clause restricts the set of students?|Students who finished may leave.|The students, who finished, may leave.|The students may leave.|Students may leave, fortunately.|Without supplementary commas, who finished limits which students qualify.","What does the comma-marked version imply about the identified students?|They finished, as additional information.|Only some unspecified students finished.|No students finished.|Completion is irrelevant and unstated.|The supplementary clause describes the identified group.","Which opening modifier attaches logically?|Having reviewed the evidence, Mina revised her claim.|Having reviewed the evidence, the claim was revised.|Having reviewed the evidence, it was noon.|Having reviewed the evidence, the page was blue.|Mina can perform both reviewing and revising.","Which purpose list is parallel?|to observe, to record and to compare|to observe, recording and comparison|observation, to record and compared|observing, recorded and to compare|All three items share the to plus base-verb structure.","Why can relative-clause commas change meaning?|They distinguish limitation from added information.|They make all claims past tense.|They create new nouns.|They always remove the subject.|Restrictive and supplementary clauses differ in scope.","Only signed reports qualify. Which wording preserves that?|Reports that contain signatures are valid.|Reports, which all contain signatures, are valid.|Every report is valid regardless of signatures.|Reports contain valid people.|The restrictive clause selects the reports satisfying the condition.","Which subject controls agreement in The list of reports is complete?|list|reports|complete|of|The main noun list is singular despite the plural object of of.","Which sentence avoids a comma splice?|The evidence was limited; the claim was revised.|The evidence was limited, the claim was revised.|The evidence was limited the claim was revised.|The evidence, the claim revised.|A semicolon appropriately connects the independent clauses.","Which pronoun reference is clearest?|Mina compared the report and the summary. The report was incomplete.|Mina compared the report and summary. It was incomplete.|They compared it with it.|It was incomplete after it.|Repeating report identifies which of two documents was incomplete.","What is the main revision priority?|Preserve the intended relationships and meaning.|Add every possible comma.|Make every sentence passive.|Replace all short words.|Grammar choices should clarify the intended claim.","Which sentence preserves a contrast clause correctly?|Although the sample was small, the pattern was useful.|Although the sample was small.|The sample was small, the pattern was useful.|Although, useful pattern sample.|The dependent contrast clause attaches to a complete main clause."],t.s)
B.a6R=new A.ce("g9","Advanced Grammar","Grammar can change a claim scope. Students who finished the draft may leave identifies only the students meeting the condition: the relative clause is restrictive. The students, who finished the draft, may leave presents completion as supplementary information about the whole identified group. Commas therefore affect meaning, not merely pauses. Modifiers also need clear attachment: Having reviewed the evidence, Mina revised her claim makes Mina the reviewer. Use parallel constructions for comparable ideas: to observe, to record and to compare. Edited grammar should preserve the intended meaning rather than apply rules mechanically.","Revise The reports which contain signatures are valid when only signed reports qualify. 1. Identify the limiting condition. 2. Keep the relative clause restrictive. 3. Do not insert supplementary-clause commas. 4. The reports that contain signatures are valid makes the restriction explicit.","Explain the difference between The teams that arrived early entered and The teams, which arrived early, entered.","The restrictive version selects early-arriving teams from possible others. The supplementary version describes the already identified teams as arriving early. Context determines whether that broader implication is intended.","Do not add commas to every relative clause. A dangling modifier can assign an action to the wrong subject.","Check what a clause restricts, what a modifier attaches to, and whether coordinated structures match.",1,B.aRr,B.aKi,B.aOL,B.aqo)
B.aQ5=s(["Choose precise academic terms by context.","Distinguish observation, inference and evaluation.","Use cautious terms matching evidence strength."],t.s)
B.aKO=s(["observe \u2192 record what happened","infer \u2192 interpret evidence","evaluate \u2192 apply criteria","suggests \u2192 limited support"],t.s)
B.aDH=s(["Describes an evidence-gathering action.","Adds a reasoned conclusion.","Judges strength or value using a basis.","Avoids overstating certainty."],t.s)
B.aI7=s(["Which verb means record or notice evidence?|observe|infer|evaluate|refute automatically|Observe concerns directly noticing or recording information.","Which verb means draw a supported interpretation?|infer|observe only|copy|enumerate only|Infer connects evidence with a conclusion beyond direct observation.","Which verb means judge using criteria?|evaluate|list only|spell|repeat|Evaluation assesses quality or strength against stated criteria.","Which term names a testable proposed explanation?|hypothesis|established proof|page title|guarantee|A hypothesis is proposed and may be tested rather than presumed true.","Which wording fits limited evidence?|The pattern suggests a change.|The pattern proves every cause.|Everyone certainly agrees.|The result is guaranteed forever.|Suggests appropriately marks support that is not conclusive.","What does compare normally examine?|Similarities and differences using a shared basis.|Only spelling lengths.|Unrelated features.|Only one text.|Comparison needs a meaningful common basis across items.","What does contrast emphasize?|differences|identical wording only|citation dates only|all possible similarities only|Contrast focuses on differences between the compared items.","Counting library visitors is mainly what?|observation|an unsupported conclusion|a value judgment by itself|a guaranteed causal explanation|The counting records directly observed occurrences.","Judging a source using authorship and methods is what?|evaluation|only repetition|only narration|only a hypothesis name|The reader applies reliability criteria to the source.","Why can proves be too strong?|Alternative explanations may remain.|It always means a title.|No evidence can matter.|Long words are always correct.|Proof claims exceed evidence that still allows competing explanations.","Which choice best serves academic clarity?|A precise familiar term used correctly.|A difficult term used incorrectly.|Many unrelated technical words.|No explained relationship.|Accurate meaning matters more than vocabulary that merely sounds impressive."],t.s)
B.a7_=new A.ce("g9","Academic Vocabulary","Academic vocabulary should make relationships precise. Observe means notice or record; infer means derive a supported interpretation; evaluate means judge using stated criteria. Compare examines similarities and differences, while contrast emphasizes differences. In an invented reading task, two texts describe the same event from different viewpoints. Comparing them requires a shared basis, such as how each describes responsibility. A hypothesis is a testable proposed explanation, not an established conclusion. Words such as suggests and is consistent with mark limited support; proves is too strong when the evidence permits alternatives. Clear plain language is preferable to an impressive term used incorrectly.","Interpret The pattern suggests increased use. 1. Pattern names observed evidence. 2. Suggests marks an inference rather than certainty. 3. Ask whether another explanation fits. 4. Do not replace suggests with proves without stronger evidence.","Choose observe, infer or evaluate for counting library visitors, proposing a reason for a change, and judging a source against reliability criteria.","Counting visitors is observation. Proposing a supported reason is inference. Judging reliability using criteria is evaluation. These activities can inform one another but are not identical.","A hypothesis is not automatically confirmed. Technical vocabulary cannot repair unsupported reasoning.","Use precise verbs and qualified claims to distinguish what was recorded, interpreted and judged.",2,B.aQ5,B.aKO,B.aDH,B.aI7)
B.axf=s(["Form an interpretive claim about an original passage.","Connect diction and imagery to that claim.","Explain textual evidence rather than merely summarize."],t.s)
B.aNU=s(["Claim: hesitation","Evidence: ticket left folded","Evidence: stepped back","Technique: bench waited"],t.s)
B.anK=s(["Interpretation of the combined details.","An action withholding preparation.","An action delaying departure.","Personification contributes anticipation."],t.s)
B.av3=s(["Which claim is supported by the passage?|Mina hesitates about departure.|Mina certainly lost her family.|The train never arrived.|The station has no clock.|Not unfolding the ticket and stepping back support hesitation.","Which phrase best supports withheld preparation?|did not unfold it|empty bench|station clock|the train arrived|The phrase shows Mina delaying a possible preparatory action.","What technique appears in the bench waited?|personification|literal dialogue|numerical evidence|a citation|Waiting attributes an expectant human-like action to a bench.","Which sentence is analysis rather than summary?|Stepping back reinforces hesitation at departure.|Mina held a ticket.|A train arrived.|A bench was beneath a clock.|The statement explains how an event supports an interpretation.","What does the clock plausibly emphasize?|passing time and anticipation|a proven deadline|a guaranteed accident|Mina exact age|The clock contributes temporal pressure without specifying a deadline.","Which claim exceeds the text?|Her hesitation is certainly caused by a family tragedy.|She stepped back.|She held a folded ticket.|The train arrived.|No family tragedy is described or established.","What should follow a short quotation in analysis?|Explanation connecting wording with the claim.|An unrelated statistic.|Only another unexplained quote.|A demand that all readers agree.|Analysis explains why the selected language matters.","Can another interpretation be defensible?|Yes, if it explains the evidence coherently.|No, only one sentence can be correct.|Yes, even without evidence.|Only if it contradicts every detail.|Interpretations depend on reasoned support rather than one fixed label.","Which evidence confirms reluctance most directly?|She stepped back when the train arrived.|The bench was empty alone.|The clock had a location alone.|There was a noun ticket.|The action occurs precisely at the opportunity to depart.","What is the difference between summary and analysis?|Summary recounts; analysis explains how details create meaning.|Summary always invents; analysis never cites.|Both mean copying the whole passage.|Analysis is only naming the author.|The two tasks use events differently: recounting versus explaining.","Which paragraph opening is an interpretive thesis?|The passage presents departure as a moment of hesitation.|This passage has sentences.|Mina is a name.|A clock measures time.|The opening makes a claim that details can develop and support."],t.s)
B.a72=new A.ce("g9","Literary Analysis","Original passage: The empty bench waited beneath the station clock. Mina touched the folded ticket but did not unfold it. When the train arrived, she stepped back. An analysis might argue that the passage creates hesitation about departure. Waited personifies the bench and prolongs anticipation. The folded ticket and stepping back supply actions supporting reluctance. Summary reports these events; analysis explains how the chosen details work together. The passage does not state why Mina hesitates, so an interpretation should not invent a family tragedy or a missed deadline as fact. More than one reading may be defensible if it explains the evidence.","Build a paragraph. 1. Claim the passage emphasizes hesitation. 2. Cite did not unfold it. 3. Explain withholding action despite holding a ticket. 4. Add stepped back as confirming behavior. 5. Limit the conclusion to hesitation, not an invented motive.","Analyze the effect of the station clock and the verb waited. How do they support anticipation without proving Mina reason?","The clock highlights passing time, while waited gives the setting an expectant quality. Together they support anticipation, but no specific reason for Mina behavior is supplied.","Do not equate analysis with a device list or plot recap. Quote only the wording needed and explain its relevance.","Make an interpretive claim, select exact details, explain their effect and acknowledge evidence limits.",3,B.axf,B.aNU,B.anK,B.av3)
B.aKR=s(["Distinguish quotation from paraphrase.","Attribute borrowed ideas as well as exact words.","Connect evidence with the scope of a claim."],t.s)
B.anC=s(["Exact words \u2192 quotation","New wording \u2192 paraphrase","Lira (2024), p. 3 \u2192 teaching locator","Twenty volunteers \u2192 limited scope"],t.s)
B.aK6=s(["Keep wording exact and mark the borrowing.","Preserve meaning while changing wording and structure.","This invented example illustrates attribution, not a real source.","The sample does not represent every pupil automatically."],t.s)
B.aFw=s(["What distinguishes a direct quotation?|Exact source wording with quotation marks and attribution.|Any new wording without a source.|Only changing a numeral.|A made-up statement.|A quotation reproduces the source wording and identifies its origin.","Does a paraphrase need attribution?|Yes, the idea is still borrowed.|No, changing words removes all borrowing.|Only if it is long.|Only if numbers are absent.|Source ideas require acknowledgment even in new wording.","What is the invented sample size?|twenty volunteers|every pupil|twelve schools|two countries|The source explicitly reports twenty volunteer participants.","How many volunteers visited twice?|twelve|twenty|three|all pupils|The quoted source reports twelve repeat visitors.","Which conclusion is supported?|Some volunteers returned during this trial.|All pupils require the corner.|The corner caused better grades.|Every school had the same result.|Repeat visits support limited interest within this sample.","Is replacing Twelve with 12 a full paraphrase?|No|Yes, any one change is sufficient.|Only if a comma is added.|Only if the title is removed.|A genuine paraphrase changes wording and structure while keeping meaning.","What does page 3 help locate?|The relevant passage in the source.|Every publication in a field.|The sample entire population.|An author age.|A page locator directs the reader to the cited evidence.","May Lira 2024 be presented as real research?|No, it is an invented teaching source.|Yes, the year makes it real.|Yes, if no one checks.|Only in a long essay.|The lesson explicitly labels the source and data as invented.","Which paraphrase preserves the claim?|Twelve trial volunteers made at least two visits.|Every volunteer visited daily.|Two volunteers visited twelve schools.|Nobody returned.|The paraphrase keeps both count and repeat-visit meaning.","Why explain evidence after citing it?|To show what it supports and what it does not.|To avoid any reasoning.|To turn a sample into everyone.|To replace the source with opinion.|Analysis establishes the connection and limits of the evidence.","What should determine real citation formatting?|The course assigned style and verified source details.|This invented example in every situation.|The longest possible title.|The writer favorite punctuation.|Requirements and actual bibliographic details govern real formatting."],t.s)
B.a7E=new A.ce("g9","Evidence & Citation","Use this invented source for practice only: Lira, 2024, School Reading Trial, page 3: Twelve of twenty volunteers visited the reading corner twice. A quotation reproduces exact words and needs quotation marks plus attribution. A paraphrase restates the idea in genuinely new wording and structure, while still naming its source. For example, Lira reports that twelve participants made at least two visits during the trial. The evidence concerns volunteers in one trial, not every pupil. This lesson uses a simple author-year-page teaching model; follow the citation style assigned in a real course. The source and figures are invented and must not be cited as real research.","Use the source responsibly. 1. Identify the limited sample. 2. Paraphrase the repeat visits accurately. 3. Attribute to invented Lira (2024). 4. Explain what the result supports. 5. Do not infer universal demand or causation.","Is changing Twelve to 12 enough to make a paraphrase? Explain how you would use the source in a real assignment.","Changing only a number format preserves the original wording and is not a genuine paraphrase. A real assignment needs a real verified source, accurate attribution and the assigned style.","Quotation marks alone do not identify a source. Reworded ideas still need attribution. Never pass invented teaching sources off as real publications.","Represent a source accurately, attribute words and ideas, and explain evidence within its actual scope.",4,B.aKR,B.anC,B.aK6,B.aFw)
B.aPa=s(["Develop a debatable text-based thesis.","Organize paragraphs by analytical claims.","Connect evidence, explanation and conclusions."],t.s)
B.aR0=s(["Thesis: hesitation through technique","Paragraph: folded ticket","Paragraph: clock and arrival","Conclusion: connect the pattern"],t.s)
B.axr=s(["Names a defensible interpretive relationship.","Analyzes withheld preparation.","Analyzes time pressure and response.","Returns to the interpretation without inventing a motive."],t.s)
B.atn=s(["Which thesis is analytical?|The scene builds hesitation through time imagery and withheld actions.|This essay is about Mina.|The passage has a train.|I will mention the station.|The claim connects textual techniques with an interpretation.","Which paragraph point develops that thesis?|Leaving the ticket folded delays preparation.|Mina has a name.|Trains have wheels.|The author used ink.|The point links a selected detail to the central interpretation.","What should follow quoted evidence?|An explanation of its analytical significance.|Only a page break.|An unrelated fact.|A claim that all readers agree.|A quotation needs reasoning connecting it with the paragraph point.","Which detail supports time imagery?|the station clock|the author invented height|an unstated deadline|a missing bus|The clock is an actual time-related detail in the passage.","Which detail supports withheld action?|the ticket remains folded|a stated successful boarding|Mina buying breakfast|a described letter delivery|Leaving the ticket folded shows preparation not completed.","Which conclusion stays within the evidence?|The pattern suggests hesitation, while the cause remains unstated.|Mina certainly feared a named relative.|The scene proves all travelers hesitate.|Every train is unsafe.|The conclusion connects the interpretation while acknowledging missing motive.","What is a weak essay thesis?|This essay is about the station.|The clock imagery increases anticipation.|Delayed actions emphasize reluctance.|The scene links time and uncertain departure.|An announcement of topic does not make an interpretive claim.","How should body paragraphs be organized?|By related analytical points.|By unrelated favorite words.|By page color.|Without any connection to the thesis.|Each paragraph should develop a part of the interpretation.","What distinguishes analytical writing from plot recap?|It explains how details produce meaning.|It never refers to events.|It must invent motives.|It consists only of quotations.|Analysis uses events as evidence for an interpretation.","Which revision improves The ticket is there?|The folded ticket represents readiness held back.|The ticket is a noun.|The ticket has letters.|The ticket exists somewhere.|The revision makes the detail function within an interpretive claim.","Why should a thesis be revisable?|Closer reading may reveal contrary evidence.|No evidence should affect it.|Every first guess is final.|Only spelling matters.|An analytical claim should respond to the actual passage evidence."],t.s)
B.a7u=new A.ce("g9","Analytical Essays","An analytical essay explains how a text constructs meaning. Use the original station scene: Mina holds a folded ticket, leaves it folded, and steps back when the train arrives. A focused thesis is The scene builds hesitation through withheld actions and time imagery. One paragraph can analyze the ticket; another can analyze the clock and arrival. Each begins with an analytical point, cites relevant wording, and explains its contribution. A thesis should not simply announce This essay is about Mina. A conclusion can draw together the pattern while respecting what the passage leaves unresolved, such as her exact reason.","Plan the ticket paragraph. 1. Claim that withheld preparation creates hesitation. 2. Cite the ticket left folded. 3. Explain that readiness is possible but delayed. 4. Connect the detail to stepping back. 5. Return to the thesis.","Revise This essay is about the station into an analytical thesis using the clock and delayed departure.","The station scene makes departure feel uncertain through time imagery and delayed actions is a focused analytical thesis. It states a relationship the essay can demonstrate.","Avoid plot retelling with no analysis. Do not introduce an unsupported motive in the conclusion or use a quotation as a self-explaining paragraph.","Develop a focused interpretation through organized claims, selected evidence and explicit reasoning.",5,B.aPa,B.aR0,B.axr,B.atn)
B.axk=s(["Explain how syntax controls emphasis and pace.","Revise wordy sentences without losing meaning.","Distinguish effective style from a universal rule."],t.s)
B.aDm=s(["After three checks, Mina crossed.","Mina crossed after three checks.","Because \u2192 not due to the fact that"],t.s)
B.aJE=s(["Preparation is foregrounded.","Main action is foregrounded.","A concise connector can preserve the reason."],t.s)
B.anA=s(["Which foregrounds preparation?|After three checks, Mina crossed.|Mina crossed after three checks.|Mina crossed.|The bridge was crossed.|The introductory phrase places preparation before the main action.","Which foregrounds the main action first?|Mina crossed after three checks.|After checking three times, Mina crossed.|Before she crossed, Mina checked.|Having checked, Mina crossed.|The sentence starts with the main subject and action.","Which concise replacement fits due to the fact that?|because|however|therefore|meanwhile|Because preserves the reason relationship with fewer words.","Which replacement fits made a decision to return?|decided to return|returned uncertainly with no decision|never returned|a decision happened somewhere|Decided to return preserves the action meaning concisely.","Why might a short sentence follow a long one?|To create emphasis or a change of pace.|To guarantee truth.|To remove all previous information.|To prevent any interpretation.|A brief contrast in length can make a turning point stand out.","What must concision preserve?|Necessary meaning and qualifications.|Only the shortest word.|No agents.|No uncertainty.|Removing limits can change the strength and accuracy of a claim.","What is a periodic sentence feature?|Its main clause is delayed.|It must contain a question.|It repeats one noun forever.|It has no verb.|Delaying the main clause can build anticipation.","What is a cumulative sentence feature?|The main clause comes first, with added detail afterward.|Every dependent clause comes first.|No detail is allowed.|It cannot have commas.|A cumulative structure develops detail after its main clause.","Which revision wrongly changes meaning?|Changing uncertain result to certain result.|Changing due to the fact that to because.|Changing made a decision to decided.|Removing an accidental duplicate word.|The certainty change removes an essential qualification.","Is there one best sentence length for every purpose?|No|Yes, always exactly five words.|Yes, always the longest sentence.|Only if prose has no audience.|Effective length depends on purpose, emphasis and readability.","Which explanation analyzes syntax?|The delayed main clause builds anticipation before the action.|The sentence is good because it is long.|Every comma proves truth.|Short words remove evidence.|It connects grammatical arrangement with a plausible reading effect."],t.s)
B.a6V=new A.ce("g10","Style & Syntax","Style includes diction, syntax and rhythm. Compare these original sentences: After checking the map twice and testing the loose board, Mina crossed the bridge. Mina crossed. The longer sentence foregrounds preparation; the short one creates abrupt emphasis but omits those details. A periodic sentence delays its main clause, building anticipation. A cumulative sentence gives the main clause first and adds detail afterward. Concision removes needless repetition, not useful qualification. Because the sample was small, the result is uncertain should not become The result is certain merely to be shorter. Style choices must serve audience, purpose and meaning.","Revise Due to the fact that the path was flooded, we made a decision to return. 1. Replace due to the fact that with because. 2. Replace made a decision to return with decided to return. 3. Keep the reason and action: Because the path flooded, we decided to return.","Compare After three checks, Mina crossed with Mina crossed after three checks. What information appears first in each?","The first sentence foregrounds the checks before giving the main action. The second foregrounds crossing and then supplies its preparation. Both preserve the event relationship.","Shorter is not always better. Do not remove evidence limits, necessary distinctions or the agent just to reduce word count.","Choose syntax and diction for emphasis, pace and clarity while preserving the claim meaning.",1,B.axk,B.aDm,B.aJE,B.anA)
B.aD5=s(["Form a focused and answerable research question.","Choose sources appropriate to the question.","Record source details and separate evidence from notes."],t.s)
B.aAa=s(["Question: one school, four weeks","Evidence: comparable attendance logs","Method: how visits were counted","Limit: timetable changes"],t.s)
B.aPV=s(["Defines a feasible scope.","Directly addresses changes in use.","Determines what a visit count means.","An alternative influence prevents automatic causal claims."],t.s)
B.aDr=s(["Which question is most focused?|How did visits change during one school four-week trial?|Are libraries good?|Is everything better?|What about technology?|The question defines site, period and measurable topic.","Which source best addresses visit changes?|Comparable attendance records.|A mascot poster.|An unrelated poem.|A travel advertisement.|Attendance records directly concern the research outcome.","Why record how visits were counted?|Different methods can change what numbers mean.|Counting methods never matter.|Only titles matter.|It makes a source real automatically.|Method determines comparability and interpretation of the recorded counts.","Which detail belongs in a source log?|author, title, date and location|only the writer favorite color|only a search rank|an invented source address|Bibliographic and locating details support traceability.","How should exact borrowed wording appear in notes?|Clearly marked as a quotation with source details.|Mixed into personal prose unmarked.|Changed only by one comma.|Presented as the researcher memory.|Quotation boundaries prevent accidental misrepresentation or plagiarism.","Is a search-result snippet enough for final evaluation?|No, read the actual source.|Yes, always.|Only if it is first.|Only if it is short.|Snippets omit context, methods and source limitations.","Which factor could complicate the trial comparison?|Changed school timetables.|The source page number alone.|The title font alone.|The spelling of library alone.|Timetable changes may influence use independently of opening hours.","What does an answerable question need?|Defined scope and observable evidence.|A guaranteed preferred result.|No key terms.|An unlimited worldwide claim.|A feasible scope helps identify what evidence can answer the question.","May invented trial data be submitted as measured research?|No|Yes, if plausible.|Yes, if rounded.|Only when a graph is added.|Illustrative lesson data must not be misrepresented as authentic records.","Which source choice best follows the question?|Choose sources for relevance and documented methods.|Choose only supportive opinions.|Choose every result without reading.|Choose sources with the longest names.|Evidence should fit the question and be assessable.","Why distinguish your analysis from paraphrased evidence?|To show which claims are yours and which come from sources.|To avoid all attribution.|To make sources interchangeable.|To conceal uncertainty.|Clear note categories preserve intellectual ownership and reasoning boundaries."],t.s)
B.a76=new A.ce("g10","Research Skills","Research begins with a question narrow enough to investigate: How did library use change during a four-week evening-opening trial at one school? This is more answerable than Are libraries good? Choose evidence that addresses the question: attendance records, opening dates and a clear description of the recording method. Keep a source log with author or organization, title, date, location and relevant page or section. Separate exact quotations, paraphrases and your own analysis in notes. The model trial is invented; real research requires authentic records and permission for any private data. A search result snippet is a lead, not a substitute for reading the source.","Plan the trial investigation. 1. Define the four-week period and school. 2. Identify comparable earlier records. 3. Check how visits were counted. 4. Record limitations such as timetable differences. 5. Avoid claiming the trial alone caused a change.","Narrow Does technology help? into a question about one defined tool, task, learner group and outcome without promising a result.","How does a spelling-check tool affect revision accuracy in one class draft task? defines tool, task and group. A study would still need a clear accuracy measure and an ethical data process.","Do not treat a top search result as automatically authoritative. Do not mix copied text into your notes without marking quotation boundaries.","Ask a focused question, find appropriate authentic evidence, log sources and track limitations.",2,B.aD5,B.aAa,B.aPV,B.aDr)
B.apD=s(["Identify a text claim, assumptions and framing.","Evaluate evidence beyond persuasive presentation.","Distinguish correlation from a supported causal explanation."],t.s)
B.aDq=s(["Observed: visits increased","Claim: opening alone caused it","Alternatives: projects or counting","Framing: A Perfect Success"],t.s)
B.aEI=s(["A stated pattern.","A causal inference requiring stronger support.","Possible competing explanations.","Evaluative wording rather than new evidence."],t.s)
B.aCR=s(["Which part is a causal claim?|Evening opening alone caused the rise.|Visits increased.|The report was published.|The school added hours.|Alone caused asserts an exclusive causal explanation.","Does an event happening earlier prove causation?|No|Yes, always.|Only if a headline says success.|Only if the event is positive.|Temporal order is necessary in many causal claims but not sufficient proof.","Which is a plausible alternative influence on visits?|New class projects.|Only the report font.|Only the title punctuation.|The word evening length.|New projects may independently change library use.","What does A Perfect Success add most directly?|positive framing|a documented control group|a measurement method|an exact sample description|The headline evaluates the result rather than supplying new method evidence.","What assumption underlies nine users prove everyone?|The selected users represent all users.|All users were measured.|No one can praise tools.|Nine is a universal sample.|The generalization depends on unsupported representativeness.","Which missing detail matters for that tool claim?|How users were selected.|The advertiser shoe color.|The office wall height.|The title word count.|Selection affects whether the praised cases represent a wider population.","Which question tests works?|What outcome and measure define success?|Is the name attractive?|Does the ad repeat itself?|Are the words long?|An operational meaning is needed to assess effectiveness.","Should a viewpoint alone make a source false?|No, evaluate its support.|Yes, every perspective is false.|Only positive viewpoints.|Only negative viewpoints.|Perspective can affect framing without automatically invalidating evidence.","Which conclusion is appropriately cautious?|The rise coincided with the change; other influences need checking.|The change certainly explains every visit.|No rise occurred because causation is uncertain.|All evidence must be discarded.|Uncertain causation does not negate the observed pattern.","What does critical reading require?|Fair testing of reasoning and evidence.|Automatic disbelief.|Automatic agreement.|Only memorizing the headline.|The task is evaluation rather than a fixed accepting or rejecting stance.","Which phrase overstates evidence most clearly?|for everyone without exception|for the recorded volunteers|in this sample|may contribute|The universal phrase exceeds limited evidence."],t.s)
B.a7z=new A.ce("g10","Critical Reading","Critical reading asks what a text claims and how it supports that claim. Invented report: After the school added evening opening, visits increased. Therefore evening opening alone caused the rise. The first sentence reports a sequence; the conclusion adds a causal claim. New class projects, changed schedules or different counting may also explain part of the rise. A heading such as A Perfect Success frames the event positively but supplies no additional evidence. Check source methods, definitions, omissions and plausible alternatives. Critical reading means fair evaluation, not automatically rejecting every source or assuming a hidden motive.","Assess the report. 1. Separate observed increase from causal inference. 2. Identify alone as a strong claim. 3. Ask about other changes and comparable records. 4. State that the timing is consistent with an effect but does not isolate the cause.","An advertisement says Nine users praised the tool, so it works for everyone. What assumptions and missing details should a reader notice?","The claim assumes nine selected users represent everyone. It omits selection method, negative responses and the definition of works. Praise alone does not establish universal effectiveness.","Suspicion is not proof. Do not dismiss a source merely because it has a viewpoint; evaluate actual support and omissions.","Read claims, evidence, assumptions and framing separately, then test whether the conclusion follows.",3,B.apD,B.aDq,B.aEI,B.aCR)
B.aHr=s(["Identify credibility, reasoning and value-based appeals.","Evaluate techniques within audience and purpose.","Distinguish a compelling message from a supported claim."],t.s)
B.aLB=s(["Librarian role","Request log","Imagine a quiet place","Trial and publish cost"],t.s)
B.azP=s(["Relevant credibility appeal.","Potential evidence requiring verification.","An appeal to needs and values.","A response to feasibility and accountability concerns."],t.s)
B.ax_=s(["Which detail establishes relevant speaker experience?|The speaker records library requests.|The speaker wears a red coat.|The speaker repeats the title.|The speaker uses a question mark.|Directly recording requests supplies relevant experience.","Which detail offers evidence to check?|The log lists thirty requests.|Imagine a quiet place.|Let us act together.|What a wonderful idea!|The count is a factual-style claim requiring source verification.","What does the imagined quiet place primarily do?|Invite an audience to picture a valued benefit.|Prove a measured learning gain.|Calculate costs.|Document every pupil request.|The wording appeals to needs or values rather than measurement.","Why use a limited trial?|Address uncertainty and initial commitment.|Guarantee permanent success.|Remove any need for records.|Prove that cost is zero.|A trial lets a cautious audience investigate feasibility.","What does publish the cost promise?|accountability and information|certainly no expense|automatic approval|no public record|Publishing costs offers a way to review the decision.","Does an expert role guarantee every claim?|No|Yes, always.|Only when repeated.|Only with an invented count.|Expertise can support credibility but does not replace checking claims.","Which interpretation overstates the persuasive effect?|All council members certainly approve.|The trial may reduce commitment concerns.|The log may support interest.|The example can make a benefit concrete.|No audience vote or response is reported.","Can one sentence use several appeals?|Yes|No, every sentence has exactly one.|Only if it has four words.|Only if it is false.|Credibility, reasoning and values can overlap in a single passage.","What should a reader do with the count in real writing?|Verify a real record and its scope.|Treat invented lesson data as a real survey.|Add a larger invented count.|Ignore its sample entirely.|Authentic evidence and scope are necessary outside the teaching example.","Which audience concern does the final wording address?|budget and feasibility|a sports score|a travel route|the speaker favorite food|Trial scope and cost reporting address practical decision concerns.","What separates persuasion analysis from agreement?|Analysis explains techniques even if the reader questions the proposal.|Analysis must endorse the claim.|Only opponents can analyze.|Agreement replaces all evidence.|Understanding rhetorical choices does not require adopting the position."],t.s)
B.a7o=new A.ce("g10","Persuasive Techniques","Original invented appeal to a school council: As the librarian who records requests, I have seen pupils ask for evening access. Our log lists thirty requests. Imagine having a quiet place to finish a project. Let us trial one evening and publish the cost. The speaker role offers relevant credibility, the log offers evidence, and the imagined situation appeals to needs or values. These can overlap. Numbers require checking, and an expert role does not guarantee correctness. A limited trial and published costs address a cautious audience concern about feasibility. The count is invented for analysis, not an actual school finding.","Analyze the final request. 1. Trial limits commitment. 2. One evening gives a concrete scope. 3. Publish the cost promises accountability. 4. Connect those choices to a budget-conscious council rather than claiming inevitable approval.","Explain the role of Imagine having a quiet place. What does it add, and what does it fail to prove?","The phrase invites listeners to picture a benefit and connects with their values. It does not measure actual demand, cost or improvement.","Do not call every statistic sound reasoning without checking it. Appeals to feeling are not automatically invalid, but they do not replace factual support.","Analyze persuasive choices for their intended audience effects, then assess factual and logical support separately.",4,B.aHr,B.aLB,B.azP,B.ax_)
B.aKS=s(["Organize a report around a research question and evidence.","Separate findings from interpretation.","State limitations and trace borrowed material."],t.s)
B.aQ1=s(["Question","Methods","Findings","Discussion and limits"],t.s)
B.aIg=s(["Defines what the report investigates.","Explains how evidence was obtained.","Reports what the records show.","Interprets the pattern and its boundaries."],t.s)
B.avW=s(["Which section explains how visits were counted?|methods|title only|conclusion only|acknowledgments only|Methods document the evidence-gathering procedure.","Which is a finding if records support it?|Recorded visits increased in the trial sessions.|The change certainly improved grades.|Every pupil loved it.|The timetable must be the cause.|The count pattern reports observed evidence rather than a further inference.","Which belongs in discussion?|Timetable changes may explain part of the pattern.|Only an unexplained table label.|An invented participant name.|The title spelling.|Discussion interprets findings and considers alternatives.","What should a conclusion match?|The evidence scope and limitations.|The strongest imaginable claim.|Only the writer preference.|A desired result regardless of data.|Conclusions should not exceed what the research supports.","What must a real report use instead of invented trial figures?|Authentic documented evidence.|Bigger fictional numbers.|A decorative graph alone.|An unnamed rumor.|The teaching model is not a real dataset.","Why distinguish findings from interpretation?|Readers can see observations separately from proposed explanations.|It makes citation unnecessary.|It guarantees causation.|It removes the need for methods.|The distinction makes the reasoning assessable.","Which is an inappropriate grade claim?|The visit rise proves grades improved without grade evidence.|The records show more visits.|The method counted entries.|The period included four extra sessions.|Visit counts alone do not measure learning outcomes.","What should happen to unexpected evidence?|Report and evaluate it honestly.|Hide it automatically.|Replace it with invented support.|Delete all sources.|Research writing must represent findings accurately, including inconvenient ones.","Which material needs attribution?|Borrowed ideas as well as exact words.|Only direct quotations longer than a page.|Only diagrams.|Only author names.|Paraphrased source ideas are still intellectual borrowing.","Which participant information should be protected?|Private identifying details not needed for the report.|All nonidentifying aggregate counts automatically.|The report topic name.|The method description always.|Reporting should avoid unnecessary private identifiers.","Which outline is coherent?|question, methods, findings, discussion, conclusion|unrelated opinions, invented data, slogan|conclusion without a question|titles without evidence|This sequence makes the evidence and reasoning traceable."],t.s)
B.a7L=new A.ce("g10","Research Writing","A research report should connect a question, methods, findings and interpretation. In an invented library trial, the question is whether recorded use changed during four extra evening sessions. Methods explain the dates, counting process and comparison period. Findings state recorded visits; discussion considers what the pattern means and its limits. Increased visits do not by themselves prove improved grades. Attribute borrowed ideas and quote accurately, using the assigned citation style. The invented trial model teaches organization only; a real report must use authentic data and protect private participant information.","Draft a report outline. 1. Introduce the question. 2. Describe comparable counting methods. 3. Present the actual pattern without exaggeration. 4. Discuss possible explanations and limitations. 5. Conclude within the evidence scope.","Place These sessions had more recorded visits and The increase may reflect altered timetables in findings or discussion. Explain why.","More recorded visits is a finding if supported by the records. The timetable explanation is an interpretation for discussion and requires further evidence.","Do not hide inconvenient findings or invent missing data. A conclusion is not stronger merely because it omits limitations.","Make the question, method, evidence, interpretation and limitations traceable to authentic sources.",5,B.aKS,B.aQ1,B.aIg,B.avW)
B.aGN=s(["Separate a central claim from supporting subclaims.","Track qualifications and operational definitions.","Annotate evidence and unanswered questions."],t.s)
B.aGI=s(["Scope: four weeks","Measure: entries, not unique readers","Finding: more entries","Limit: no learning outcomes"],t.s)
B.ay7=s(["Defines the observation period.","Explains what was counted.","Reports the observed pattern.","Prevents an unsupported grade-improvement inference."],t.s)
B.art=s(["What was counted in the invented trial?|visits, not unique readers|every pupil grades|all national libraries|only book titles|The abstract explicitly defines entries as visits.","Which is the stated observation period?|four weeks|four years|one century|no defined period|The trial scope is four weeks.","Which claim exceeds the abstract?|The trial proves learning gains.|Recorded entries increased.|Visits may include repeat readers.|Learning outcomes were not measured.|The abstract reports no measure of learning gains.","Why annotate operational definitions?|They specify what evidence measures.|They replace all evidence.|They guarantee causation.|They identify only punctuation.|Definitions such as visits versus readers determine interpretation.","Which summary preserves a limitation?|Entries increased, but unique users and learning gains were not established.|Every pupil learned more.|The trial proved universal demand.|No library entries were recorded.|The summary retains the finding without expanding its scope.","What should a qualification do in a summary?|Remain if it limits the claim.|Disappear to make the summary stronger.|Become a guarantee.|Be replaced by a slogan.|Removing limits can misrepresent the source argument.","Which annotation category fits no learning outcomes measured?|limitation|central proof of learning|author surname|a direct measure of grades|It specifies a boundary on the possible conclusions.","What is an academic summary mainly reconstructing?|The argument and evidence relationships.|Only impressive vocabulary.|Only page color.|An unrelated opinion.|A summary should accurately represent reasoning and its scope.","Why preview headings before close reading?|To locate relevant argument and method sections.|To avoid reading all relevant evidence.|To invent a conclusion.|To treat headings as proof.|Headings help navigate; relevant content still needs close reading.","Can repeat visits increase entries without increasing unique readers?|Yes|No, entries always equal people.|Only if no one visits.|Only if grades improve.|The defined visit count can include the same reader repeatedly.","Which follow-up question is warranted?|How many unique readers made those visits?|Why did every pupil grades improve?|Why was universal demand proven?|Why are all libraries identical?|The measure leaves unique participation unresolved."],t.s)
B.a7a=new A.ce("g11","Academic Reading","Academic reading reconstructs a text argument before judging it. Invented abstract: A four-week trial recorded more library entries during extra evening sessions. Entries counted visits, not unique readers. The report suggests increased use but does not measure learning outcomes. The central finding concerns recorded entries; the qualification limits what use means. An annotation can mark claim, method, evidence and limitation in separate categories. Summarize the actual conclusion rather than a stronger claim the author did not make. Preview headings to locate methods and discussion, then read the relevant sections closely. A summary is a map of reasoning, not a collection of highlighted impressive words.","Annotate the abstract. 1. Mark the four-week period as scope. 2. Mark visits not unique readers as a definition. 3. Mark increased use as the interpretation. 4. Mark no learning-outcome measure as a limit. 5. Keep these distinctions in the summary.","Write a one-sentence summary that mentions the observed pattern and one important limitation. Do not infer grade improvement.","The invented trial recorded more evening entries, but its visit counts do not establish unique users or learning gains. The summary retains the finding and its boundaries.","Do not erase may, suggests or sample limits when summarizing. Highlighting without reconstructing connections is not close reading.","Read for claim, support, definitions and qualifications, then summarize the argument at its actual strength.",1,B.aGN,B.aGI,B.ay7,B.art)
B.aqy=s(["Analyze audience, purpose and constraints.","Explain how appeals interact in a specific passage.","Evaluate intended effects without inventing audience reactions."],t.s)
B.anR=s(["Audience: council","Ethos: librarian role","Logos: request log","Feasibility: limited trial and budget"],t.s)
B.aNm=s(["Has authority over the proposal and cost.","Supplies relevant experience.","Offers evidence requiring method checks.","Addresses the audience practical constraints."],t.s)
B.anM=s(["Who is the specified audience?|the school council|a jury deciding a crime|an unrelated sports crowd|all future readers|The passage is explicitly addressed to the council.","Which constraint shapes the final proposal?|budget and initial commitment|the speaker favorite meal|an unstated race time|a fictional court sentence|A limited trial and published budget address feasibility.","What does the librarian role support?|relevant credibility|automatic proof of every claim|a guaranteed vote|absence of bias forever|The role establishes experience relevant to request records.","What remains unknown about the thirty requests?|Their uniqueness and representativeness.|Their stated count.|The existence of a proposed trial.|The passage intended audience.|The excerpt gives a count without describing those method details.","Which analysis connects wording and effect?|Trial limits commitment for a cautious council.|Trial is a word, so it persuades everyone.|Every number proves the proposal.|The council already voted yes.|The explanation ties the specific term to audience constraints.","What does the rhetorical question invite?|Reflection about shared responsibility.|A reported unanimous reaction.|A measured budget total.|An exact number of unique users.|The question frames closure as a choice for the audience.","Can appeals overlap in one sentence?|Yes|No, categories are mutually exclusive.|Only if the sentence is incorrect.|Only in poetry.|Credibility, reasoning and values can interact in the same wording.","Which claim invents an audience response?|All council members agreed after hearing it.|The speech may address cost concerns.|The question can prompt reflection.|The role may contribute credibility.|No actual audience agreement is reported.","What is the difference between effectiveness and reliability?|A persuasive message may still use weak evidence.|Persuasive wording guarantees accurate evidence.|Reliable evidence always persuades everyone.|The concepts mean exactly the same thing.|Audience appeal and evidence quality are separate dimensions.","How should the count be treated outside this lesson?|As invented data, requiring real replacement for actual research.|As a real national statistic.|As proof for every school.|As a legal rule.|The example count is explicitly fictional teaching material.","Which analysis is incomplete?|It lists ethos and logos without explaining their role.|It connects role with relevant experience.|It connects trial with feasibility.|It notes method limitations.|Device labels alone do not analyze how communication works."],t.s)
B.a7D=new A.ce("g11","Rhetoric","Rhetoric examines communication in a situation. Original invented council speech: As the librarian who logs requests, I know thirty requests asked for later access this month. Will we keep the doors closed? A one-evening trial with a published budget lets us test the idea. The audience controls a budget, so limited commitment and accountability are relevant. The role supports ethos, the request log supports a possible logos appeal, and the shared question can appeal to values. Analyze how these choices interact rather than assigning one permanent category to every sentence. The invented count and unspecified request method still need evaluation; rhetorical effectiveness is not the same as factual reliability.","Analyze the final sentence. 1. Identify the budget-conscious audience. 2. Trial reduces initial commitment. 3. Published budget addresses accountability. 4. Test the idea presents the proposal as investigable. 5. Explain possible effects rather than claiming a vote occurred.","Explain how the librarian role and request count work together, and name one evidentiary limitation.","The role suggests relevant firsthand experience; the count offers a concrete basis for interest. However, the passage does not establish unique requests or representative demand.","Do not treat ethos as proof or pathos as automatic deception. Naming appeals without analyzing wording and situation is incomplete.","Connect wording, audience, purpose and constraints, then distinguish persuasive design from evidence quality.",2,B.aqy,B.anR,B.aNm,B.anM)
B.anx=s(["Evaluate authorship, methods and relevance.","Compare samples and corroborating evidence.","Distinguish expertise from unchecked authority."],t.s)
B.au8=s(["Authorship","Method","Scope","Corroboration"],t.s)
B.aOI=s(["Who produced the information and relevant competence.","How the evidence was gathered.","Which population, period and outcome it covers.","Whether independent evidence supports the claim."],t.s)
B.ap3=s(["Which source is more traceable for local trial visits?|The dated librarian log.|The anonymous universal advertisement.|An unrelated poster.|A copied slogan.|The log names relevant records and a defined local period.","Can the visit log alone establish learning benefits for all pupils?|No|Yes, every count proves learning.|Only if the title is long.|Only if the librarian repeats it.|Visits and learning outcomes are different measures.","Which source detail explains evidence collection?|method|font size|cover color|number of exclamation marks|Methods establish how information was obtained.","Why should expertise match the claim subject?|Expertise in one field does not guarantee another.|All titles prove every claim.|No expertise ever matters.|Every field uses identical methods.|Relevant competence matters more than prestige alone.","What is genuine corroboration?|Independent supporting evidence.|Ten sites copying one unnamed claim.|A repeated headline.|The author own repeated slogan.|Copies of one source do not provide independent confirmation.","Does a recent date guarantee reliability?|No|Yes, automatically.|Only if the page is colorful.|Only with a famous name.|Date is one contextual factor, not a quality guarantee.","Which issue matters for the anonymous advertisement?|No stated outcome or sampling method.|It has words.|It uses a short title.|It can be read on a phone.|Without methods, the broad benefit claim is hard to assess.","Which statement best expresses source relevance?|A visit log supports visit claims, not every possible outcome.|Every source supports every topic.|A source title supplies all proof.|Famous sources need no relevance check.|Relevance depends on what was actually measured and argued.","How should possible incentives be used?|As a reason to examine evidence carefully.|As automatic proof the source is false.|As a substitute for reading.|As a guarantee of accuracy.|Incentives can affect selection or framing but do not alone settle truth.","Which question checks scope?|Which pupils, dates and outcomes are included?|What color is the page?|How many letters are in the author name?|Is the heading large?|Population, period and measure determine the supported scope.","What should an evaluator do if evidence conflicts?|Compare methods and limitations before concluding.|Ignore the less convenient source.|Average every claim mechanically.|Assume both are fake automatically.|Different methods and scopes can explain apparently conflicting findings."],t.s)
B.a7q=new A.ce("g11","Source Evaluation","Evaluate sources by the claim you need to support. Invented Source A is a school librarian log describing visit counts and dates. Invented Source B is an anonymous advertisement saying every pupil benefits, with no method. A is more traceable for local visit counts; it still cannot establish every pupil learning benefit. Check who produced the source, how evidence was obtained, its date and scope, possible incentives, and whether other reliable evidence corroborates it. A recent source can be poor, and an older source can remain relevant depending on the question. Expertise should fit the subject, not merely a prestigious title.","Compare A and B. 1. Define the claim: visits during a trial. 2. A documents relevant records. 3. B supplies no sampling or outcome method. 4. Prefer A for that limited claim. 5. Still check record accuracy and seek corroboration.","A verified expert in bridge engineering comments on language learning without evidence. How should expertise and support be weighed?","Engineering credentials are not automatically language-learning expertise. Assess the actual evidence and relevant competence instead of relying on the title alone.","Do not rank a source solely by date, website appearance or a famous name. Corroboration requires independent evidence, not many sites copying one claim.","Evaluate a source for the specific claim using traceability, methods, relevant expertise and independent support.",3,B.anx,B.au8,B.aOI,B.ap3)
B.atI=s(["Identify premises, conclusions and implicit warrants.","Test conditional reasoning and alternative explanations.","Build rebuttals addressing the actual inference."],t.s)
B.aJY=s(["P \u2192 Q; P \u2192 Q follows","P \u2192 Q; not Q \u2192 not P follows","P \u2192 Q; Q \u2192 P does not necessarily follow"],t.s)
B.aAE=s(["Modus ponens is valid.","Modus tollens is valid.","Affirming the consequent permits alternative causes."],t.s)
B.aLj=s(["If P implies Q and P is true, what follows?|Q|not Q|not P|nothing at all|Modus ponens derives Q from the conditional and P.","If P implies Q and Q is false, what follows?|not P|P|Q|both P and Q|Modus tollens rules out P when its necessary consequence is absent.","What is wrong with P implies Q; Q; therefore P?|Q may have another cause.|Q is never true.|P and Q have no letters.|Every conditional is false.|Affirming the consequent fails because the consequent need not identify its cause.","What does validity evaluate?|The premise-to-conclusion connection.|Only whether the conclusion sounds attractive.|The author fame.|Only the source date.|Validity asks whether true premises require the conclusion.","What does soundness additionally require?|True premises.|A longer conclusion.|An emotional slogan.|A unanimous audience.|A sound argument is valid and has true premises.","The light is on. Does that prove the library opens under the stated conditional?|No|Yes, necessarily.|Only if the claim is repeated.|Only in daylight.|The light could be on for an alternative reason.","Which rebuttal addresses the inference?|The hall light can be on during maintenance.|The speaker wears old shoes.|The library name is short.|The question uses English.|Maintenance supplies a counterexample to the necessary opening inference.","Can an invalid argument have a true conclusion?|Yes|No, never.|Only without premises.|Only if all words are nouns.|A conclusion can happen to be true even when the reasoning does not establish it.","If incomplete forms are rejected and this form is not rejected, what follows?|It is not incomplete.|It is certainly perfect.|It was certainly rejected.|It has no title.|The contrapositive inference is valid but does not establish every quality.","What is an implicit warrant?|An unstated bridge from evidence to claim.|A decorative heading.|Only the claim spelling.|An unrelated citation number.|A warrant explains why the premises are relevant to the conclusion.","Which response is ad hominem rather than substantive?|The speaker is foolish, so the reasoning fails.|Another cause can explain the light.|The premise is unsupported.|The sample does not support the claim scope.|A personal attack does not test the argument logical support."],t.s)
B.a6P=new A.ce("g11","Argumentation","An argument has premises offered to support a conclusion. If the library opens, the hall light is on. The library opens. Therefore the hall light is on is a valid conditional pattern: if P then Q; P; therefore Q. But If the library opens, the light is on; the light is on; therefore the library opens is invalid. The light could be on for another reason. Validity concerns whether the conclusion must follow if the premises are true; soundness also requires true premises. Practical proposals often use defeasible reasoning rather than certainty, so state assumptions and respond to alternatives. The library-light scenario is invented.","Test the second argument. 1. Identify P as opening and Q as light on. 2. The argument affirms Q. 3. Another event could produce Q. 4. Therefore P is not guaranteed. 5. A counterexample refutes the claimed necessary inference.","If a form is incomplete, it is rejected. This form was not rejected. What follows if both premises are true?","The form is not incomplete. This is modus tollens: if P then Q; not Q; therefore not P. It does not prove the form is excellent in every other respect.","Do not confuse a true conclusion with a valid inference. Refute a reason or warrant rather than attacking the person.","Map premises and conclusion, test the logical connection, and distinguish validity from premise truth.",4,B.atI,B.aJY,B.aAE,B.aLj)
B.aK3=s(["Align sections with the research question and design.","Separate method, findings and interpretation.","Use an outline that makes evidence traceable."],t.s)
B.azS=s(["Question and prior work","Method","Results","Discussion and conclusion"],t.s)
B.aG3=s(["Establishes the problem and context.","Makes evidence production assessable.","Reports recorded findings.","Interprets findings while retaining limitations."],t.s)
B.aAo=s(["Which section describes door-entry counting?|methods|results only|bibliography only|conclusion only|Counting procedures explain how evidence was obtained.","Which section reports observed visit totals?|results|author biography|title only|acknowledgments|Results present the findings rather than proposed explanations.","Which section evaluates timetable influence?|discussion|source title|page header|data collection instruction only|Alternative explanations belong in interpreting the findings.","What should a literature review do?|Relate existing sources to the research question.|List unrelated summaries only.|Invent publications.|Replace every method detail.|A review builds context and identifies relationships or gaps.","Must every humanities paper use identical empirical headings?|No, structure depends on discipline and task.|Yes, always.|Only if it uses prose.|Only if it cites evidence.|Humanities analysis can organize around interpretive claims.","What is a reference list function?|Help locate cited works.|Prove every inference automatically.|Replace paragraph analysis.|Hide source details.|References provide traceability for borrowed evidence and ideas.","What should the introduction establish?|The focused question and its context.|All unrelated possible results.|Only final recommendations.|An invented universal answer.|The reader needs to know what problem the paper investigates.","Which conclusion is appropriately bounded?|The trial pattern warrants further investigation, with method limits.|All libraries now have proven benefits.|The data prove every pupil learned more.|No method needs checking.|The conclusion respects the limited invented trial evidence.","Can good headings repair weak evidence?|No|Yes, automatically.|Only with a bibliography.|Only with many pages.|Organization supports assessment but cannot create missing support.","Where should an interpretive humanities body paragraph focus?|A text-based claim supported by analysis.|An invented statistical table regardless of purpose.|Only a title.|Only unrelated methods.|Humanities structure can develop supported interpretive points.","Which outline makes the empirical argument traceable?|question, prior work, methods, results, discussion|slogan, unrelated quotation, conclusion|title, certainty, no evidence|references without any claims|The sequence connects the problem with evidence and interpretation."],t.s)
B.a6W=new A.ce("g11","Research Paper Structure","A research paper organization should match the discipline and task. An empirical report often moves from question and relevant prior work to methods, results, discussion and conclusion. A humanities paper may organize around interpretive claims instead of using identical section names. In an invented library trial, methods explain counting and comparison periods; results report visits; discussion interprets possible reasons and limitations. A literature review explains how existing sources shape the question rather than listing summaries with no relationship. A bibliography or reference list helps readers locate cited works. No structure guarantees a strong paper if the question and evidence remain weak.","Outline the trial paper. 1. State a bounded research question. 2. Review relevant authentic prior evidence. 3. Explain the counting method. 4. Report the actual pattern. 5. Discuss alternative explanations. 6. Conclude within the scope.","Where would you put A changed timetable may explain increased visits and Entries were counted at the door? Explain the distinction.","The timetable interpretation belongs in discussion. Door-entry counting belongs in methods. Both are relevant but serve different reasoning functions.","Do not force every humanities essay into an empirical template. Do not hide methods or use the conclusion to introduce unsupported findings.","Choose a discipline-appropriate structure that connects question, sources, method or analysis, evidence and conclusions.",5,B.aK3,B.azS,B.aG3,B.aAo)
B.asV=s(["Edit clause boundaries and parallel comparisons.","Place modifiers next to their intended subjects.","Preserve meaning while revising academic prose."],t.s)
B.aOz=s(["Two independent clauses \u2192 appropriate boundary","Counts before / counts during","Using records, the researcher ..."],t.s)
B.aO8=s(["A connector or semicolon must fit the relationship.","Parallel comparisons retain the same measure.","The actor using the records is identified."],t.s)
B.aRX=s(["Which repairs the comma splice?|The sample was small; the result was uncertain.|The sample was small, the result was uncertain.|The sample was small the result was uncertain.|The sample, result uncertain.|A semicolon joins two related independent clauses.","Which comparison is parallel?|Visit counts before versus visit counts during the trial.|Visit counts versus the trial.|Visits versus a researcher name.|Attendance versus four weeks as unlike measures.|Both terms compare the same feature in different periods.","Which modifier names its logical actor?|Using the records, the researcher estimated use.|Using the records, the table was blue.|Using the records, it was noon.|Using the records, the door was open.|The researcher can perform the action of using records.","Which conjunction expresses a reason?|because|although|meanwhile|and without a reason relation|Because explicitly marks the reason for the main claim.","Which revision preserves uncertainty?|The limited sample suggests a pattern.|The limited sample proves every case.|The limitation guarantees truth.|All cases are certainly identical.|Suggests maintains the limited strength of the evidence.","Which subject controls The quality of the records is important?|quality|records|important|of|Quality is the singular main noun controlling is.","Which list is grammatically parallel?|collecting, comparing and interpreting|collecting, to compare and interpretation|collect, compared and interpreting|collection, comparing and to interpret|The items share matching ing forms.","Which pair can a semicolon join directly?|Two closely related independent clauses.|A noun and its adjective only.|A preposition and its object only.|Any two random letters.|A semicolon commonly links complete related clauses.","What is wrong with comparing attendance to the trial itself?|The comparison terms are not the same kind of feature.|Attendance is never measurable.|Trial is not an English noun.|Comparisons need no basis.|A fair comparison should identify equivalent measures or categories.","Why identify the method actor when relevant?|To clarify accountability and procedure.|To ban all passive voice.|To guarantee results.|To eliminate sources.|Naming the researcher can clarify who performed the method.","Which sentence has a complete contrast construction?|Although the sample was small, its limits were reported.|Although the sample was small.|The sample small although.|Although, limits.|The dependent clause is completed by an independent main clause."],t.s)
B.a7c=new A.ce("g12","College-Ready Grammar","College-ready editing tests meaning as well as correctness. A comma cannot alone join independent clauses in standard edited prose: The sample was small, the result was uncertain is a comma splice. Use a semicolon or a reason connector as appropriate. Keep comparisons parallel: The report compares attendance before the trial with attendance during it, not attendance with the trial itself. Modifiers need logical subjects: Using the records, the researcher estimated use. Using the records, use was estimated can obscure who used the records. Grammar should help the reader track agents, relationships and qualifications.","Edit The sample was small, we interpreted cautiously. 1. Identify two independent clauses. 2. Decide that sample size explains caution. 3. Write Because the sample was small, we interpreted cautiously. 4. Preserve the qualification rather than claiming certainty.","Revise The study compares visit counts with the trial to compare the same measure in two periods.","The study compares visit counts before the trial with visit counts during the trial. Both sides now name the same measured feature at different times.","Do not shorten away the comparison basis or uncertainty. A passive sentence can be appropriate, but avoid unclear agents where method accountability matters.","Edit clause boundaries, comparison terms and modifiers so the intended academic relationships remain clear.",1,B.asV,B.aOz,B.aO8,B.aRX)
B.aN5=s(["Reconstruct a claim and its necessary assumptions.","Compare explanations against the same evidence.","Write a qualified judgment using explicit criteria."],t.s)
B.az5=s(["Claim: learning improved","Evidence: visits rose","Assumption: visits imply gains","Judgment: use measured; learning not measured"],t.s)
B.aMj=s(["The proposed outcome.","The actual recorded variable.","A link requiring evidence.","A qualified evaluation using relevance criteria."],t.s)
B.aCG=s(["What outcome does the report conclusion claim?|learning improvement|only door color|only title length|only page count|The authors move from visit counts to a learning claim.","What is actually measured?|visits|learning gains|every pupil attitude|all homework accuracy|The excerpt reports recorded use rather than learning performance.","What missing assumption connects the claim?|More visits necessarily establish learning gains.|Visits are a kind of title.|Libraries cannot be counted.|All records are fictional in reality.|The conclusion requires an unsupported link between use and learning.","Which judgment is balanced?|Use increased, but learning gains were not measured.|No visits occurred because the claim is weak.|Every pupil learned more.|All research is pointless.|It recognizes the finding while limiting the unmeasured claim.","Which alternative explanation might affect visits?|A new project deadline.|Only a comma in the title.|Only the researcher shoe size.|The number of letters in library.|A deadline can influence library use independently of extra opening hours.","Which criterion tests whether evidence fits a claim?|relevance of the measure|attractiveness of the logo|writer popularity alone|sentence count alone|A measure must address the claimed outcome.","Does critical analysis require rejecting every argument?|No|Yes, all evidence must be dismissed.|Only if a claim is interesting.|Only for positive findings.|Analysis can identify strengths as well as limitations.","What further evidence would address learning gains?|An appropriate learning-outcome measure.|More door paint.|A larger title.|A second unrelated slogan.|The claimed outcome requires a measure that actually assesses learning.","Why compare explanations against the same evidence?|To assess which accounts for the observed pattern.|To choose the most dramatic story.|To avoid checking methods.|To remove all uncertainty by wording.|Explanations should be judged by their fit with relevant records.","Which phrase maintains appropriate certainty?|The records are consistent with increased use.|The records prove every educational benefit.|The records guarantee causation.|The records show nothing because uncertainty exists.|The qualified statement reports what the evidence supports.","What should a reasoned judgment make explicit?|The criteria and evidence supporting it.|Only whether the reader likes it.|A personal attack.|An invented fact.|Explicit criteria make the evaluation assessable."],t.s)
B.a78=new A.ce("g12","Critical Analysis","Critical analysis evaluates how an argument or text works. Invented report: A school trial added evening library hours, and visits rose. The authors conclude the extra hours improved learning. The reported measure is visits, while the conclusion concerns learning; the missing assumption is that more visits necessarily produce measurable learning gains. A fair analysis acknowledges the useful use data but identifies the unmeasured outcome. Compare explanations, such as timetable changes or a new project deadline, against the same records. A judgment should state criteria: relevance of measure, method quality and scope. Critical does not mean merely negative.","Analyze the learning claim. 1. Identify the outcome learning. 2. Identify the evidence visits. 3. State the missing link. 4. Ask for an appropriate learning measure and controls. 5. Conclude that increased use alone does not establish the claimed gain.","Write a balanced judgment that credits the trial observation while challenging the stronger conclusion.","The trial offers evidence of greater recorded use, but not of learning improvement because no learning outcome was measured. Further investigation would need an appropriate outcome and competing explanations.","Do not reject the observed pattern just because the conclusion overreaches. Do not accept an appealing claim without checking its assumed links.","Evaluate measures, assumptions, explanations and scope, preserving both strengths and limits.",2,B.aN5,B.az5,B.aMj,B.aCG)
B.ayB=s(["Group sources by related claims rather than author order.","Explain differences in samples and methods.","Develop a combined conclusion without blind averaging."],t.s)
B.aFh=s(["A: 12/20 = 60% volunteers","B: 80/200 = 40% random sample","Difference: selection method","Synthesis: method-sensitive interpretation"],t.s)
B.aob=s(["Self-selection can affect representativeness.","Random selection still has design and response limits.","Percentages do not remove sampling differences.","The disagreement should be explained, not hidden."],t.s)
B.aqg=s(["What percentage supports the proposal in Source A?|60%|40%|20%|12%|Twelve divided by twenty is 0.6, or 60%.","What percentage supports it in Source B?|40%|60%|80%|20%|Eighty divided by two hundred is 0.4, or 40%.","Which sampling difference matters?|A uses volunteers; B uses random selection.|A has no people; B has no records.|Both necessarily measure all pupils.|Neither states a sample size.|The different selection methods affect representativeness.","Why is a simple average of 60% and 40% inadequate?|Samples and methods differ.|Percentages can never be computed.|Both sources have identical scope.|Averaging guarantees population truth.|The reported percentages are not interchangeable population estimates.","What should a synthesis paragraph organize around?|A shared issue such as sampling and support.|Only author names in isolation.|Only title length.|Unrelated source colors.|Thematic organization establishes relationships among source claims.","Does random selection remove every limitation?|No|Yes, all design problems disappear.|Only if the percentage is large.|Only if the source is invented.|Response, measurement and design limits can remain.","What could affect volunteer results?|People with stronger interest may self-select.|Every volunteer is certainly dishonest.|Volunteers are not people.|Sample size is always irrelevant.|Self-selection can make the sample unlike the broader population.","Which statement is synthesis?|The findings differ, and selection methods help explain their scope.|Source A says 60%; no connection follows.|Only Source B matters because it is longer.|All pupils support the proposal.|The statement connects both results with an explanatory method comparison.","Can raw supporting counts automatically estimate whole-school preference?|No|Yes, regardless of overlap or sampling.|Only if added loudly.|Only if formatted in a graph.|Population inference needs a defined design and compatible data.","How should invented sources be used in a real essay?|As teaching examples only, not real research evidence.|As verified publications.|Without disclosing invention.|As actual pupil surveys.|The model sources and figures are explicitly fictional.","What should synthesis preserve?|Agreement, differences and relevant limitations.|Only the preferred result.|No method details.|A guaranteed single answer.|Responsible synthesis retains important relationships instead of flattening evidence."],t.s)
B.a79=new A.ce("g12","Synthesis of Sources","Synthesis puts sources into a reasoned relationship. Invented Source A reports support from 12 of 20 volunteers. Invented Source B reports support from 80 of 200 randomly selected pupils. The percentages are 60% and 40%, but the samples differ. Volunteers may have unusually strong interest; random selection can improve representation, subject to response and design limits. Do not simply average percentages and declare the whole school 50% supportive. A thematic paragraph can compare how the two methods affect the preference claims. Both sources are invented teaching examples, not real publications or actual pupil records.","Synthesize A and B. 1. Calculate their stated percentages. 2. Identify volunteer versus random sampling. 3. Compare the same outcome support. 4. Explain scope and bias limits. 5. Conclude that differing findings need method-sensitive interpretation.","Why would combining the raw supporting counts, 12 and 80, not automatically produce a valid school preference estimate?","The groups may differ in selection, overlap and measurement conditions. A combined count without a defined population and sampling design cannot automatically represent the whole school.","Synthesis is not a string of separate summaries. Do not mechanically average incompatible results or erase genuine disagreement.","Compare claims, evidence and methods, then develop a conclusion that preserves relationships and limits.",3,B.ayB,B.aFh,B.aob,B.aqg)
B.aJa=s(["Write precise and appropriately qualified claims.","Develop cohesive paragraphs with explicit reasoning.","Attribute evidence without obscuring agency."],t.s)
B.aRU=s(["Scope: in this trial","Measure: recorded visits","Link: these visit counts","Limit: no learning measure"],t.s)
B.azy=s(["Avoids a universal claim.","Names the outcome actually observed.","Gives a clear reference for the next sentence.","Qualifies the inference appropriately."],t.s)
B.ayO=s(["Which claim is appropriately qualified?|Visits increased in this trial; learning was not measured.|The trial proves every benefit.|Every reader improved forever.|The result is universally perfect.|The statement separates measured use from an unmeasured outcome.","Which revision makes an actor and action clear?|The researcher recorded visits.|The implementation of utilization occurred.|There was a doing of observation.|An occurrence was undertaken.|The revision uses a concrete actor and active verb.","What is cohesion?|Clear links among sentence ideas.|Only having many long words.|Removing all repeated key terms.|Using random connectors.|Cohesion helps readers follow related ideas across sentences.","Which reference is clearer than a bare This?|These visit counts|This, with no antecedent|It, with two possible sources|That thing|The noun phrase names what the next claim concerns.","What does however appropriately signal?|contrast|a reason alone|the next time step always|proof by itself|However should connect genuinely contrasting ideas.","Does formal writing ban I in every discipline?|No, conventions depend on task and discipline.|Yes, universally.|Only when a method is described.|Only if evidence is real.|Academic conventions vary; clarity and assigned requirements matter.","What must attribution cover?|Borrowed ideas and exact wording.|Only direct quotes longer than ten pages.|Only titles.|Only numbers.|Rephrased source ideas still need acknowledgment.","Why retain limitations?|They keep the claim at its supported strength.|They make every finding false.|They remove the need for evidence.|They guarantee acceptance.|Qualifiers define what the evidence does and does not establish.","Which sentence uses evidence-linked reasoning?|The visits increased, but that measure does not assess learning.|Visits increased, therefore every pupil is excellent.|A big word proves the method.|The title is formal, so all claims are true.|It explains the relationship between outcome measure and claim.","Are all abstract nouns bad style?|No, assess whether they help or obscure meaning.|Yes, remove every one.|Only if they contain vowels.|Only in reports.|Some abstract terms are necessary; needless nominalization can obscure actions.","Which wording avoids universal overclaiming?|among the recorded participants|for everyone everywhere|without any exception|guaranteed forever|The scoped phrase matches a limited evidence base."],t.s)
B.a6T=new A.ce("g12","Academic Writing","Academic writing aims for clear, traceable reasoning rather than inflated vocabulary. Compare The trial proves every reader benefits with In this invented trial, recorded visits increased; learning benefits were not measured. The second sentence is precise about scope and evidence. Cohesion links sentences through repeated key ideas and meaningful connectors: These visit counts ... This limitation ... Such phrases should have clear referents. Formality does not require avoiding I in every discipline or making every sentence passive; follow the task and explain the method actor when relevant. Borrowed words and ideas need attribution, and limitations belong in the argument rather than being hidden.","Revise The implementation of the utilization observation was conducted. 1. Identify the actor and action. 2. If known, write The researcher recorded visits. 3. Add the defined period if relevant. 4. Preserve necessary limitations instead of adding abstract nouns.","Improve This proves success after a sentence reporting more visits. State the measured outcome and one limit.","The visit increase suggests greater recorded use during the trial, but does not establish learning improvement. This names the outcome and preserves uncertainty.","A vague this can hide what is being discussed. Long nominalized phrases can obscure actors, but not every abstract noun is wrong.","Use precise claims, clear connections, attribution and necessary qualifications to make reasoning readable.",4,B.aJa,B.aRU,B.azy,B.ayO)
B.aLM=s(["Organize a short talk for a defined audience.","Explain a visual without reading every label.","Respond to questions with evidence and acknowledged limits."],t.s)
B.aI4=s(["Opening: decision sought","Middle: demand and cost","Visual: measure and takeaway","Ending: request and questions"],t.s)
B.auX=s(["Orients the council.","Develops relevant evidence and feasibility.","Makes data understandable to listeners.","Connects the talk to the next action."],t.s)
B.axv=s(["What should the opening establish?|The purpose and decision sought.|Every unrelated detail.|Only the speaker favorite quote.|A guaranteed result without evidence.|Listeners need to know what the presentation asks them to consider.","Which signpost clarifies organization?|First demand, then cost.|Everything is obvious.|Just look at this.|No questions matter.|The phrase previews the sequence of relevant topics.","How should a speaker explain a chart?|Name its measure, axes and main takeaway.|Read only every tiny label.|Assume everyone sees the same detail.|Claim the graph proves unrelated outcomes.|A visual needs an explanation of what its data represent.","What is the honest grade-improvement response?|Visits do not measure grades; more evidence is needed.|The visit chart guarantees better grades.|Everyone certainly improves.|The question must be ignored.|The response separates the actual measure from the requested outcome.","Which ending gives a specific next action?|Please approve a four-session trial with cost reporting.|That is all, with no stated request.|Everything will be perfect.|No one may ask questions.|The ending identifies a bounded decision for the audience.","What does an oral source attribution do?|Identify the evidence origin for listeners.|Replace every explanation.|Make invented data real.|Guarantee universal relevance.|Listeners need to understand where borrowed evidence comes from.","Which accessibility support is useful?|Readable visuals and a written summary.|Tiny crowded text only.|No explanation of a chart.|Audio required for all content.|Accessible formats help listeners receive and revisit the information.","Why tailor a talk to a council audience?|Its members decide feasibility and resources.|Every audience has identical needs.|It removes all need for evidence.|It guarantees approval.|Audience responsibilities shape relevant points and explanations.","What should happen when an answer is unknown?|Acknowledge the limit and suggest needed evidence.|Invent a confident result.|Change the question secretly.|Insult the questioner.|Honesty protects the boundary between knowledge and speculation.","Which use of a slide is effective?|A clear visual supporting the spoken explanation.|A dense page read without explanation.|An unrelated picture only.|A chart with no named measure.|Slides should help the audience understand the reasoning.","Does speaking loudly replace organization?|No|Yes, always.|Only for short talks.|Only with a graph.|A coherent structure and evidence remain necessary."],t.s)
B.a7m=new A.ce("g12","Oral Communication","A clear talk connects purpose, audience and evidence. In an invented three-minute council presentation, a speaker proposes a limited library trial. The opening states the request, the middle explains relevant demand and cost questions, and the ending states the decision sought. Signposting such as First, the demand; next, the cost helps listeners follow. If a chart is used, explain its measure, axes and takeaway rather than merely reading labels. Spoken citation identifies a relevant source in understandable form. When asked for unmeasured learning benefits, acknowledge the gap rather than inventing a result. Accessibility can include a readable visual and a written summary.","Plan the talk. 1. Identify the council decision. 2. State a limited trial proposal. 3. Present verified demand evidence. 4. Explain cost uncertainties. 5. End with a specific request and invite relevant questions.","A listener asks whether the trial will improve grades, but you have only visit counts. Draft an honest response.","The visit records show use, not grade improvement. We would need a suitable learning measure to answer that question. The response remains helpful without claiming nonexistent evidence.","Do not read a crowded slide word for word or manufacture an answer to avoid saying unknown. Loudness alone does not create a coherent presentation.","State a purpose, guide the audience through evidence, explain visuals and respond within what is known.",5,B.aLM,B.aI4,B.auX,B.axv)
B.avV=s(["Use calibrated claims and discipline-appropriate register.","Make reference chains and agency clear.","Distinguish language precision from unnecessary complexity."],t.s)
B.aRT=s(["Observation: recorded visits","Interpretation: possible access benefit","Scope: this trial","Agency: the team recorded"],t.s)
B.aMm=s(["Names the measured variable.","Marks a reasoned but limited inference.","Prevents universal extension.","Makes the method actor visible."],t.s)
B.avL=s(["Which statement is closest to direct observation?|Recorded visits increased.|Learning certainly improved.|Every pupil benefited.|The trial solved all access problems.|The visit count is the recorded variable in the model.","Which wording calibrates an uncertain interpretation?|suggests a possible access benefit|proves every benefit|guarantees lasting success|demonstrates all causes|The phrase signals limited support rather than certainty.","What makes these counts a clear reference?|Its antecedent is identified attendance counts.|It can mean every noun on the page.|It replaces all definitions.|It hides the method.|A named antecedent allows the reader to track the reference.","Which revision clarifies agency?|The team recorded visits.|The implementation of recording occurred.|There was a recording occurrence.|Utilization implementation existed.|The team is explicitly identified as the actor.","Does academic English require needless complexity?|No|Yes, longer wording always improves reasoning.|Only in introductions.|Only when citing sources.|Precision and clarity matter more than inflated language.","When is hedging appropriate?|When evidence leaves uncertainty or alternatives.|In every sentence regardless of evidence.|Only to avoid all responsibility.|Only without any data.|A qualifier should match actual evidentiary limits.","Can first person be appropriate in academic work?|Yes, depending on task and discipline.|No, universally forbidden.|Only if the work lacks sources.|Only if the text is fictional.|Conventions vary, and first person can clarify the researcher role.","Which claim exceeds attendance evidence?|Universal learning improvement was proven.|Visits rose during the trial.|The team used entry counts.|The period was limited.|Attendance is not a direct learning-outcome measure.","What should utilization mean in this report?|A clearly defined measured variable such as visits.|Every possible educational benefit.|An unexplained impressive word.|Whatever the reader guesses.|An operational definition makes the technical term assessable.","Are nominalizations always wrong?|No, they can name useful concepts.|Yes, every abstract noun must go.|Only if they are plural.|Only in methods.|The issue is whether they clarify concepts or obscure agency.","Which sentence preserves both outcome and scope?|Recorded use increased during this trial.|Every institution improved permanently.|All learners mastered the subject.|Success was universal without measurement.|The statement names the measured outcome and limited setting."],t.s)
B.a70=new A.ce("college","Academic English","Academic English makes claims and relationships assessable. In an invented report, The trial recorded higher attendance accurately names an observed result. The trial suggests a possible access benefit adds a qualified interpretation. The trial proves universal learning improvement overstates what attendance measures. Discipline conventions differ: active voice and first person can be appropriate when they clarify methods. A reference chain such as attendance counts \u2192 these counts \u2192 this measure keeps the topic identifiable. Nominalization can name an abstract process, but excessive abstraction can hide agency: The implementation of recording occurred is less clear than The team recorded visits. Precision includes clear scope, defined terms and justified hedging.","Edit A utilization increase was observed and it proves everything. 1. Name the actor if known. 2. Define utilization as recorded visits. 3. Separate observation from interpretation. 4. Write The team recorded more visits; this suggests greater use during the trial, not every learning benefit.","Replace These results demonstrate success with a claim that names the outcome and preserves the limited trial scope.","The increased recorded visits indicate greater observed use during the trial. This does not by itself demonstrate learning gains or lasting demand.","Do not use complexity to disguise a weak inference. Hedge claims for evidentiary reasons, not by adding may to every sentence mechanically.","Use precise definitions, traceable reference, clear agency and evidence-calibrated claims.",1,B.avV,B.aRT,B.aMm,B.avL)
B.aqV=s(["Evaluate causal and normative claims separately.","Identify operational gaps and counterexamples.","Construct a fair critique of a limited source."],t.s)
B.aAJ=s(["Observation: more visits","Causal claim: learning gains","Normative claim: all schools should adopt","Gaps: outcomes, alternatives, feasibility"],t.s)
B.aPb=s(["The directly recorded pattern.","Needs evidence of outcome and causal attribution.","Needs context and value-based justification.","Different claim types require different support."],t.s)
B.aCJ=s(["Which is a normative recommendation?|Every school should adopt evening opening.|Recorded visits increased.|The trial lasted four weeks.|Counts were logged at the door.|Should recommends an action rather than merely reporting a finding.","Which is a causal claim?|Evening opening caused learning gains.|Visits were recorded.|The report has a title.|A librarian kept a log.|Caused asserts an explanatory link between intervention and outcome.","What does the observation directly establish?|A recorded visit pattern in the trial.|Universal learning gains.|All-school feasibility.|Every learner preference.|The measured variable supports only the visit finding directly.","Which counterexample challenges visits imply learning?|Repeated visits for unrelated activities without improved understanding.|A record of more visits alone.|The word library has letters.|The report names a date.|The scenario allows higher visits while the claimed learning gain is absent.","What else does universal adoption require?|Evidence of feasibility and benefits across relevant contexts.|Only a more forceful slogan.|Only the local count.|No value assumptions.|A broad recommendation requires more than a local observed association.","What is charitable reconstruction?|An accurate strong presentation before critique.|Inventing stronger premises the author rejects.|Agreeing with every conclusion.|Removing all limitations.|Charity avoids distortion while keeping the actual argument intact.","Which critique is fair?|The visit data are useful, but learning and universal feasibility remain unsupported.|The data are false because the conclusion is too broad.|All librarians are dishonest.|No one ever learns in libraries.|The critique separates evidence value from unsupported extensions.","Does causal uncertainty refute the visit count automatically?|No|Yes, every observation disappears.|Only if it is positive.|Only if repeated.|A weak causal inference does not itself disprove the observed pattern.","Which design issue bears on causal attribution?|Competing explanations such as changed timetables.|The writer font choice alone.|The report cover color.|A decorative border.|Other changes can influence the outcome independently of the intervention.","Which is a proportionate conclusion?|The local trial warrants further outcome and cost investigation.|Universal adoption is proven.|No future evidence can matter.|Every pupil must support it.|The narrower recommendation matches the unresolved evidence needs.","Why separate normative from causal support?|Benefits do not alone settle costs, priorities and contexts.|Recommendations contain no reasoning.|Every should claim is false.|Causal data automatically settle all values.|Policy decisions require evaluative premises in addition to empirical claims."],t.s)
B.a7e=new A.ce("college","Critical Reading","A rigorous reader separates kinds of claims. Invented argument: Evening opening increased recorded visits. Therefore it caused learning gains, and every school should adopt it. The observed variable is visits. The causal learning claim needs an appropriate outcome and a design addressing competing explanations. The normative recommendation also assumes benefits justify costs across different contexts. Even if one local benefit were established, universal adoption would need additional evidence and value reasoning. Charitable reconstruction states the strongest accurate version of the argument before critique; it does not add premises the author never accepts. Evaluate what is supported, what is assumed and what would resolve the uncertainty.","Reconstruct the argument. 1. Identify the visit observation. 2. Separate causal learning claim. 3. Separate universal recommendation. 4. Name missing learning and feasibility support. 5. Offer a narrower conclusion: investigate whether a local trial is useful.","Give a counterexample to If visits rose, learning must have risen that is consistent with the measured variable.","A reader might visit repeatedly for unrelated activities without improved assessed understanding. This possibility shows that visits alone do not logically establish learning gains.","Do not attack a weak caricature. Do not treat uncertainty about causation as proof that the observed increase is false.","Disentangle observations, causes and recommendations, then test each required inferential link.",2,B.aqV,B.aAJ,B.aPb,B.aCJ)
B.awB=s(["Integrate quotation and paraphrase accurately.","Preserve source scope and make evidence retrievable.","Distinguish a teaching citation from authentic scholarship."],t.s)
B.aR3=s(["Source: increased attendance","Source limit: learning not assessed","Paraphrase: both claims retained","Citation: actual source used"],t.s)
B.atF=s(["The measured finding.","An essential qualification.","Faithful integration preserves scope.","Traceability must match genuine reading and source details."],t.s)
B.aLJ=s(["What does the invented Orin source report?|Higher attendance in four sessions, with no learning assessment.|Proven learning gains for all pupils.|No attendance data.|Every school adoption.|The source pairs a visit finding with an explicit outcome limitation.","Which paraphrase is faithful?|Attendance rose in four evening sessions, but learning was not measured.|Evening access proved learning gains.|Every school improved.|Attendance and learning were both tested successfully.|The faithful version preserves both the finding and qualification.","Does paraphrasing remove the need for citation?|No|Yes, if words change.|Only if names are absent.|Only if a number is changed.|Borrowed ideas still require acknowledgment.","What must a quotation preserve?|The selected source wording accurately.|Only the general topic.|Any stronger claim the writer prefers.|Unmarked invented details.|Quotation represents actual wording rather than a rewritten claim.","What should a bibliography match?|The sources actually used and cited.|Unverified impressive titles.|Documents never read presented as read.|Only the longest author names.|Bibliographic traceability depends on genuine source use.","How should a secondhand quotation be handled?|Follow assigned secondary-citation requirements and be transparent.|Pretend the original was read directly.|Remove every source name.|Invent the original page.|The reader should know which work was actually consulted.","Can Orin 2024 be used as authentic research evidence?|No, it is invented lesson material.|Yes, any author-year pair is real.|Only if the title is italicized.|Only if the number is plausible.|The source is explicitly fictional and not retrievable scholarship.","What is wrong with Orin proved learning improvement?|It contradicts the source stated limitation.|It has too few words.|It uses a name.|It mentions an outcome.|Learning outcomes were not assessed, so the paraphrase misrepresents the source.","What governs citation details in a real course?|The assigned style and verified source information.|A fictional example universally.|A guessed publication date.|A random page number.|Formatting requirements depend on the assignment and real bibliographic facts.","Why include a locator when required?|To help readers find the specific evidence.|To guarantee causal truth.|To replace the quotation.|To create a source that does not exist.|Locators improve traceability of a passage within its source.","Can an accurate-looking citation fix a distorted claim?|No|Yes, automatically.|Only with a year.|Only with a long reference list.|Attribution and faithful representation are separate responsibilities."],t.s)
B.a6M=new A.ce("college","Research & Citation","Invented source for practice: Orin (2024), Access Trial Notes, page 7, states: Attendance increased during four evening sessions; learning outcomes were not assessed. A quotation must reproduce the selected wording accurately and mark it as borrowed. A paraphrase changes wording and structure while preserving both the increased attendance and the learning limit. Citation gives attribution and enough information to locate evidence according to the assigned style. A bibliographic entry must match the source actually used. If you only encountered an original claim quoted in another work, follow the course requirements for secondary citation and do not pretend to have read the original. Orin and this document are invented, not retrievable research.","Paraphrase Orin. 1. Identify the two claims. 2. Write that four extra sessions had higher attendance. 3. Retain that learning was not measured. 4. Attribute the idea using the practice author and year. 5. In a real assignment, replace it with an authentic verified source.","Evaluate According to Orin, evening access improved learning. Does that accurately paraphrase the invented source?","No. The source explicitly says learning outcomes were not assessed. The paraphrase changes an attendance finding into an unsupported learning claim.","Do not omit a qualification because it weakens your thesis. A citation does not make an inaccurate paraphrase accurate.","Attribute exact words and borrowed ideas while preserving meaning, limitations and source traceability.",3,B.awB,B.aR3,B.atF,B.aLJ)
B.aDA=s(["Construct a qualified thesis with explicit warrants.","Integrate evidence and counterarguments proportionately.","Distinguish a defensible proposal from universal overreach."],t.s)
B.aD1=s(["Thesis: one-term local trial","Evidence: documented requests","Warrant: possible unmet demand","Counterargument: workload \u2192 measure"],t.s)
B.aBw=s(["Limits context and commitment.","Requires real traceable records.","Names the assumption connecting requests with use.","Responds through evidence rather than dismissal."],t.s)
B.avq=s(["Which thesis has a defensible scope?|Test evening access at one school for one term.|Every school should open forever without cost review.|All pupils certainly attend.|No evidence is needed.|The bounded trial limits the claim and practical commitment.","Which warrant connects requests with the proposal?|Requests may indicate unmet local demand.|Requests are the same as proven attendance.|Every request proves universal learning.|The title is persuasive.|The warrant expresses a plausible but testable evidence-to-claim link.","Which assumption needs checking?|Requesting pupils can actually attend.|Every pupil is identical.|The school has no walls.|All costs are always zero.|Expressed interest may not translate into feasible attendance.","Which counterargument is relevant?|Staff workload may increase.|The author has an unusual name.|The essay has commas.|The library sign is orange.|Workload affects implementation feasibility.","Which response is substantive?|Measure staffing hours and costs during the trial.|Deny all costs without evidence.|Call staff concerns foolish.|Repeat the thesis only.|The response addresses the actual practical concern with an evidence plan.","What does a concession do?|Acknowledge a valid opposing point before responding.|Automatically abandon the whole thesis.|Misrepresent another position.|Replace sources with insults.|Concession recognizes legitimate concerns while refining the argument.","Which conclusion follows proportionately?|Review measured use and cost before permanent expansion.|The trial certainly proves every school benefit.|No measurements can matter.|Everyone must agree.|The recommendation respects uncertainty and the proposed limited investigation.","Why are unexplained quotations insufficient?|The writer must connect evidence to the thesis.|Quotations never contain information.|Sources replace all reasoning.|Only longer quotes count.|An essay argument needs analysis and warrants, not an evidence pile.","What should replace invented teaching claims in real writing?|Authentic documented evidence.|More dramatic fictional claims.|Unnamed rumor.|A decorative bibliography.|Illustrative data cannot be presented as empirical research.","Which rebuttal is a straw-man response?|Critics want pupils never to read, when they raised staffing cost.|Staffing time should be measured.|Costs may affect feasibility.|The proposed term limits commitment.|The response distorts a practical concern into a different extreme position.","Which feature makes a claim contestable?|Reasonable readers can evaluate and disagree with its reasons.|It is merely a spelling fact.|It has no proposition.|It contains only a title.|Argumentative theses take positions supported by assessable reasoning."],t.s)
B.a6Y=new A.ce("college","Argumentative Writing","An argumentative essay develops a contestable thesis through reasons, evidence and warrants. Invented proposal: A school should test evening library access for one term because documented requests suggest unmet local demand. The warrant connects requests with potential use, but assumes requesting pupils can attend and staffing is feasible. Address those assumptions with a trial design and records rather than claiming every school benefits. A counterargument about staff workload deserves a real response. Conceding that workload must be measured strengthens a limited proposal; it does not require abandoning the thesis. In real writing, replace all illustrative claims with authentic documented evidence.","Develop the essay. 1. Bound the proposal by school and term. 2. Present verified request evidence. 3. Explain why requests indicate possible demand. 4. Address attendance and staffing assumptions. 5. Recommend measurement before permanence.","Respond to The proposal increases staff workload without denying the concern or claiming the cost is already known.","Staff workload is a valid feasibility concern. The trial should record required hours and costs, and any permanent decision should weigh those records against actual use.","Do not accumulate quotations without warrants. A rebuttal should answer a strong accurate counterargument rather than a caricature.","Build a bounded thesis through evidence, explicit inferential links, fair alternatives and a proportionate conclusion.",4,B.aDA,B.aD1,B.aBw,B.avq)
B.asq=s(["Write a clear actionable message for a defined recipient.","Separate facts, requests and responsibilities.","Use respectful precise language and protect private information."],t.s)
B.av1=s(["Subject: review timetable","Fact: booking moved to Tuesday","Request: corrections by Friday noon","Owner: Mina consolidates"],t.s)
B.ard=s(["Names the message task.","Separates the actual change from the request.","Gives a concrete action and deadline.","Identifies responsibility for the next step."],t.s)
B.aDd=s(["Which subject is most actionable?|Review revised timetable by Friday.|Hello.|An important thing.|Please look sometime.|The subject identifies the document, task and deadline.","What changed in the invented email?|The booking moved from Monday to Tuesday.|Friday became Monday.|Mina left the team.|The room was destroyed.|The body explicitly states the booking change.","What action is requested?|Check the timetable and send corrections.|Ignore every attachment.|Cancel all meetings.|Share private learner records.|The message asks recipients to review and report corrections.","Who consolidates responses?|Mina|every unnamed pupil|the room itself|an unstated manager|The email assigns consolidation to Mina.","Why replace soon with a precise deadline?|Recipients need an unambiguous action time.|It guarantees compliance.|It removes all responsibility.|It makes the message a legal contract automatically.|A defined deadline coordinates work more clearly than soon.","When is a time zone especially relevant?|When recipients work across time zones.|Only when no deadline exists.|Never.|Only in fictional stories.|Cross-zone teams may interpret noon differently without a specified zone.","Why identify document version 2?|To ensure recipients review the intended revision.|To make every older file illegal.|To prove all facts.|To hide the document name.|Version information prevents review of an outdated or different document.","Which tone is respectful and clear?|Please send corrections to Mina by Friday noon.|Do the thing soon or else.|You are all careless.|Everyone must guess the deadline.|The message is direct about the task without insult or vagueness.","What private material belongs in this routine timetable email?|Only necessary authorized information, not unrelated private identifiers.|All learner weaknesses.|Every personal contact record.|Unneeded health details.|Recipients should receive only information relevant and appropriate to the task.","Which message separates fact from request?|The booking moved to Tuesday; please review the updated timetable.|The thing changed, so do something.|Everyone caused the problem.|The timetable is certainly perfect.|The two clauses distinguish the reported event from the recipient action.","Does this lesson send a real email?|No, it uses an invented model.|Yes, to every team.|Only if a learner answers correctly.|Only on Friday.|The content is an offline educational example with no sending action."],t.s)
B.a73=new A.ce("college","Professional Communication","Professional messages help a recipient understand what happened and what action is needed. Original invented email: Subject: Review revised timetable by Friday. Team, the room booking moved from Monday to Tuesday. Please check the attached timetable and send corrections by Friday noon. Mina will consolidate responses. Thank you. The subject names the action and deadline; the body gives the changed fact, recipient task and responsible person. Dates, time zones and document versions should be explicit when ambiguity matters. Respectful tone does not require vague requests. Include only information recipients need, and avoid unnecessary personal or private identifiers. No real email or attachment is sent by this lesson.","Revise Please handle the thing soon. 1. Name the document. 2. State the action review. 3. State a precise deadline and relevant time zone if needed. 4. Name where corrections go. 5. Keep the tone direct and respectful.","Draft a message asking a team to review version 2 of a timetable by Friday noon and identify who consolidates feedback.","Please review timetable version 2 and send corrections to Mina by Friday noon. Mina will consolidate feedback. In a real cross-zone team, specify the calendar date and time zone.","Soon and the thing are ambiguous. Do not attach unrelated private participant information or blame a person when reporting an unverified error.","State the fact, requested action, document version, deadline and responsibility with respectful clarity.",5,B.asq,B.av1,B.ard,B.aDd)
