B.aDQ=s(["Simplify rational expressions with retained restrictions.","Use logarithms as inverse exponents.","Solve an exponential or rational equation."],t.s)
B.b5E={"(x\xb2 \u2212 9)/(x \u2212 3)":0,"log\u20828 = 3":1,"1/(x \u2212 1) = 2":2}
B.aTv=new A.au(B.b5E,["x + 3 with x \u2260 3 retained.","Two to power three is eight.","x = 3/2, with x \u2260 1."],t.w)
B.avD=s(["(x\xb2 \u2212 9)/(x \u2212 3) simplifies to what where defined?|x + 3|x \u2212 3;x\xb2 + 3;1|Factor the difference of squares before cancellation.","Which input is excluded from (x\xb2 \u2212 9)/(x \u2212 3)?|3|\u22123;0;1|The original denominator vanishes at three.","log\u20828 equals what?|3|4;2;8|Two cubed equals eight.","2\u02e3 = 16 gives x what?|4|8;2;16|Sixteen is two to the fourth power.","Real domain of log\u2082(x \u2212 1)?|x > 1|x \u2265 1;All real x;x < 1|The logarithm argument must be positive.","1/(x \u2212 1) = 2 gives x what?|3/2|1;2;1/2|Multiply by the nonzero denominator and solve.","For real logs, can the base be one?|No|Yes;Only for positive arguments;Only for integers|One to every power cannot invert distinct positive inputs.","For positive x, log\u2082(4x) equals what?|2 + log\u2082x|4 + log\u2082x;4log\u2082x;log\u20824 + x|The product law adds logarithms, with log\u20824 equal two.","Solve 3\u02e3 = 27.|3|9;2;27|Twenty-seven is three cubed.","Excluded inputs of 1/(x\xb2 \u2212 4)?|2 and \u22122|2 only;\u22122 only;0 only|Both values make the factored denominator zero.","log\u2081\u2080100 equals what?|2|10;100;0|Ten squared equals one hundred."],t.s)
B.a8y=new A.cu("Advanced Algebra",B.aDQ,"Algebraic transformations must preserve the permitted input set.","The expression (x\xb2 \u2212 9)/(x \u2212 3) factors to (x \u2212 3)(x + 3)/(x \u2212 3), so it equals x + 3 only for x \u2260 3. Cancellation does not restore the excluded input. A logarithm answers an exponent question: log\u20828 = 3 because 2\xb3 = 8. For real logarithms, the base is positive and not one, and the argument is positive. Logarithm product laws require positive arguments.",B.aTv,"Solve 2\u02e3 = 16. Step 1: write sixteen as 2\u2074. Step 2: compare powers of the same valid base. Step 3: x = 4. Step 4: substitute to verify 2\u2074 = 16.","Find the real domain of log\u2082(x \u2212 1), and solve 1/(x \u2212 1) = 2.","Require x \u2212 1 > 0, so the logarithm domain is x > 1. The rational equation gives one = 2(x \u2212 1), hence x = 3/2.","Do not cancel across addition without factoring. Keep original denominator restrictions. Logarithms do not accept zero or negative real arguments.","Use factoring and inverse operations while preserving every domain restriction.",B.avD)
B.ar3=s(["Distinguish terms from sums.","Use arithmetic and geometric patterns.","Apply finite or infinite geometric sums with restrictions."],t.s)
B.b6r={"2, 6, 18, 54":0,"2, 5, 8, 11, 14":1,"1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + ...":2}
B.aV4=new A.au(B.b6r,["Ratio three; sum eighty.","Difference three; sum forty.","Ratio one half; infinite sum two."],t.w)
B.aso=s(["Common ratio of 2, 6, 18?|3|4;2;6|Each term is multiplied by three.","Fourth term of 2, 6, 18, ...?|54|80;36;24|Multiply eighteen by three.","Sum of 2, 6, 18, 54?|80|54;72;26|Add all four terms.","Common difference of 2, 5, 8?|3|2;5;4|Each consecutive increase is three.","Fifth term of 2, 5, 8, ...?|14|15;11;40|Add four differences of three to two.","Sum of 2, 5, 8, 11, 14?|40|14;28;30|Five terms have average eight.","Infinite sum 1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + ...?|2|1;3;No convergence|The ratio one half meets the convergence condition.","When may the infinite geometric-sum formula be used?|Ratio absolute value less than one|Every ratio;Ratio greater than one;Only ratio exactly one|Terms must shrink sufficiently toward zero.","Fourth term of 3, 6, 12, ...?|24|45;18;36|The common ratio is two.","Sum of 3, 6, 12, 24?|45|24;48;36|Add the four terms.","Does 3 + 6 + 12 + ... converge?|No|Yes, to six;Yes, to three;Yes, to forty-five|Its nonzero terms grow rather than approach zero."],t.s)
B.a8k=new A.cu("Sequences & Series",B.ar3,"A sequence lists terms; a series adds them.","Arithmetic terms change by a fixed difference, while geometric terms multiply by a fixed ratio. For 2, 6, 18, the ratio is three and a\u2099 = 2 \xd7 3\u207f\u207b\xb9. Its first four terms sum to eighty. A finite geometric sum with r \u2260 1 is a\u2081(1 \u2212 r\u207f)/(1 \u2212 r). An infinite geometric sum is a\u2081/(1 \u2212 r) only when the absolute value of r is less than one; otherwise nonzero terms do not yield that convergence.",B.aV4,"Sum first three terms starting at five with ratio two. Step 1: list five, ten, twenty. Step 2: add to thirty-five. Step 3: formula gives 5(1 \u2212 2\xb3)/(1 \u2212 2). Step 4: both methods give thirty-five.","Find the fourth term and first-four-term sum for 3, 6, 12, ... . Does the infinite series converge?","The fourth term is twenty-four; the sum is forty-five. Ratio two has absolute value above one, so the infinite series does not converge.","Do not confuse the final term with total sum. A finite geometric sum is valid for growing ratios, but the infinite-sum formula needs a ratio with absolute value below one.","Identify the pattern and requested quantity, then use a sum formula with its conditions.",B.aso)
B.av0=s(["Convert between degrees and radians.","Interpret unit-circle sine and cosine.","Find amplitude and period of a sinusoid."],t.s)
B.b4j={"\u03b8 = \u03c0/2":0,"y = 2 sin(3x)":1,"r = 2, \u03b8 = \u03c0/2":2}
B.aUZ=new A.au(B.b4j,["Unit-circle point (0, 1).","Amplitude two, period 2\u03c0/3.","Arc length \u03c0."],t.w)
B.aN7=s(["180 degrees equals what in radians?|\u03c0|2\u03c0;\u03c0/2;180\u03c0|A half turn is pi radians.","60 degrees equals what in radians?|\u03c0/3|\u03c0/6;3\u03c0;2\u03c0/3|Scale pi radians by sixty over 180.","Sin(\u03c0/2) equals what?|1|0;\u22121;1/2|The unit-circle y-coordinate is one.","Cos \u03c0 equals what?|\u22121|1;0;1/2|The unit-circle x-coordinate at a half turn is negative one.","Amplitude of 2 sin(3x)?|2|3;6;2\u03c0/3|Amplitude is the absolute vertical coefficient.","Period of 2 sin(3x)?|2\u03c0/3|2\u03c0;3\u03c0;\u03c0/3|Divide the sine base period by three.","Radius two and angle \u03c0/2 gives arc length what?|\u03c0|2\u03c0;\u03c0/2;1|Multiply radius by angle in radians.","Amplitude of \u22123 sin(2x)?|3|\u22123;2;\u03c0|Amplitude is a nonnegative size.","Period of 4 cos(2x)?|\u03c0|2\u03c0;4\u03c0;\u03c0/2|Cosine period two pi divides by two.","Tan(\u03c0/2) is what?|Undefined|0;1;\u22121|Cosine is zero, so sine divided by cosine is undefined.","Sin(\u03b8 + 2\u03c0) relates to sin \u03b8 how?|They are equal|They always have opposite signs;The first doubles;The first is zero|Sine repeats after a full turn."],t.s)
B.a8h=new A.cu("Trigonometric Functions",B.av0,"The unit circle extends trigonometry beyond acute right-triangle angles.","A full turn is 2\u03c0 radians, so 180 degrees equals \u03c0 radians. At angle \u03b8 on the unit circle, the point is (cos \u03b8, sin \u03b8). Thus cos \u03c0 = \u22121 and sin(\u03c0/2) = 1. Sine and cosine repeat every 2\u03c0. In y = A sin(Bx), amplitude is the absolute value of A and period is 2\u03c0 divided by the absolute value of nonzero B. Arc length s = r\u03b8 uses \u03b8 in radians.",B.aUZ,"Analyze y = \u22123 sin(2x). Step 1: amplitude is three. Step 2: negative sign reflects outputs vertically. Step 3: period is 2\u03c0/2 = \u03c0. Step 4: a half-turn input interval completes one output cycle.","Convert sixty degrees to radians and find amplitude and period of 4 cos(2x).","Sixty degrees is \u03c0/3. The cosine amplitude is four and period is \u03c0.","Do not use a degree value directly in s = r\u03b8. A negative amplitude coefficient reflects the graph but amplitude itself is nonnegative.","Use unit-circle coordinates and radians, then track vertical size and horizontal repetition separately.",B.aN7)
B.aJn=s(["Read conic parameters from equations.","Complete squares for a circle.","Distinguish semiaxes from full axes."],t.s)
B.b5w={"(x \u2212 1)\xb2 + (y + 2)\xb2 = 9":0,"x\xb2/9 + y\xb2/4 = 1":1,"y\xb2 = 8x":2}
B.aUK=new A.au(B.b5w,["Center (1, \u22122), radius three.","Horizontal semiaxis three; vertical two.","p = 2, focus (2, 0), directrix x = \u22122."],t.w)
B.asI=s(["Center of (x \u2212 1)\xb2 + (y + 2)\xb2 = 9?|(1, \u22122)|(\u22121, 2);(1, 2);(\u22121, \u22122)|The center makes both grouped coordinates zero.","Radius of (x \u2212 1)\xb2 + (y + 2)\xb2 = 9?|3|9;6;\u221a3|Radius is the positive square root of nine.","Horizontal semiaxis of x\xb2/9 + y\xb2/4 = 1?|3|9;6;2|The denominator is the semiaxis square.","Full vertical axis length of x\xb2/9 + y\xb2/4 = 1?|4|2;8;9|The vertical semiaxis two is doubled.","For y\xb2 = 8x, p equals what?|2|8;4;\u22122|Compare eight with four p.","Focus of y\xb2 = 8x?|(2, 0)|(0, 2);(\u22122, 0);(0, \u22122)|The standard right-opening focus is p on the x-axis.","Directrix of y\xb2 = 8x?|x = \u22122|x = 2;y = 2;y = \u22122|The directrix lies p units opposite the focus.","Circle x\xb2 + y\xb2 \u2212 4x + 6y \u2212 12 = 0 has radius what?|5|12;25;3|Completing squares gives right side twenty-five.","Semiaxes of x\xb2/16 + y\xb2/9 = 1?|4 and 3|16 and 9;8 and 6;2 and 3|Take positive square roots of denominators.","Focus of y\xb2 = 12x?|(3, 0)|(0, 3);(12, 0);(\u22123, 0)|Four p equals twelve, so p is three.","X\xb2/9 \u2212 y\xb2/4 = 1 describes which conic?|Hyperbola|Ellipse;Circle;A line|The difference of squared coordinate terms identifies this hyperbola."],t.s)
B.a8H=new A.cu("Analytic Geometry",B.aJn,"Coordinate equations describe circles, ellipses, parabolas, and hyperbolas.","A circle has (x \u2212 h)\xb2 + (y \u2212 k)\xb2 = r\xb2. The signs inside locate the center; the right side is radius squared. An ellipse x\xb2/9 + y\xb2/4 = 1 has semiaxes three and two. A hyperbola with a minus between those terms opens along its positive squared coordinate. In y\xb2 = 4px, the vertex is zero, focus (p, 0), and directrix x = \u2212p. These forms assume the displayed axis alignment.",B.aUK,"Complete x\xb2 + y\xb2 \u2212 4x + 6y \u2212 12 = 0. Step 1: move twelve to the right. Step 2: add four and nine to complete both squares. Step 3: write (x \u2212 2)\xb2 + (y + 3)\xb2 = 25. Step 4: center is (2, \u22123), radius five.","Find semiaxes of x\xb2/16 + y\xb2/9 = 1 and the focus of y\xb2 = 12x.","Semiaxes are four and three, so full axes are eight and six. In the parabola, 4p = 12 gives focus (3, 0).","Do not read squared radius as radius or denominators as semiaxes. Completing squares changes both equation sides. Standard forms depend on coordinate orientation.","Match the conic form and read parameters only after accounting for squares and signs.",B.asI)
B.ao0=s(["Interpret a limit near a point.","Compute a difference quotient.","Connect a derivative with instantaneous slope."],t.s)
B.b54={"[(a + h)\xb2 \u2212 a\xb2]/h":0,"h \u2192 0":1,"(x\xb2 \u2212 1)/(x \u2212 1), x \u2192 1":2}
B.aTH=new A.au(B.b54,["2a + h for nonzero h.","Derivative 2a.","Nearby rule x + 1 gives limit two."],t.w)
B.aCH=s(["Derivative of x\xb2 at x = 3?|6|3;9;12|The limiting slope is twice the input.","Difference quotient of x\xb2 at a simplifies to what for nonzero h?|2a + h|2a;a\xb2 + h;h\xb2|Expand the numerator before cancelling h.","Limit of (x\xb2 \u2212 1)/(x \u2212 1) as x approaches one?|2|0;1;Undefined limit|For nearby nonzero denominator the rule equals x plus one.","Value of (x\xb2 \u2212 1)/(x \u2212 1) at x = 1?|Undefined|2;1;0|The original denominator is zero.","Average rate of x\xb2 from two to four?|6|4;8;12|Output change twelve divided by input change two.","Derivative of x\xb2 at two?|4|2;6;8|Twice two equals four.","Why not set h zero before quotient simplification?|It creates division by zero|It makes the slope one;It changes a into h;It removes limits forever|Simplify for nonzero h before taking its limit.","Does a limit always require the function to be defined at the point?|No|Yes;Only for polynomials;Only at zero|Nearby behavior can approach a value despite a hole.","Derivative of x\xb2 at five?|10|5;25;20|The general derivative is two x.","Average rate of x\xb2 from one to three?|4|2;3;8|Eight units of output change over two input units.","At x = 0, derivative of x\xb2 is what?|0|1;2;Undefined|The limiting slope is twice zero."],t.s)
B.a82=new A.cu("Intro Calculus",B.ao0,"Calculus studies limiting behavior and rates of change.","For f(x) = x\xb2, the average rate from a to a + h is [(a + h)\xb2 \u2212 a\xb2]/h for h \u2260 0. Expanding and cancelling gives 2a + h. As h tends to zero, the derivative is 2a. A limit concerns nearby values, not necessarily the value at the point. The expression (x\xb2 \u2212 1)/(x \u2212 1) is undefined at one, but simplifies to x + 1 nearby and has limit two.",B.aTH,"Find derivative slope of x\xb2 at three. Step 1: form [(3 + h)\xb2 \u2212 9]/h. Step 2: expand to (6h + h\xb2)/h. Step 3: cancel nonzero h, giving 6 + h. Step 4: take h toward zero to get six.","Find average rate of x\xb2 from two to four, then its derivative at two.","Average rate is (16 \u2212 4)/(4 \u2212 2) = 6. The derivative at two is four; the interval rate and instantaneous rate need not match.","Do not set h = 0 before simplifying a quotient. A function can have a limit where its own value is undefined. Average and instantaneous rates are different concepts.","Use nearby values and valid simplification to obtain limits and instantaneous rates.",B.aCH)
B.Fz=s([B.a8y,B.a8k,B.a8h,B.a8H,B.a82],t.LQ)
