B.df=s(["Mathematics","Science","English","Filipino","Araling Panlipunan"],t.s)
B.ayR=s(["Solve using factors or the quadratic formula.","Interpret the discriminant.","Retain all real solutions."],t.s)
B.b4Q={"x\xb2 \u2212 5x + 6 = 0":0,"x\xb2 \u2212 6x + 9 = 0":1,"x\xb2 + 4x + 5 = 0":2}
B.aUy=new A.au(B.b4Q,["Roots two and three; D = 1.","Repeated root three; D = 0.","D = \u22124, so no real roots."],t.w)
B.aFj=s(["Roots of x\xb2 \u2212 5x + 6 = 0?|2 and 3|\u22122 and \u22123;1 and 6;0 and 5|The factors x minus two and x minus three vanish.","Discriminant of x\xb2 \u2212 5x + 6?|1|25;24;49|Twenty-five minus twenty-four equals one.","Positive discriminant gives how many distinct real roots?|2|1;0;Infinitely many|The square-root term is nonzero and real.","Zero discriminant gives what?|One repeated real root|Two distinct roots;No real root;Every number|The plus and minus results coincide.","Discriminant of x\xb2 + 4x + 5?|\u22124|4;36;16|Sixteen minus twenty equals negative four.","Roots of x\xb2 \u2212 x \u2212 6 = 0?|3 and \u22122|\u22123 and 2;1 and 6;\u22121 and \u22126|The formula gives one plus or minus five, divided by two.","Why set the equation equal to zero before factoring to solve?|The zero-product property requires zero|Every expression is zero;It removes all roots;Only positive factors work|A nonzero product need not have a zero factor.","Formula denominator for coefficient a is what?|2a|a;2;4ac|Both numerator branches divide by twice the leading coefficient.","Roots of 2x\xb2 \u2212 8 = 0?|2 and \u22122|4 and \u22124;2 only;\u22122 only|Both positive and negative two square to four.","Root of x\xb2 + 2x + 1 = 0?|\u22121 repeated|1 repeated;\u22122;No real root|The expression is the square of x plus one.","Roots of x\xb2 \u2212 9 = 0?|3 and \u22123|9 and \u22129;3 only;\u22123 only|Both signs solve x squared equals nine."],t.s)
B.a8D=new A.cu("Quadratic Equations",B.ayR,"A quadratic equation may have two, one, or no real roots.","For ax\xb2 + bx + c = 0 with a \u2260 0, the formula is x = (\u2212b \xb1 \u221a(b\xb2 \u2212 4ac))/(2a). The discriminant D = b\xb2 \u2212 4ac determines real-root count: positive gives two, zero one repeated root, negative no real roots. Factoring x\xb2 \u2212 5x + 6 gives (x \u2212 2)(x \u2212 3), hence roots two and three. The zero-product rule requires a product equal to zero.",B.aUy,"Solve x\xb2 \u2212 x \u2212 6 = 0 by formula. Step 1: a = 1, b = \u22121, c = \u22126. Step 2: D = 1 + 24 = 25. Step 3: x = (1 \xb1 5)/2. Step 4: obtain three and negative two; both satisfy the equation.","Solve 2x\xb2 \u2212 8 = 0 and classify the real roots of x\xb2 + 2x + 1 = 0.","The first reduces to x\xb2 = 4, so x = 2 or \u22122. The second is (x + 1)\xb2 = 0 with one repeated root \u22121.","The \xb1 sign gives two candidates unless they coincide. Divide the entire numerator by 2a. Negative discriminant means no real roots, not no complex roots.","Choose a valid solving method and use the discriminant to classify real solutions.",B.aFj)
B.aFK=s(["Identify real-valued domain restrictions.","Read intercepts and transformations.","Distinguish a vertical asymptote from a permitted input."],t.s)
B.b5M={"1/(x \u2212 2)":0,"\u221a(x + 3)":1,"(x \u2212 2)\xb2 + 3":2}
B.aTW=new A.au(B.b5M,["Domain excludes two; vertical asymptote x = 2.","Real domain x \u2265 \u22123.","Vertex (2, 3)."],t.w)
B.aCN=s(["Which input is excluded from 1/(x \u2212 2)?|2|\u22122;0;1|At two the denominator is zero.","Real domain of \u221a(x + 3)?|x \u2265 \u22123|x \u2265 3;All real x;x < \u22123|The quantity under a real square root must be nonnegative.","Vertex of (x \u2212 2)\xb2 + 3?|(2, 3)|(\u22122, 3);(2, \u22123);(3, 2)|The square is zero at x two.","Y-intercept of y = \u22122x + 6?|6|\u22122;3;\u22123|At x zero the output is six.","X-intercept of y = \u22122x + 6?|3|6;\u22123;2|Set output zero and solve.","Vertical asymptote of 1/(x \u2212 2)?|x = 2|y = 2;x = \u22122;y = \u22122|The denominator vanishes at x two.","Vertex of (x + 1)\xb2 \u2212 5?|(\u22121, \u22125)|(1, \u22125);(\u22121, 5);(5, 1)|The group vanishes at x negative one.","Why is x = 2 not a value of 1/(x \u2212 2)?|Division by zero is undefined|The output is automatically zero;The output is automatically two;All positive inputs are forbidden|A vertical asymptote does not assign a finite value.","Excluded input of 1/(x + 4)?|\u22124|4;0;\u22121|Negative four makes the denominator zero.","X-intercept of y = 3x \u2212 9?|3|\u22129;9;\u22123|Set y zero and divide nine by three.","Real domain of \u221a(x \u2212 5)?|x \u2265 5|x \u2265 \u22125;All real x;x < 5|The radicand must be nonnegative."],t.s)
B.a7T=new A.cu("Functions & Graphs",B.aFK,"Function rules and graph features must agree on allowed inputs.","For f(x) = 1/(x \u2212 2), x = 2 is excluded because division by zero is undefined; x = 2 is a vertical asymptote. For \u221a(x + 3) over real numbers, require x + 3 \u2265 0, giving x \u2265 \u22123. The graph y = (x \u2212 2)\xb2 + 3 shifts y = x\xb2 two units right and three up. For y = \u22122x + 6, the y-intercept is six and the x-intercept is three. Intercepts are found by setting the other coordinate to zero.",B.aTW,"Find intercepts of y = 3x \u2212 9. Step 1: set x = 0 to get y = \u22129. Step 2: y-intercept is (0, \u22129). Step 3: set y = 0 and solve 3x = 9. Step 4: x-intercept is (3, 0).","State the domain of 1/(x + 4) and the vertex of (x + 1)\xb2 \u2212 5.","The rational rule excludes x = \u22124. The quadratic vertex is (\u22121, \u22125).","A graph does not fill excluded inputs automatically. Horizontal shifts use the value that makes the grouped expression zero, so x + 1 shifts left.","Check algebraic restrictions and connect shifts, intercepts, and asymptotes to the rule.",B.aCN)
B.aK9=s(["Identify sides relative to an acute angle.","Use sine, cosine, and tangent ratios.","Apply a ratio to find a side."],t.s)
B.b68={"Opposite 3, adjacent 4, hypotenuse 5":0,"sin 30\xb0 = 1/2":1,"tan 45\xb0 = 1":2}
B.aV5=new A.au(B.b68,["sin \u03b8 = 3/5; cos \u03b8 = 4/5; tan \u03b8 = 3/4","Opposite is half the hypotenuse.","The two legs are equal."],t.w)
B.aM8=s(["For opposite 3 and hypotenuse 5, sine is what?|3/5|5/3;4/5;3/4|Sine divides opposite by hypotenuse.","For adjacent 4 and hypotenuse 5, cosine is what?|4/5|5/4;3/5;4/3|Cosine divides adjacent leg by hypotenuse.","For opposite 3 and adjacent 4, tangent is what?|3/4|4/3;3/5;4/5|Tangent compares the two legs.","Which side is always hypotenuse?|Opposite the right angle|Opposite any selected angle;The horizontal side;The shortest leg|The hypotenuse is the right-triangle longest side.","Sin 30\xb0 equals what?|1/2|1;0;\u221a3/2|The opposite leg is half the hypotenuse.","Tan 45\xb0 equals what?|1|0;1/2;\u221a3|The two legs are equal.","Angle 30\xb0, hypotenuse ten. Opposite length?|5|10;20;\u221a3|Multiply the sine ratio one half by ten.","Why set calculator mode to degrees for a 30\xb0 input?|Angle units must match|Degrees and radians are identical;It changes triangle size;It removes the hypotenuse|A different unit gives a different interpreted angle.","Opposite six, adjacent eight. Tangent?|3/4|4/3;3/5;4/5|Six eighths simplifies to three fourths.","Angle 30\xb0, hypotenuse fourteen. Opposite length?|7|14;28;6|One half of fourteen is seven.","Can sine of an acute right-triangle angle exceed one?|No|Yes, always;Only if legs are long;Only in degrees|Opposite leg is shorter than hypotenuse."],t.s)
B.a7Z=new A.cu("Trigonometry Basics",B.aK9,"Right-triangle trigonometry compares sides relative to a chosen angle.","For acute angle \u03b8 in a right triangle, sin \u03b8 = opposite/hypotenuse, cos \u03b8 = adjacent/hypotenuse, and tan \u03b8 = opposite/adjacent. The adjacent leg is not the hypotenuse. With opposite three, adjacent four, and hypotenuse five, the ratios are 3/5, 4/5, and 3/4. Changing the chosen acute angle swaps the opposite and adjacent legs. This lesson gives angles in degrees; calculator mode must match.",B.aV5,"Find opposite side when \u03b8 = 30\xb0 and hypotenuse ten. Step 1: choose sine. Step 2: 1/2 = opposite/10. Step 3: multiply by ten to get five. Step 4: check the opposite is smaller than the hypotenuse.","For opposite six and adjacent eight, find tangent. With angle thirty degrees and hypotenuse fourteen, find the opposite side.","Tangent is 6/8 = 3/4. The second opposite side is seven because sine thirty degrees equals one half.","Choose the angle before naming opposite and adjacent. Do not use a slanted leg as hypotenuse unless it is opposite the right angle. Degrees and radians are different input units.","Identify sides relative to the chosen angle, select the matching ratio, and check units and size.",B.aM8)
B.aJR=s(["Distinguish radius and diameter.","Calculate circumference and area.","Relate central and inscribed angles on one arc."],t.s)
B.b5R={"Radius 3, diameter 6":0,"Central angle 100\xb0":1,"Diameter endpoints":2}
B.aUd=new A.au(B.b5R,["Circumference 6\u03c0; area 9\u03c0.","Same-arc inscribed angle 50\xb0.","An inscribed angle intercepting the semicircle is 90\xb0."],t.w)
B.aMT=s(["Radius three gives diameter what?|6|3;9;1.5|A diameter contains two radii.","Radius three gives circumference what?|6\u03c0|3\u03c0;9\u03c0;12\u03c0|Use twice pi times radius.","Radius three gives area what?|9\u03c0|6\u03c0;3\u03c0;18\u03c0|Square the radius in the area formula.","Diameter ten gives radius what?|5|10;20;2|Radius is half the diameter.","Diameter ten gives area what?|25\u03c0|100\u03c0;10\u03c0;50\u03c0|Use radius five, then square it.","Central angle 100\xb0, same arc inscribed angle what?|50\xb0|100\xb0;200\xb0;25\xb0|The inscribed angle is half the central measure.","Which unit fits circle area?|Square units|Length units only;Degrees;Seconds|Area measures a two-dimensional region.","Inscribed angle intercepting a diameter measures what?|90\xb0|180\xb0;45\xb0;360\xb0|Its central angle is 180 degrees.","Radius four gives circumference what?|8\u03c0|16\u03c0;4\u03c0;32\u03c0|Multiply radius by two pi.","Radius four gives area what?|16\u03c0|8\u03c0;4\u03c0;32\u03c0|Four squared times pi is sixteen pi.","Central angle eighty, same arc inscribed angle what?|40\xb0|80\xb0;160\xb0;20\xb0|Halve the central angle measure."],t.s)
B.a7S=new A.cu("Circles",B.aJR,"Circle length, area, and angles describe different geometric quantities.","A radius joins center to circumference; a diameter passes through the center and is twice the radius. Circumference is 2\u03c0r and area is \u03c0r\xb2. With radius three, circumference is 6\u03c0 and area 9\u03c0. A central angle has its vertex at the center. An inscribed angle with the same intercepted arc has half the central angle measure. The stated same-arc condition matters.",B.aUd,"Use diameter ten. Step 1: radius is five. Step 2: circumference is 2\u03c0 \xd7 5 = 10\u03c0. Step 3: area is \u03c0 \xd7 25 = 25\u03c0. Step 4: attach length units to circumference and square units to area.","For radius four, find circumference and area. Find a same-arc inscribed angle for central angle eighty degrees.","Circumference is 8\u03c0 units, area 16\u03c0 square units, and the inscribed angle is forty degrees.","Do not use diameter as radius in \u03c0r\xb2. Circumference is not area. Angle halving applies only when both angles intercept the same arc.","Choose radius, apply the proper formula and units, and track the intercepted arc.",B.aMT)
B.aAt=s(["Distinguish ordered selections from unordered ones.","Calculate small permutations and combinations.","State probability assumptions."],t.s)
B.b57={"Ordered two from three":0,"Unordered two from three":1,"Two fair coin tosses":2}
B.aUE=new A.au(B.b57,["AB, AC, BA, BC, CA, CB: six","AB, AC, BC: three","HH, HT, TH, TT: four equally likely outcomes"],t.w)
B.aMl=s(["Ordered two different letters from three gives how many?|6|3;9;2|Three first choices and two second choices.","Unordered pairs from three distinct letters gives how many?|3|6;9;2|Each ordered pair has its reversed duplicate.","Unordered pairs of people from five gives how many?|10|20;5;25|Divide five times four by two.","Three-letter codes without repetition from four letters gives what count?|24|64;12;4|Choices decrease four, three, then two.","Two independent fair coin tosses give how many paths?|4|2;3;8|Each of two stages has two possibilities.","Exactly one head in two independent fair coin tosses has probability what?|1/2|1/4;3/4;1|HT and TH are two of four paths.","Which selection makes order matter?|A first-place and second-place award|An unordered two-person committee;A set of two books;Two names listed alphabetically as a group|Swapping award recipients changes the outcome.","Without replacement, why does a later choice count fall?|A selected item is no longer available|Every probability becomes zero;Order stops mattering;The original set grows|The chosen item is removed.","Choose two people from six without order. Count?|15|30;12;36|Six times five divided by two is fifteen.","Two letters with repetition from three letters gives how many?|9|6;3;12|Both stages retain three choices.","Can favorable-count divided by total-count be used directly for biased unequal outcomes?|No|Yes;Only if the total is even;Only if outcomes have names|Count ratios assume equal likelihood."],t.s)
B.a8E=new A.cu("Probability & Combinatorics",B.aAt,"Counting rules must match whether order and repetition matter.","Choosing two different letters in order from A, B, C gives 3 \xd7 2 = 6 codes. Selecting two letters without order gives three pairs: AB, AC, BC. Each unordered pair was counted twice in the ordered list, so divide by 2!. In general nPr = n!/(n \u2212 r)! and nCr = n!/[r!(n \u2212 r)!]. For equally likely outcomes, probability is favorable divided by total. Sampling without replacement changes later available choices.",B.aUE,"Choose two representatives from five people without order. Step 1: ordered choices total 5 \xd7 4 = 20. Step 2: each pair appears twice. Step 3: divide by two to get ten pairs. Step 4: check by systematic listing rather than treating order as a new team.","Count three-letter codes without repetition from four letters. For two independent fair coin tosses, find probability of exactly one head.","The code count is 4 \xd7 3 \xd7 2 = 24. Exactly one head occurs in HT or TH, so probability is 2/4 = 1/2.","Do not use permutations for an unordered committee. Independent fair tosses justify equally likely paths; arbitrary outcomes need not share probability.","Specify order, repetition, and likelihood before choosing a count or probability formula.",B.aMl)
