B.b4D={"(x\xb2 \u2212 4)/(x \u2212 2), x \u2192 2":0,"Step: zero on left, one on right":1,"3/x as x \u2192 +\u221e":2}
B.aUj=new A.au(B.b4D,["Cancel for nearby x; limit four.","Two-sided limit at zero does not exist.","Outputs tend to zero."],t.w)
B.aDe=s(["Limit of (x\xb2 \u2212 4)/(x \u2212 2) as x approaches two?|4|0;2;Undefined necessarily|Nearby values equal x plus two.","Value of (x\xb2 \u2212 4)/(x \u2212 2) at x = 2?|Undefined|4;0;2|The denominator vanishes at two.","Limit of (x\xb2 \u2212 9)/(x \u2212 3) at three?|6|0;3;9|Nearby rule x plus three approaches six.","Left limit zero and right limit one at a point imply what?|No two-sided limit|Limit zero;Limit one;Limit one half|Both approaches must agree.","Limit of 3/x as x tends to positive infinity?|0|3;1;Positive infinity|The denominator grows while numerator stays fixed.","For g(x) = 2x + 2 except g(0) = 5, limit at zero?|2|5;0;No limit|Nearby values use the linear rule.","For g(x) = 2x + 2 when x \u2260 0 and g(0) = 5, is g continuous at zero?|No|Yes;Only from above;Only because five is positive|The limit two differs from the point value five.","What does 0/0 from direct substitution tell?|The direct method is inconclusive|The limit equals zero;The limit equals one;No limit can ever exist|Further algebra or another argument is needed.","Limit of (x\xb2 \u2212 16)/(x \u2212 4) at four?|8|4;0;16|Cancel the factor to obtain nearby rule x plus four.","Limit of (2x + 1)/(x \u2212 1) as x tends to positive infinity?|2|0;1;3|Divide by x; the leading-coefficient ratio is two.","For f(x) = 7x \u2212 2, limit at x = 3?|19|21;5;\u22122|This polynomial is continuous, so substitution gives nineteen."],t.s)
B.a7W=new A.cu("Limits",B.asZ,"A limit describes what function outputs approach as inputs approach a target.","For (x\xb2 \u2212 4)/(x \u2212 2), direct substitution gives zero divided by zero, not a result. Factoring gives x + 2 for x \u2260 2, so the limit at two is four even though the original value is undefined. A two-sided limit exists only when left and right approaches agree. If f(x) is zero for x < 0 and one for x \u2265 0, the one-sided limits at zero differ. Continuity requires the limit to equal the defined point value.",B.aUj,"Find the limit of (x\xb2 \u2212 9)/(x \u2212 3) at three. Step 1: factor numerator as (x \u2212 3)(x + 3). Step 2: cancel only for x \u2260 3. Step 3: nearby outputs follow x + 3. Step 4: approach three to obtain six.","If g(x) = 2x + 2 for x \u2260 0 but g(0) = 5, find the limit at zero and decide continuity.","The limit is two, but g(0) is five. They differ, so g is not continuous at zero.","Zero divided by zero signals that another method is needed, not that the limit is zero. A point value alone cannot establish a limit.","Simplify valid nearby expressions, compare both approaches, and test continuity separately.",B.aDe)
