B.axw=s(["Count objects carefully using one number for each object.","Count forward and backward within 100.","Tell the number before and after a given number.","Compare two small quantities using more, fewer, or the same."],t.s)
B.aYA=new A.a6("Learning objectives","",B.axw,"cyan",null,null,null,!1)
B.aDZ=s(["Touch, point to, or move each object once as you count. Say one number for each object: \u201cone, two, three\u2026\u201d This is called one-to-one counting.","The last number you say tells how many objects are in the whole group. If you count 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, then there are 5 objects.","Objects do not have to be in a straight row. You can still count them correctly as long as you keep track of which objects have already been counted."],t.s)
B.b_T=new A.a6("1. Count one object at a time","1",B.aDZ,"cyan",null,"g1-counting-counters","Eight counters counted one by one",!1)
B.aOR=s(["When we count forward, each next number is exactly one more: 17, 18, 19, 20, 21. When we count backward, each next number is one less.","After 29 comes 30. After 39 comes 40. The same pattern continues through 49, 59, 69, 79, 89, and 99.","The decade numbers\u201410, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80, 90, and 100\u2014help us organize the long counting sequence."],t.s)
B.b00=new A.a6("2. Notice the pattern in the number names","10",B.aOR,"violet",null,"g1-counting-number-line","Numbers increase by one as you move right",!1)
B.aAi=s(["Step 1: Start with an object you can recognize easily, such as the top-left counter.","Step 2: Point to each new object once while saying the next number name.","Step 3: Stop when every object has been counted. If the last number said is 8, the group has 8 objects.","Step 4: Check by counting again in a different direction. The total should stay the same."],t.s)
B.b_r=new A.a6("3. Worked example: count a group","\u2192",B.aAi,"orange",null,"g1-counting-counters","Step-by-step counting model",!1)
B.aMS=s(["On a number line, numbers grow larger as you move to the right. The number just before 42 is 41, and the number just after 42 is 43.","A number can also be between two neighbors. For example, 56 is between 55 and 57.","To compare quantities, count both groups. The group with the greater counting number has more objects."],t.s)
B.aZy=new A.a6("4. Before, after, and between","\u2194",B.aMS,"green",null,"g1-counting-number-line","Use a number line to find neighbors",!1)
B.ax5=s(["Do not count one object twice. Moving counted objects to one side can help.","Do not skip an object just because it is far from the others. Scan the whole group before you finish.","Do not guess the total from the size of the objects. Five large blocks are still fewer than eight tiny buttons."],t.s)
B.aYP=new A.a6("5. Common counting mistakes","!",B.ax5,"magenta",null,null,null,!1)
B.auk=s([B.aYA,B.b_T,B.b00,B.b_r,B.aZy,B.aYP],t.u)
B.aYd=new A.hD("Counting tells us how many","Counting is more than saying number names in order. We count to find how many objects are in a group, to tell which number comes before or after another number, and to describe where a number belongs on a number line. In this lesson, you will build a strong counting pattern from 0 all the way to 100.",B.auk,"The last counting number tells the total","Count each object exactly once. When you have counted every object, the final number you say is the number of objects in the group.",50)
B.aZk=new A.a6("LEARNING GOALS \ud83c\udfaf","",B.e,"cyan","By the end of this lesson, the learner should be able to:\n\n\u2022 Name important external body parts.\n\u2022 Explain simple jobs of different body parts.\n\u2022 Identify the five commonly taught senses.\n\u2022 Match each sense with the body part mainly used for it.\n\u2022 Describe how our senses help us learn about our surroundings.\n\u2022 Explain simple ways to protect important sense organs.",null,null,!1)
B.b_q=new A.a6("OPENING \u2014 YOUR AMAZING BODY \ud83e\uddd2","",B.e,"cyan","Your body has many different parts.\n\nSome body parts help you move.\n\nSome help you hold things.\n\nSome help you eat.\n\nSome help you see, hear, smell, taste, and feel.\n\nYour body parts work together every day.\n\nThink about what happens when you see a ball and catch it. \u26bd\n\nYour eyes see the ball.\n\nYour brain helps you understand where it is.\n\nYour arms and hands move.\n\nYour fingers help you hold it.\n\nSeveral parts of your body worked together!\n\nIn this lesson, we will learn about:\n\n\ud83e\uddcd body parts\n\n\ud83d\udc40 sight\n\n\ud83d\udc42 hearing\n\n\ud83d\udc43 smell\n\n\ud83d\udc45 taste\n\n\u270b touch",null,null,!1)
B.aYt=new A.a6("SECTION 1 \u2014 IMPORTANT BODY PARTS \ud83e\uddcd","",B.e,"cyan","Our bodies have many parts.\n\nEach part can have one or more important jobs.\n\nLet's look at some common external body parts.\n\nHEAD\n\nYour head is at the top of your body.\n\nIt contains important sense organs such as:\n\n\ud83d\udc40 eyes\n\n\ud83d\udc42 ears\n\n\ud83d\udc43 nose\n\n\ud83d\udc45 tongue inside the mouth\n\nYour brain is also protected inside your skull.\n\nNECK\n\nYour neck connects your head to the rest of your body.\n\nIt also helps you turn and move your head.\n\nARMS \ud83d\udcaa\n\nYour arms help you:\n\n\u2022 reach;\n\u2022 lift;\n\u2022 carry;\n\u2022 push;\n\u2022 pull.\n\nHANDS \u270b\n\nYour hands help you:\n\n\u2022 hold objects;\n\u2022 write;\n\u2022 pick things up;\n\u2022 feel surfaces;\n\u2022 perform many careful movements.\n\nLEGS \ud83e\uddb5\n\nYour legs help support your body.\n\nThey help you:\n\n\u2022 stand;\n\u2022 walk;\n\u2022 run;\n\u2022 jump.\n\nFEET \ud83e\uddb6\n\nYour feet help support your body and help you move.",null,null,!1)
B.aZN=new A.a6("VISUAL 1 \u2014 BASIC BODY PARTS","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-3-1","Different body parts help us do different jobs.",!1)
B.aZJ=new A.a6("SECTION 2 \u2014 BODY PARTS WORK TOGETHER \ud83e\udd1d","",B.e,"cyan","Your body parts often work together.\n\nImagine riding a bicycle. \ud83d\udeb2\n\nYour eyes help you see where you are going.\n\nYour hands hold the handlebars.\n\nYour arms help steer.\n\nYour legs push the pedals.\n\nYour feet stay on the pedals.\n\nYour ears may help you notice sounds around you.\n\nMany parts work together to help you ride safely.\n\n\ud83d\udca1 REMEMBER:\n\nOne action can use several body parts at the same time.",null,null,!1)
B.aYO=new A.a6("SECTION 3 \u2014 WHAT ARE SENSES? \ud83e\udde0","",B.e,"cyan","Our senses help us collect information about the world around us.\n\nIn Grade 1, we usually learn five main senses:\n\n\ud83d\udc40 Sight\n\n\ud83d\udc42 Hearing\n\n\ud83d\udc43 Smell\n\n\ud83d\udc45 Taste\n\n\u270b Touch\n\nEach sense helps us notice different kinds of information.\n\nOur sense organs collect information, and the brain helps us understand it.",null,null,!1)
B.aZs=new A.a6("VISUAL 2 \u2014 THE FIVE SENSES","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-3-2","Our senses help us learn about our surroundings.",!1)
B.aYC=new A.a6("SECTION 4 \u2014 SIGHT \ud83d\udc40","",B.e,"cyan","We use our eyes mainly for sight.\n\nSight helps us notice:\n\n\ud83c\udfa8 colors;\n\n\ud83d\udd3a shapes;\n\n\ud83d\udccf sizes;\n\n\ud83d\udccd locations;\n\n\ud83c\udfc3 movement;\n\n\ud83d\ude42 faces;\n\n\ud83d\udcd6 words and pictures.\n\nEXAMPLE\n\nImagine you see a red apple. \ud83c\udf4e\n\nYour eyes help you notice:\n\n\u2022 its red color;\n\u2022 its round shape;\n\u2022 where it is.\n\nSight gives you information before you even touch the apple.",null,null,!1)
B.b0e=new A.a6("SECTION 5 \u2014 HEARING \ud83d\udc42","",B.e,"cyan","We use our ears mainly for hearing.\n\nHearing helps us notice sounds.\n\nWe can hear:\n\n\ud83c\udfb5 music;\n\n\ud83d\udde3\ufe0f voices;\n\n\ud83d\udc26 birds;\n\n\ud83d\udd14 bells;\n\n\ud83d\ude97 vehicles;\n\n\ud83c\udf27\ufe0f rain.\n\nEXAMPLE\n\nSuppose you hear a school bell.\n\nYour ears detect the sound.\n\nYour brain helps you recognize what the sound may mean.\n\nHearing can help us communicate and notice what is happening nearby.",null,null,!1)
B.aZc=new A.a6("SECTION 6 \u2014 SMELL \ud83d\udc43","",B.e,"cyan","We use our nose mainly for smelling.\n\nSmell helps us notice odors in the air.\n\nWe may smell:\n\n\ud83c\udf38 flowers;\n\n\ud83c\udf5e bread;\n\n\ud83c\udf4a fruit;\n\n\ud83c\udf72 food;\n\n\ud83c\udf27\ufe0f wet soil after rain.\n\nEXAMPLE\n\nImagine you walk near a flower garden.\n\nYou may smell the flowers before you touch them.\n\nYour nose helps collect information from the air.",null,null,!1)
B.b_3=new A.a6("SECTION 7 \u2014 TASTE \ud83d\udc45","",B.e,"cyan","Our tongue helps us taste.\n\nTaste helps us notice different flavors in food.\n\nCommon taste words include:\n\n\ud83c\udf6c sweet;\n\n\ud83c\udf4b sour;\n\n\ud83e\uddc2 salty;\n\n\ud83e\udd6c bitter;\n\n\ud83c\udf72 savory.\n\nTaste and smell can work together.\n\nFor example, food may seem different when your nose is blocked.\n\nSAFETY NOTE \u26a0\ufe0f\n\nDo not taste unknown things just to learn what they are.\n\nOnly taste food or drinks that a trusted adult says are safe.",null,null,!1)
B.aZt=new A.a6("SECTION 8 \u2014 TOUCH \u270b","",B.e,"cyan","We sense touch through our skin.\n\nYour skin covers your body.\n\nTouch can help you notice:\n\n\ud83d\udd25 warm;\n\n\ud83e\uddca cold;\n\n\ud83e\uddf8 soft;\n\n\ud83e\udea8 rough;\n\n\ud83e\udeb5 hard;\n\n\ud83e\uddfd smooth or bumpy surfaces;\n\ngentle pressure.\n\nEXAMPLE\n\nTouch a soft towel.\n\nThen touch a rough rock.\n\nThey feel different because your skin can detect information about their surfaces.\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f Never touch something dangerous just to test how it feels.",null,null,!1)
B.aZW=new A.a6("VISUAL 3 \u2014 WHAT CAN EACH SENSE NOTICE?","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-3-3","Different senses give us different kinds of information.",!1)
B.aZv=new A.a6("SECTION 9 \u2014 MORE THAN ONE SENSE AT A TIME \ud83e\udde0","",B.e,"cyan","We often use several senses together.\n\nImagine eating an orange. \ud83c\udf4a\n\nYou can:\n\n\ud83d\udc40 see its color;\n\n\u270b feel its peel;\n\n\ud83d\udc43 smell it;\n\n\ud83d\udc45 taste it;\n\n\ud83d\udc42 hear a small sound as you peel it.\n\nUsing several senses can give us more information about an object.",null,null,!1)
B.b0a=new A.a6("WORKED EXAMPLE \u2014 EXPLORING AN APPLE \ud83c\udf4e","",B.e,"cyan","Suppose we want to learn about an apple.\n\nWhat can our senses tell us?\n\nSTEP 1 \u2014 SIGHT \ud83d\udc40\n\nWe can see that the apple is red and round.\n\nSTEP 2 \u2014 TOUCH \u270b\n\nWe can feel that the skin is smooth and firm.\n\nSTEP 3 \u2014 SMELL \ud83d\udc43\n\nWe may notice the apple's smell.\n\nSTEP 4 \u2014 TASTE \ud83d\udc45\n\nIf the apple is safe to eat, we can taste it.\n\nSTEP 5 \u2014 HEARING \ud83d\udc42\n\nWe may hear a crunch when someone bites it.\n\nCONCLUSION:\n\nSeveral senses can work together to help us learn about one object.",null,null,!1)
B.b0u=new A.a6("Picture guide","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-3-apple",null,!1)
B.b0y=new A.a6("SECTION 10 \u2014 WHICH SENSE SHOULD I USE? \ud83e\udd14","",B.e,"cyan",'Different questions may need different senses.\n\nQUESTION:\n\n"What color is the flower?"\n\nBest sense:\n\ud83d\udc40 Sight\n\n\nQUESTION:\n\n"Is the bell ringing?"\n\nBest sense:\n\ud83d\udc42 Hearing\n\n\nQUESTION:\n\n"Does this flower have a smell?"\n\nBest sense:\n\ud83d\udc43 Smell\n\n\nQUESTION:\n\n"Is this towel soft?"\n\nBest sense:\n\u270b Touch\n\n\nQUESTION:\n\n"Does this safe fruit taste sweet?"\n\nBest sense:\n\ud83d\udc45 Taste\n\nChoosing the right sense helps us gather the information we need.',null,null,!1)
B.aZC=new A.a6("GUIDED EXAMPLE \u2014 A RINGING PHONE \ud83d\udcf1","",B.e,"cyan","A phone is somewhere nearby.\n\nYou cannot see it yet.\n\nThen you hear it ringing.\n\nWhich sense helped you know that something was happening?\n\n\ud83d\udc42 Hearing\n\nYour ears detected the sound.\n\nNow suppose you look around and find the phone.\n\nWhich sense helped you locate it by looking?\n\n\ud83d\udc40 Sight\n\nWe can use different senses one after another.",null,null,!1)
B.aYJ=new A.a6("SECTION 11 \u2014 PROTECTING OUR EYES \ud83d\udc40","",B.e,"cyan","Our eyes are important.\n\nWe should take care of them.\n\nGood habits include:\n\n\u2705 keeping sharp objects away from the eyes;\n\n\u2705 using appropriate eye protection when needed;\n\n\u2705 keeping dirty hands away from the eyes;\n\n\u2705 telling an adult if your eyes hurt or something gets into them.\n\nDo not stare directly at the Sun. \u2600\ufe0f\n\nVery bright light can harm the eyes.",null,null,!1)
B.b_0=new A.a6("SECTION 12 \u2014 PROTECTING OUR EARS \ud83d\udc42","",B.e,"cyan","Our ears help us hear.\n\nWe should protect them too.\n\nGood habits include:\n\n\u2705 keeping the volume at a comfortable level;\n\n\u2705 moving away from sounds that are painfully loud;\n\n\u2705 never pushing objects deep into the ears;\n\n\u2705 telling an adult if an ear hurts or hearing suddenly changes.",null,null,!1)
B.aYy=new A.a6("SECTION 13 \u2014 PROTECTING OUR NOSE, MOUTH, AND SKIN \ud83e\uddfc","",B.e,"cyan","We can take care of other sense organs too.\n\nNOSE \ud83d\udc43\n\nDo not put objects inside the nose.\n\nAvoid smelling unknown chemicals closely.\n\nMOUTH AND TONGUE \ud83d\udc45\n\nOnly eat and taste safe food.\n\nBrush your teeth and keep your mouth clean.\n\nSKIN \u270b\n\nWash your skin regularly.\n\nProtect it from very hot objects.\n\nTell an adult if you have a painful injury or burn.",null,null,!1)
B.b_e=new A.a6("Picture guide","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-3-safety",null,!1)
B.b_B=new A.a6("SECTION 14 \u2014 BODY PART OR SENSE? \ud83d\udd0e","",B.e,"cyan","Body parts can help with actions, while sense organs help us gather information.\n\nExamples:\n\n\ud83e\uddb5 Leg\nhelps us walk and run\n\n\u270b Hand\nhelps us hold objects\n\n\ud83d\udc40 Eyes\nhelp us see\n\n\ud83d\udc42 Ears\nhelp us hear\n\n\ud83d\udc43 Nose\nhelps us smell\n\n\ud83d\udc45 Tongue\nhelps us taste\n\n\ud83d\udd90\ufe0f Skin\nhelps us sense touch\n\nSome body parts can have more than one job.\n\nFor example, your hand can hold something AND the skin on your hand can help you feel it.",null,null,!1)
B.b0l=new A.a6("VISUAL 4 \u2014 MATCH THE SENSE TO THE BODY PART","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-3-4","",!1)
B.b__=new A.a6("SECTION 15 \u2014 OUR BRAIN HELPS US UNDERSTAND \ud83e\udde0","",B.e,"cyan",'Our eyes, ears, nose, tongue, and skin collect information.\n\nThe brain helps us understand that information.\n\nFor example:\n\nYou hear a bark. \ud83d\udc15\n\nYour ears detect the sound.\n\nYour brain helps you recognize that the sound may be a dog.\n\nYou see a yellow banana. \ud83c\udf4c\n\nYour eyes detect color and shape.\n\nYour brain helps you recognize the object.\n\nFor Grade 1, remember:\n\n"Sense organs collect information. The brain helps us understand it."',null,null,!1)
B.b0i=new A.a6("SECTION 16 \u2014 NOT EVERYONE SENSES THE WORLD THE SAME WAY \u2764\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan","People can experience the world in different ways.\n\nSome people may not see clearly.\n\nSome may not hear clearly.\n\nThey can use tools and other ways to learn and communicate.\n\nExamples can include:\n\n\ud83d\udc53 glasses;\n\n\ud83e\uddbb hearing devices;\n\n\ud83e\uddaf mobility tools;\n\n\ud83e\udd1f sign language;\n\n\u283f Braille.\n\nPeople can learn, communicate, and explore in many different ways.\n\nTreat everyone with respect.",null,null,!1)
B.b0c=new A.a6("ACTIVITY \u2014 WHICH SENSE? \ud83e\udde0","",B.e,"cyan","Think about each situation.\n\n\nYou want to know the color of a ball. \u26bd\n\nUse:\n\ud83d\udc40 Sight\n\n\nYou want to know whether music is playing. \ud83c\udfb5\n\nUse:\n\ud83d\udc42 Hearing\n\n\nYou want to know whether a blanket feels soft. \ud83e\uddf8\n\nUse:\n\u270b Touch\n\n\nYou want to notice the smell of a flower. \ud83c\udf38\n\nUse:\n\ud83d\udc43 Smell\n\n\nYou want to know the flavor of a safe piece of fruit. \ud83c\udf53\n\nUse:\n\ud83d\udc45 Taste",null,null,!1)
B.b0p=new A.a6("ACTIVITY \u2014 BODY PART AND JOB \ud83d\udcaa","",B.e,"cyan","Match the body part to a job.\n\nLEGS \ud83e\uddb5\n\u2192 walking and running\n\nHANDS \u270b\n\u2192 holding and picking up\n\nEYES \ud83d\udc40\n\u2192 seeing\n\nEARS \ud83d\udc42\n\u2192 hearing\n\nNOSE \ud83d\udc43\n\u2192 smelling\n\nTONGUE \ud83d\udc45\n\u2192 tasting\n\nSKIN \ud83d\udd90\ufe0f\n\u2192 sensing touch",null,null,!1)
B.b_5=new A.a6("Picture guide","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-3-jobs",null,!1)
B.b0H=new A.a6("COMMON MISTAKES \u26a0\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan",'MISTAKE 1:\n\n"We only use one sense at a time."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nWe often use several senses together.\n\n\nMISTAKE 2:\n\n"Touch only happens in the hands."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nYour skin covers your whole body and helps you sense touch.\n\n\nMISTAKE 3:\n\n"The nose is used for taste."\n\n\u274c Not exactly.\n\nThe tongue is an important organ for taste.\n\nSmell can also affect how food seems to taste.\n\n\nMISTAKE 4:\n\n"Ears only help us when someone is talking."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nEars can detect many sounds such as music, bells, animals, and vehicles.\n\n\nMISTAKE 5:\n\n"The eyes understand everything we see by themselves."\n\n\u274c Not quite.\n\nThe eyes collect visual information, and the brain helps us understand it.',null,null,!1)
B.b_g=new A.a6("QUICK CHECK \ud83e\udde0","",B.e,"cyan","1. WHICH BODY PART HELPS YOU SEE? \ud83d\udc40\n\nAnswer:\nEyes\n\nWhy?\nEyes collect visual information.\n\n\n2. WHICH SENSE HELPS YOU HEAR A BELL? \ud83d\udd14\n\nAnswer:\nHearing\n\nWhy?\nOur ears detect sounds.\n\n\n3. WHICH BODY PART HELPS YOU SMELL A FLOWER? \ud83c\udf38\n\nAnswer:\nNose\n\nWhy?\nThe nose helps detect smells in the air.\n\n\n4. WHICH SENSE HELPS YOU KNOW THAT A BLANKET IS SOFT? \ud83e\uddf8\n\nAnswer:\nTouch\n\nWhy?\nThe skin helps us feel texture.\n\n\n5. CAN MORE THAN ONE SENSE WORK AT THE SAME TIME?\n\nAnswer:\nYes\n\nWhy?\nWe often use several senses together to learn more about an object or situation.",null,null,!0)
B.aZG=new A.a6("KEY CONCEPT \u2b50","",B.e,"cyan","TITLE:\n\nOur body parts and senses help us explore the world.\n\nBODY:\n\nDifferent body parts have different jobs.\n\nOur eyes help us see.\n\nOur ears help us hear.\n\nOur nose helps us smell.\n\nOur tongue helps us taste.\n\nOur skin helps us feel touch.\n\nOur sense organs collect information, and the brain helps us understand it.",null,null,!1)
B.aYN=new A.a6("FINAL RECAP \ud83c\udf1f","",B.e,"cyan","Remember:\n\n\ud83e\uddcd Our bodies have many different parts.\n\n\ud83d\udcaa Arms and hands help us reach, carry, and hold.\n\n\ud83e\uddb5 Legs and feet help us stand and move.\n\n\ud83d\udc40 Eyes help us see.\n\n\ud83d\udc42 Ears help us hear.\n\n\ud83d\udc43 The nose helps us smell.\n\n\ud83d\udc45 The tongue helps us taste.\n\n\u270b Skin helps us feel touch.\n\n\ud83e\udde0 The brain helps us understand information from our senses.\n\n\ud83e\udd1d Several body parts and senses can work together.\n\n\ud83d\udee1\ufe0f We should protect our sense organs and use them safely.",null,null,!1)
B.ayW=s([B.aZk,B.b_q,B.aYt,B.aZN,B.aZJ,B.aYO,B.aZs,B.aYC,B.b0e,B.aZc,B.b_3,B.aZt,B.aZW,B.aZv,B.b0a,B.b0u,B.b0y,B.aZC,B.aYJ,B.b_0,B.aYy,B.b_e,B.b_B,B.b0l,B.b__,B.b0i,B.b0c,B.b0p,B.b_5,B.b0H,B.b_g,B.aZG,B.aYN],t.u)
B.aYe=new A.hD("Our Body & Senses \ud83d\udc40\ud83d\udc42","Learn how body parts help us move, explore, and understand the world around us.\n20\u201325 minutes",B.ayW,"","",50)
B.aLN=s(["Explain addition as joining groups or counting on.","Read and write simple addition equations.","Find sums within 20 using objects, drawings, or a number line.","Choose an efficient strategy such as counting on from the larger number."],t.s)
B.b_a=new A.a6("Learning objectives","",B.aLN,"cyan",null,null,null,!1)
B.aAj=s(["Suppose you have 3 red counters and someone gives you 2 more. Put the groups together and count all the counters: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.","We write this as 3 + 2 = 5. The numbers being added are called addends, and the answer is called the sum.","Addition does not change just because the objects are different. 3 apples + 2 apples and 3 blocks + 2 blocks both have a total of 5 objects."],t.s)
B.aYI=new A.a6("1. Join two groups","+",B.aAj,"cyan",null,"g1-addition-groups","3 joined with 2 makes 5",!1)
B.aGE=s(["For 6 + 3, you do not have to count 1 through 6 again. Start at 6 and count on three more numbers: 7, 8, 9.","Counting on is especially helpful when one addend is small.","You can start with the larger addend to make the counting shorter. For 2 + 8, start at 8 and count 9, 10."],t.s)
B.aZM=new A.a6("2. Count on instead of starting over","\u2192",B.aGE,"violet",null,"g1-counting-number-line","Start at 6 and jump forward 3 spaces",!1)
B.apW=s(["Step 1: Start with 7 because it is already one of the groups.","Step 2: Count on 5 more: 8, 9, 10, 11, 12.","Step 3: The last number is 12, so 7 + 5 = 12.","Step 4: Check by drawing 7 dots and 5 more dots, then count all 12 dots."],t.s)
B.aYw=new A.a6("3. Worked example: 7 + 5","1-2-3",B.apW,"orange",null,null,null,!1)
B.aPt=s(["Changing the order does not change the sum: 4 + 3 = 7 and 3 + 4 = 7.","Adding zero keeps a number the same: 9 + 0 = 9.","Doubles are useful facts to remember: 4 + 4 = 8, 5 + 5 = 10, and so on."],t.s)
B.aZb=new A.a6("4. Addition facts are related","\u2194",B.aPt,"green",null,null,null,!1)
B.aN2=s(["When counting on, do not count the starting number as one of the jumps. For 5 + 2, say 6, 7\u2014not 5, 6.","The equal sign does not mean \u201cthe answer comes next.\u201d It means the amount on the left is the same as the amount on the right.","Check that you added every object once and did not accidentally count an object twice."],t.s)
B.b_G=new A.a6("5. Common addition mistakes","!",B.aN2,"magenta",null,null,null,!1)
B.aHg=s([B.b_a,B.aYI,B.aZM,B.aYw,B.aZb,B.b_G],t.u)
B.aYf=new A.hD("Addition joins amounts together","Addition helps us find a total when two or more groups are combined. We can model addition with real objects, drawings, number lines, fingers, or equations. The plus sign (+) means join or add, and the equal sign (=) tells us that both sides have the same value.",B.aHg,"Addition finds the total after joining","You can model a + b by starting with one amount and counting forward by the other amount. The number you reach is the sum.",50)
B.auP=s(["Explain that 10 ones make 1 ten.","Identify the tens digit and ones digit in a two-digit number.","Build and break apart numbers from 10 to 99.","Compare two-digit numbers by looking at tens first."],t.s)
B.b_d=new A.a6("Learning objectives","",B.auP,"cyan",null,null,null,!1)
B.aGC=s(["Instead of counting 34 separate objects one by one, we can group them. Every group of 10 objects is called one ten.","Thirty-four means 3 tens and 4 ones. The 3 is in the tens place, so it represents 30. The 4 is in the ones place, so it represents 4.","We can write the same idea as 34 = 30 + 4."],t.s)
B.aZn=new A.a6("1. Make groups of ten","10",B.aGC,"cyan",null,"g1-place-value-blocks","Three tens and four ones make 34",!1)
B.aFv=s(["In a two-digit number, the left digit is the tens digit and the right digit is the ones digit.","For 57, the digit 5 means 5 tens, or 50. The digit 7 means 7 ones.","For 80, the digit 8 means 8 tens. The 0 tells us there are no extra ones."],t.s)
B.aZX=new A.a6("2. Read the tens and ones places","TO",B.aFv,"violet",null,"g1-place-value-blocks","Tens are on the left; ones are on the right",!1)
B.aC7=s(["Step 1: Look at the tens digit. The 4 tells us to make 4 groups of ten.","Step 2: Look at the ones digit. The 6 tells us to add 6 single objects.","Step 3: Combine the values: 40 + 6 = 46.","Step 4: Check by counting by tens first\u201410, 20, 30, 40\u2014then count on six more: 41, 42, 43, 44, 45, 46."],t.s)
B.b_6=new A.a6("3. Worked example: build 46","\u2192",B.aC7,"orange",null,null,null,!1)
B.aLz=s(["Compare the tens digits first. A number with more tens is greater. For example, 63 is greater than 48 because 6 tens is more than 4 tens.","If the tens digits are the same, compare the ones. For example, 52 is greater than 49 already because 5 tens is more than 4 tens. But for 52 and 57, both have 5 tens, so compare 2 ones and 7 ones.","Equal numbers have the same number of tens and the same number of ones."],t.s)
B.aZ0=new A.a6("4. Compare two-digit numbers",">",B.aLz,"green",null,null,null,!1)
B.asR=s(["Do not read 42 as 4 + 2. The 4 is worth 40 because it is in the tens place.","A zero can be important. In 60, the zero means there are no ones, not that the whole number is zero.","When comparing, start with tens. Looking only at the last digit can give the wrong answer."],t.s)
B.aZp=new A.a6("5. Common place-value mistakes","!",B.asR,"magenta",null,null,null,!1)
B.aQq=s([B.b_d,B.aZn,B.aZX,B.b_6,B.aZ0,B.aZp],t.u)
B.aYh=new A.hD("A digit has value because of its place","Two-digit numbers are easier to understand when we group objects into tens and ones. Ten single objects can be bundled together to make one ten. The position of a digit tells whether it counts tens or ones.",B.aQq,"10 ones = 1 ten","For a two-digit number, the left digit counts tens and the right digit counts ones. Example: 72 = 7 tens + 2 ones = 70 + 2.",50)
B.aE2=s(["Name circles, triangles, squares, and rectangles.","Describe shapes using sides and corners.","Sort shapes by their properties instead of only by color or size.","Identify and continue simple repeating patterns such as AB and AAB."],t.s)
B.aZS=new A.a6("Learning objectives","",B.aE2,"cyan",null,null,null,!1)
B.aHC=s(["A circle is round. It has no straight sides and no corners.","A triangle has 3 straight sides and 3 corners.","A square has 4 equal straight sides and 4 square corners, like the corners of a sheet of paper. A rectangle has 4 straight sides and 4 square corners; its side lengths do not all have to be equal. A square is a special rectangle."],t.s)
B.b_f=new A.a6("1. Look at sides and corners","\u25b3",B.aHC,"cyan",null,"g1-shapes-basic","Common Grade 1 shapes",!1)
B.aDC=s(["Turning a triangle upside down does not stop it from being a triangle. It still has 3 straight sides and 3 corners.","A square can look like a diamond when it is turned, but its properties are still the same.","Use properties\u2014not the direction, color, or size\u2014to decide what shape you see."],t.s)
B.b_M=new A.a6("2. A shape stays the same when it turns","\u21bb",B.aDC,"violet",null,null,null,!1)
B.avu=s(["Step 1: Count the straight sides.","Step 2: Count the corners.","Step 3: Compare those properties with the shape rules. A shape with 3 straight sides and 3 corners is a triangle.","Step 4: Turn the page or imagine the shape rotated. Its name should stay the same."],t.s)
B.aYB=new A.a6("3. Worked example: classify a shape","1-2-3",B.avu,"orange",null,null,null,!1)
B.aNH=s(["A repeating pattern has a small part that repeats again and again. In circle, square, circle, square, the repeating unit is \u201ccircle, square.\u201d","An AB pattern uses two items in turn: A, B, A, B. An AAB pattern repeats two of one item followed by another: A, A, B, A, A, B.","To find what comes next, say the repeating unit aloud and continue it in the same order."],t.s)
B.b_b=new A.a6("4. Find the repeating unit in a pattern","AB",B.aNH,"green",null,"g1-shapes-pattern","Circle, square, circle, square...",!1)
B.awX=s(["Do not name a shape only by how it is facing. Count its sides and corners.","Do not assume every four-sided shape is a square. A rectangle can have two long sides and two short sides.","When continuing a pattern, identify the full repeating unit first. Guessing from only the last item can lead to the wrong answer."],t.s)
B.b0b=new A.a6("5. Common shape and pattern mistakes","!",B.awX,"magenta",null,null,null,!1)
B.aEZ=s([B.aZS,B.b_f,B.b_M,B.aYB,B.b_b,B.b0b],t.u)
B.aYi=new A.hD("Shapes have properties and patterns repeat by a rule","Geometry begins by noticing what makes shapes alike or different. Patterns begin by noticing what repeats. In this lesson, you will describe common two-dimensional shapes and use repeating rules to predict what comes next.",B.aEZ,"Properties name shapes; rules continue patterns","Count sides and corners to identify a shape. For a repeating pattern, find the smallest group that repeats, then continue that group in order.",50)
B.aAA=s(["Model subtraction by taking objects away.","Read and solve subtraction equations within 20.","Count backward to find a difference.","Connect addition and subtraction as related operations."],t.s)
B.b07=new A.a6("Learning objectives","",B.aAA,"cyan",null,null,null,!1)
B.aOt=s(["For 7 \u2212 2, begin with 7 objects. Remove or cross out 2 objects. Count what remains: 5.","We write 7 \u2212 2 = 5. The first number tells the starting amount, the second tells how many are removed, and the answer tells what remains.","Subtraction makes sense only when you know what the numbers represent. A drawing can help you see the story."],t.s)
B.aZU=new A.a6("1. Start with the whole, then take away","\u2212",B.aOt,"cyan",null,"g1-subtraction-take-away","Seven counters with two crossed out leaves five",!1)
B.aHl=s(["To solve 9 \u2212 3, start at 9 and move backward three steps: 8, 7, 6. The answer is 6.","Moving left on a number line makes the number smaller. Each single jump left subtracts 1.","Counting back is useful when the amount being subtracted is small."],t.s)
B.b_S=new A.a6("2. Count backward on a number line","\u2190",B.aHl,"violet",null,"g1-counting-number-line","Start at a number and jump left to subtract",!1)
B.aIa=s(["Step 1: Start at 13.","Step 2: Count backward five numbers: 12, 11, 10, 9, 8.","Step 3: Stop after exactly five backward moves. The answer is 8.","Step 4: Check with addition. Since 8 + 5 = 13, we know 13 \u2212 5 = 8."],t.s)
B.b08=new A.a6("3. Worked example: 13 \u2212 5","1-2-3",B.aIa,"orange",null,null,null,!1)
B.awK=s(["Addition and subtraction are related. The facts 6 + 4 = 10, 4 + 6 = 10, 10 \u2212 6 = 4, and 10 \u2212 4 = 6 belong to the same fact family.","If you solve 12 \u2212 7 = 5, add 5 + 7. If the result is 12, your subtraction is correct.","Subtracting zero changes nothing: 8 \u2212 0 = 8. Subtracting a number from itself gives zero: 8 \u2212 8 = 0."],t.s)
B.b_L=new A.a6("4. Addition can check subtraction","+/\u2212",B.awK,"green",null,null,null,!1)
B.aMD=s(["Do not count the starting number as the first backward jump. For 8 \u2212 2, move to 7 and then 6.","Pay attention to which number comes first. 9 \u2212 4 is not the same as 4 \u2212 9 in Grade 1 whole-number subtraction.","If you use a drawing, cross out exactly the number being taken away, then count only the objects that are still visible."],t.s)
B.aZE=new A.a6("5. Common subtraction mistakes","!",B.aMD,"magenta",null,null,null,!1)
B.ayM=s([B.b07,B.aZU,B.b_S,B.b08,B.b_L,B.aZE],t.u)
B.aYk=new A.hD("Subtraction finds what is left or the difference","Subtraction can describe taking objects away, finding how many remain, or comparing two amounts. The minus sign (\u2212) tells us to subtract. You can use counters, drawings, or a number line to make the change visible.",B.ayM,"Subtract by removing or counting backward","Start with the whole amount, take away the stated amount, and count what remains. You can check the answer by adding the parts back together.",50)
B.b_s=new A.a6("LEARNING GOALS \ud83c\udfaf","",B.e,"cyan","By the end of this lesson, the learner should be able to:\n\n\u2022 Identify plants and animals as living things.\n\u2022 Describe basic needs shared by plants and animals.\n\u2022 Recognize important parts of common plants and animals.\n\u2022 Explain simple differences between plants and animals.\n\u2022 Match plants and animals to places where they can live.",null,null,!1)
B.b06=new A.a6("OPENING \u2014 TWO KINDS OF LIVING THINGS \ud83c\udf31\ud83d\udc15","",B.e,"cyan","In Lesson 1, we learned that living things grow, need resources, respond to their surroundings, and have life cycles.\n\nNow let's look more closely at two important groups of living things:\n\n\ud83c\udf3f Plants\n\nand\n\n\ud83d\udc3e Animals\n\nPlants and animals can look very different.\n\nA sunflower does not look like a puppy.\n\nA fish does not look like a tree.\n\nBut plants and animals are both living things.\n\nThey both need resources from their environment.\n\nThey both grow and change.\n\nThey both have parts that help them live.",null,null,!1)
B.aZx=new A.a6("SECTION 1 \u2014 WHAT IS A PLANT? \ud83c\udf31","",B.e,"cyan","A plant is a living thing.\n\nPlants come in many shapes and sizes.\n\nSome are tiny.\n\nSome grow into very tall trees. \ud83c\udf33\n\nExamples of plants include:\n\n\ud83c\udf3b Sunflowers\n\ud83c\udf33 Trees\n\ud83c\udf35 Cacti\n\ud83c\udf3e Grass\n\ud83c\udf3f Ferns\n\ud83c\udf39 Roses\n\nMost plants stay rooted in one place.\n\nBut staying in one place does NOT mean they are nonliving.\n\nPlants grow, use resources, respond to their surroundings, and have life cycles.",null,null,!1)
B.b_Y=new A.a6("VISUAL 1 \u2014 MANY KINDS OF PLANTS","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-2-1","Plants can look different, but they are all living things.",!1)
B.b_n=new A.a6("SECTION 2 \u2014 BASIC PARTS OF A PLANT \ud83c\udf31","",B.e,"cyan","Plants have parts that help them survive.\n\nLet's learn four important parts.\n\nROOTS\n\nRoots are usually found under the ground.\n\nRoots help:\n\n\u2022 hold the plant in place;\n\u2022 take in water;\n\u2022 take in minerals from the soil.\n\nThink of roots as the plant's underground support system.\n\nSTEM\n\nThe stem helps hold the plant upright.\n\nIt also helps move water and other materials through the plant.\n\nSome stems are soft and green.\n\nTree trunks are also stems.\n\nLEAVES \ud83c\udf43\n\nLeaves help the plant make food using light energy.\n\nMany leaves are green.\n\nLeaves also exchange gases with the air.\n\nFLOWERS \ud83c\udf38\n\nMany plants produce flowers.\n\nFlowers can help plants make seeds.\n\nNot every plant has obvious flowers, but many familiar plants do.",null,null,!1)
B.b0z=new A.a6("VISUAL 2 \u2014 PARTS OF A PLANT","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-2-2","Different plant parts have different jobs.",!1)
B.aZK=new A.a6("SECTION 3 \u2014 WHAT DO PLANTS NEED? \u2600\ufe0f\ud83d\udca7","",B.e,"cyan","Plants need resources to live and grow.\n\nMany plants need:\n\n\u2600\ufe0f light\n\n\ud83d\udca7 water\n\n\ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f air\n\n\ud83c\udf31 nutrients\n\n\ud83c\udfe1 enough space and a suitable place to grow\n\nPlants use light energy to help make sugars they can use for energy and growth.\n\nRoots take in water and minerals.\n\nLeaves help the plant capture light.\n\nEXAMPLE \ud83c\udf3b\n\nImagine a sunflower growing outside.\n\nIt receives light from the Sun.\n\nIts roots take in water.\n\nIts leaves capture light.\n\nIts stem supports the plant.\n\nTogether, these parts help the sunflower live and grow.",null,null,!1)
B.b0x=new A.a6("SECTION 4 \u2014 WHAT IS AN ANIMAL? \ud83d\udc3e","",B.e,"cyan","An animal is also a living thing.\n\nAnimals come in many shapes and sizes.\n\nExamples include:\n\n\ud83d\udc15 Dog\n\ud83d\udc08 Cat\n\ud83d\udc26 Bird\n\ud83d\udc1f Fish\n\ud83e\udd8b Butterfly\n\ud83d\udc18 Elephant\n\ud83d\udc38 Frog\n\nAnimals usually move from place to place during at least part of their lives.\n\nAnimals get energy by eating food.\n\nDifferent animals eat different things.\n\nSome eat plants.\n\nSome eat other animals.\n\nSome eat both.",null,null,!1)
B.aYM=new A.a6("VISUAL 3 \u2014 MANY KINDS OF ANIMALS","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-2-3","Animals can look very different, but they are all living things.",!1)
B.b_t=new A.a6("SECTION 5 \u2014 ANIMAL BODY PARTS \ud83d\udc15","",B.e,"cyan","Animals have body parts that help them survive.\n\nDifferent animals have different parts.\n\nLEGS \ud83d\udc3e\n\nMany animals use legs to:\n\n\u2022 walk;\n\u2022 run;\n\u2022 jump;\n\u2022 climb.\n\nDogs, cats, frogs, and many other animals have legs.\n\nWINGS \ud83e\udebd\n\nMany birds and insects have wings.\n\nWings can help some animals fly.\n\nFINS \ud83d\udc1f\n\nFish use fins to help them move and steer through water.\n\nEYES \ud83d\udc40\n\nEyes help many animals see their surroundings.\n\nEARS \ud83d\udc42\n\nEars help many animals detect sounds.\n\nNot every animal has ears that look like ours.\n\nMOUTHS OR BEAKS\n\nAnimals use mouths or beaks to take in food.\n\nA bird may use its beak.\n\nA dog uses its mouth.",null,null,!1)
B.aZ_=new A.a6("SECTION 6 \u2014 DIFFERENT PARTS FOR DIFFERENT ANIMALS \ud83d\udc1f\ud83e\udd85\ud83d\udc38","",B.e,"cyan","Animal bodies are not all the same.\n\nDifferent body parts can help animals live in different places.\n\nA fish has fins that help it swim. \ud83d\udc1f\n\nA bird has wings that may help it fly. \ud83d\udc26\n\nA frog has strong back legs that help it jump. \ud83d\udc38\n\nA duck has webbed feet that help it move through water. \ud83e\udd86\n\n\ud83d\udca1 IMPORTANT:\n\nAnimals do not need to have the same body parts to be animals.\n\nDifferent animals have different structures that help them survive.",null,null,!1)
B.aYV=new A.a6("VISUAL 4 \u2014 ANIMAL PARTS AND THEIR JOBS","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-2-4","Animal body parts help animals move and survive.",!1)
B.aZ1=new A.a6("SECTION 7 \u2014 WHAT DO ANIMALS NEED? \ud83d\udca7\ud83c\udf4e","",B.e,"cyan",'Animals need resources too.\n\nMost animals need:\n\n\ud83d\udca7 water\n\n\ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f air\n\n\ud83c\udf4e food\n\n\ud83c\udfe1 shelter or a safe place\n\n\ud83c\udf3f enough space to live\n\nDifferent animals need different kinds of food and habitats.\n\nA rabbit may eat plants. \ud83d\udc07\n\nA lion eats other animals. \ud83e\udd81\n\nA bear may eat both plants and animals. \ud83d\udc3b\n\nFor Grade 1, remember:\n\n"Animals get energy by eating food."',null,null,!1)
B.b_2=new A.a6("SECTION 8 \u2014 PLANTS AND ANIMALS ARE ALIKE \ud83e\udd1d","",B.e,"cyan","Plants and animals are different, but they also have things in common.\n\nBoth are living things.\n\nBoth:\n\n\ud83d\udca7 need water;\n\n\u26a1 need energy;\n\n\ud83c\udf31 grow and change;\n\n\ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f interact with their environment;\n\n\ud83d\udc23 have life cycles.\n\nThis means a tree and a dog may look very different, but both show signs of life.",null,null,!1)
B.b_A=new A.a6("VISUAL 5 \u2014 PLANTS AND ANIMALS: WHAT THEY SHARE","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-2-5","",!1)
B.aZw=new A.a6("SECTION 9 \u2014 HOW ARE PLANTS AND ANIMALS DIFFERENT? \ud83c\udf3f VS \ud83d\udc15","",B.e,"cyan","Plants and animals have some important differences.\n\nPLANTS\n\nMost plants stay rooted in one place.\n\nPlants use light energy to help make their own food.\n\nPlants often have:\n\n\ud83c\udf3f roots\n\ud83c\udf31 stems\n\ud83c\udf43 leaves\n\ud83c\udf38 flowers\n\nANIMALS\n\nAnimals get energy by eating food.\n\nMany animals can move from place to place.\n\nAnimals can have:\n\n\ud83d\udc3e legs\n\ud83e\udebd wings\n\ud83d\udc1f fins\n\ud83d\udc40 eyes\n\ud83d\udc42 ears\n\ud83e\uddb7 teeth or beaks\n\nDifferent animals may have different body parts.",null,null,!1)
B.b_R=new A.a6("SECTION 10 \u2014 WHERE DO PLANTS AND ANIMALS LIVE? \ud83c\udfe1","",B.e,"cyan","A habitat is a place where a living thing lives and gets what it needs.\n\nDifferent living things live in different habitats.\n\nFOREST \ud83c\udf33\n\nForests may contain:\n\n\ud83c\udf33 trees\n\ud83d\udc26 birds\n\ud83e\udd8c deer\n\ud83d\udc3f\ufe0f squirrels\n\nPOND OR LAKE \ud83d\udca7\n\nFreshwater habitats may contain:\n\n\ud83d\udc1f fish\n\ud83d\udc38 frogs\n\ud83c\udf3f water plants\n\nGRASSLAND \ud83c\udf3e\n\nGrasslands may contain:\n\n\ud83c\udf3e grasses\n\ud83d\udc07 rabbits\n\ud83e\udd8b insects\n\ud83d\udc26 birds\n\nGARDEN \ud83c\udf3b\n\nGardens may contain:\n\n\ud83c\udf3b flowers\n\ud83c\udf3f vegetables\n\ud83d\udc1d bees\n\ud83e\udd8b butterflies\n\nA habitat provides resources that living things need.",null,null,!1)
B.aZ5=new A.a6("VISUAL 6 \u2014 MATCH THE HABITAT","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-2-6","Living things live where they can find the resources they need.",!1)
B.aYs=new A.a6("SECTION 11 \u2014 STEP-BY-STEP: IS IT A PLANT OR AN ANIMAL? \ud83e\udde0","",B.e,"cyan","Suppose we see a sunflower.\n\nHow can we classify it?\n\nSTEP 1:\nLook at its parts.\n\nIt has roots, a stem, leaves, and a flower.\n\nSTEP 2:\nAsk how it gets energy.\n\nIt uses light energy to help make food.\n\nSTEP 3:\nAsk whether it is rooted in place.\n\nYes.\n\nCONCLUSION:\n\n\ud83c\udf3b A sunflower is a plant.\n\n\nNow think about a rabbit.\n\nSTEP 1:\nLook at its body.\n\nIt has legs, eyes, ears, and a mouth.\n\nSTEP 2:\nAsk how it gets energy.\n\nIt eats food.\n\nSTEP 3:\nAsk how it moves.\n\nIt can hop and move from place to place.\n\nCONCLUSION:\n\n\ud83d\udc07 A rabbit is an animal.",null,null,!1)
B.aZq=new A.a6("WORKED EXAMPLE \u2014 PLANT OR ANIMAL? \ud83e\udd8b","",B.e,"cyan","We see a butterfly.\n\nLet's investigate.\n\nSTEP 1:\nDoes it have roots and leaves?\n\nNo.\n\nSTEP 2:\nDoes it eat or take in food?\n\nYes.\n\nSTEP 3:\nCan it move from place to place?\n\nYes.\n\nSTEP 4:\nDoes it have animal body parts?\n\nYes.\n\nIt has wings, legs, eyes, and other animal structures.\n\nCONCLUSION:\n\n\ud83e\udd8b A butterfly is an animal.",null,null,!1)
B.aZd=new A.a6("GUIDED EXAMPLE \u2014 WHAT DOES THIS PLANT PART DO? \ud83c\udf43","",B.e,"cyan","Suppose we look at a leaf.\n\nWhat is one important job of a leaf?\n\nA leaf helps the plant capture light.\n\nPlants use light energy to help make food.\n\nSo:\n\n\ud83c\udf43 Leaf \u2192 helps capture light for the plant.",null,null,!1)
B.b0f=new A.a6("SECTION 12 \u2014 BABY AND ADULT ANIMALS \ud83d\udc23\u27a1\ufe0f\ud83d\udc14","",B.e,"cyan","Animals grow and change during their lives.\n\nA baby animal may look similar to its adult form.\n\nA puppy grows into an adult dog. \ud83d\udc36\u27a1\ufe0f\ud83d\udc15\n\nA kitten grows into an adult cat. \ud83d\udc31\u27a1\ufe0f\ud83d\udc08\n\nSome animals change more dramatically.\n\nA caterpillar can become a butterfly. \ud83d\udc1b\u27a1\ufe0f\ud83e\udd8b\n\nA tadpole can grow into a frog. \ud83d\udc38\n\nThese changes are part of an animal's life cycle.",null,null,!1)
B.aYZ=new A.a6("SECTION 13 \u2014 PLANTS HAVE LIFE CYCLES TOO \ud83c\udf31","",B.e,"cyan","Plants also have life cycles.\n\nA simple flowering plant life cycle may look like:\n\nSeed \ud83c\udf30\n\u2193\n\nSprout \ud83c\udf31\n\u2193\n\nYoung Plant \ud83e\udeb4\n\u2193\n\nAdult Plant \ud83c\udf3b\n\u2193\n\nSeeds\n\nThen the cycle can begin again.",null,null,!1)
B.b0k=new A.a6("VISUAL 7 \u2014 TWO SIMPLE LIFE CYCLES","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-2-7","Plants and animals grow and change during their life cycles.",!1)
B.aZe=new A.a6("SECTION 14 \u2014 HOW PLANTS AND ANIMALS CAN HELP EACH OTHER \ud83c\udf3b\ud83d\udc1d","",B.e,"cyan",'Plants and animals often share the same environment.\n\nSometimes they can help each other.\n\nFor example:\n\n\ud83d\udc1d A bee may visit a flower to collect nectar.\n\n\ud83c\udf38 While visiting flowers, bees can help move pollen from flower to flower.\n\n\ud83d\udc26 Some animals eat fruits and can carry seeds to new places.\n\n\ud83c\udf33 Plants can provide food and shelter for animals.\n\nLiving things are connected to their environments and to other living things.\n\nFor Grade 1, remember:\n\n"Plants and animals can depend on each other."',null,null,!1)
B.aZZ=new A.a6("COMMON MISTAKES \u26a0\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan",'MISTAKE 1:\n\n"Plants are not living because they do not walk."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nPlants grow, use resources, respond, and have life cycles.\n\n\nMISTAKE 2:\n\n"All animals have four legs."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nBirds, fish, insects, snakes, and many other animals have different body structures.\n\n\nMISTAKE 3:\n\n"Plants get food by eating like animals."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nPlants use light energy, water, and gases to help make sugars they can use.\n\n\nMISTAKE 4:\n\n"Every animal lives on land."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nFish and many other animals live in water.\n\n\nMISTAKE 5:\n\n"Every plant has a flower."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nMany plants have flowers, but not all plants produce obvious flowers.',null,null,!1)
B.b01=new A.a6("ACTIVITY \u2014 PLANT OR ANIMAL? \ud83c\udf3f\ud83d\udc3e","",B.e,"cyan",'Look at each example.\n\n\ud83c\udf3b Sunflower\n\ud83d\udc15 Dog\n\ud83c\udf33 Tree\n\ud83d\udc1f Fish\n\ud83c\udf35 Cactus\n\ud83e\udd8b Butterfly\n\nSort them.\n\nPLANTS \ud83c\udf3f\n\n\ud83c\udf3b Sunflower\n\ud83c\udf33 Tree\n\ud83c\udf35 Cactus\n\nANIMALS \ud83d\udc3e\n\n\ud83d\udc15 Dog\n\ud83d\udc1f Fish\n\ud83e\udd8b Butterfly\n\nNow ask:\n\n"What clues helped you decide?"\n\nGood clues include:\n\n\u2022 plant parts;\n\u2022 animal body parts;\n\u2022 how the organism gets energy;\n\u2022 how it moves;\n\u2022 where it lives.',null,null,!1)
B.aZV=new A.a6("Picture guide","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-2-sort",null,!1)
B.aYu=new A.a6("ACTIVITY \u2014 MATCH THE PART TO ITS JOB \ud83d\udd0e","",B.e,"cyan","ROOTS \ud83c\udf3f\n\u2192 take in water and help hold a plant in place\n\nLEAVES \ud83c\udf43\n\u2192 help capture light\n\nSTEM \ud83c\udf31\n\u2192 supports the plant and helps move materials\n\nFINS \ud83d\udc1f\n\u2192 help a fish swim\n\nWINGS \ud83e\udebd\n\u2192 help many birds and insects move through the air\n\nLEGS \ud83d\udc3e\n\u2192 help many animals walk, run, jump, or climb",null,null,!1)
B.b0E=new A.a6("QUICK CHECK \ud83e\udde0","",B.e,"cyan","1. SUNFLOWER \ud83c\udf3b\n\nPlant or animal?\n\nAnswer:\nPlant\n\nWhy?\nIt has plant structures such as roots, a stem, leaves, and a flower.\n\n\n2. FISH \ud83d\udc1f\n\nPlant or animal?\n\nAnswer:\nAnimal\n\nWhy?\nIt eats food and has animal body structures such as fins.\n\n\n3. ROOT \ud83c\udf3f\n\nWhat kind of living thing has roots?\n\nAnswer:\nPlant\n\nWhy?\nRoots are important plant structures.\n\n\n4. WING \ud83e\udebd\n\nWhich group often has animals with wings?\n\nAnswer:\nAnimals\n\nWhy?\nBirds and many insects use wings.\n\n\n5. DO PLANTS AND ANIMALS BOTH NEED WATER? \ud83d\udca7\n\nAnswer:\nYes\n\nWhy?\nWater is an important resource for both plants and animals.",null,null,!0)
B.aYF=new A.a6("KEY CONCEPT \u2b50","",B.e,"cyan","TITLE:\n\nPlants and animals are different kinds of living things.\n\nBODY:\n\nPlants and animals both need resources, grow, respond to their environments, and have life cycles.\n\nPlants often have roots, stems, and leaves and use light to help make food.\n\nAnimals eat food and have body parts that help them move, sense, and survive.",null,null,!1)
B.b_7=new A.a6("FINAL RECAP \ud83c\udf1f","",B.e,"cyan","Remember:\n\n\ud83c\udf3f Plants are living things.\n\n\ud83d\udc3e Animals are living things.\n\n\ud83d\udca7 Both need resources such as water.\n\n\ud83c\udf31 Both grow and change.\n\n\ud83c\udf43 Plants have parts such as roots, stems, and leaves.\n\n\ud83d\udc1f Animals can have parts such as legs, wings, fins, eyes, and ears.\n\n\u2600\ufe0f Plants use light energy to help make food.\n\n\ud83c\udf4e Animals get energy by eating food.\n\n\ud83c\udfe1 Plants and animals live in habitats where they can get what they need.\n\n\ud83d\udc23 Both have life cycles.",null,null,!1)
B.aqr=s([B.b_s,B.b06,B.aZx,B.b_Y,B.b_n,B.b0z,B.aZK,B.b0x,B.aYM,B.b_t,B.aZ_,B.aYV,B.aZ1,B.b_2,B.b_A,B.aZw,B.b_R,B.aZ5,B.aYs,B.aZq,B.aZd,B.b0f,B.aYZ,B.b0k,B.aZe,B.aZZ,B.b01,B.aZV,B.aYu,B.b0E,B.aYF,B.b_7],t.u)
B.aYl=new A.hD("Plants & Animals \ud83c\udf3f\ud83d\udc3e","Discover how plants and animals are alike, how they are different, and what they need to live.\n20\u201325 minutes",B.aqr,"","",50)
B.b0J=new A.a6("LEARNING GOALS \ud83c\udfaf","",B.e,"cyan","By the end of this lesson, the learner should be able to:\n\n\u2022 Identify common living and nonliving things.\n\u2022 Describe simple signs that show something is living.\n\u2022 Explain that living things need resources such as water and energy.\n\u2022 Recognize that living things grow and change.\n\u2022 Use evidence to decide whether something is living or nonliving.",null,null,!1)
B.aZY=new A.a6("OPENING \u2014 LOOK AROUND YOU \ud83d\udc40","",B.e,"cyan","Look around you.\n\nYou might see a person, a plant, a pet, a chair, a pencil, a rock, or a toy.\n\nSome of these are living things.\nOthers are nonliving things.\n\nA dog is living. \ud83d\udc15\nA tree is living. \ud83c\udf33\nYou are living. \ud83e\uddd2\n\nA rock is nonliving. \ud83e\udea8\nA chair is nonliving. \ud83e\ude91\nA toy car is nonliving. \ud83d\ude97\n\nSometimes the answer seems easy.\n\nBut what about a seed that is not moving?\nWhat about a robot that can walk?\nWhat about a wooden table that came from a tree?\n\nTo answer correctly, we need to look for signs of life.",null,null,!1)
B.aZR=new A.a6("SECTION 1 \u2014 WHAT IS A LIVING THING? \ud83c\udf31","",B.e,"cyan","A living thing is something that carries out the processes of life.\n\nPeople, animals, and plants are living things.\n\nLiving things have several important characteristics.\n\nLiving things:\n\n\ud83d\udca7 need resources such as water;\n\u26a1 need energy;\n\ud83c\udf31 grow and change;\n\ud83d\udc40 respond to things around them;\n\ud83d\udc23 come from other living things of their kind.\n\nWe should not use only one clue.\n\nInstead, we look for several signs of life together.\n\nEXAMPLE: A PUPPY \ud83d\udc36\n\nThink about a puppy.\n\nA puppy drinks water.\n\nIt eats food to get energy.\n\nIt grows bigger.\n\nIt reacts when it hears a sound.\n\nIt grows into an adult dog.\n\nThese are signs that the puppy is living.",null,null,!1)
B.b0g=new A.a6("VISUAL 1 \u2014 LIVING OR NONLIVING?","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-1-1","Living things show signs of life. Nonliving things do not.",!1)
B.aZ8=new A.a6("SECTION 2 \u2014 LIVING THINGS NEED RESOURCES \ud83d\udca7","",B.e,"cyan",'Living things need things from their surroundings in order to live.\n\nDifferent living things have different needs, but many need water, air, energy, and a suitable place to live.\n\nWATER \ud83d\udca7\n\nPeople need water.\n\nAnimals need water.\n\nPlants need water too.\n\nWithout enough water, living things may not stay healthy.\n\nAIR \ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f\n\nPeople and many animals take oxygen from the air.\n\nPlants also exchange gases with their surroundings.\n\nAir is part of the environment that supports life.\n\nENERGY \u26a1\n\nLiving things need energy.\n\nAnimals get energy from food. \ud83c\udf4e\n\nPlants use light energy to make sugars they can use. \u2600\ufe0f\ud83c\udf31\n\nFor Grade 1, remember:\n\n"Animals eat food. Plants use light to help make their food."\n\nA PLACE TO LIVE \ud83c\udfe1\n\nLiving things need a place where their needs can be met.\n\nA fish lives in water. \ud83d\udc1f\n\nA bird may live in a nest. \ud83d\udc26\n\nA cactus can live in a dry place. \ud83c\udf35\n\nDifferent living things can live in different environments.',null,null,!1)
B.aZP=new A.a6("VISUAL 2 \u2014 WHAT LIVING THINGS NEED","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-1-2","Living things depend on resources from their environment.",!1)
B.aYS=new A.a6("SECTION 3 \u2014 LIVING THINGS GROW AND CHANGE \ud83c\udf31\u27a1\ufe0f\ud83c\udf3b","",B.e,"cyan","Living things do not stay exactly the same throughout their lives.\n\nThey grow and develop.\n\nThink about a plant.\n\nIt may begin as a seed.\n\nSTEP 1:\nA seed begins to germinate when conditions are suitable.\n\nSTEP 2:\nA tiny root and shoot begin to grow.\n\nSTEP 3:\nLeaves begin to develop.\n\nSTEP 4:\nThe plant becomes larger and more mature.\n\nA living thing grows because of processes happening inside its body.\n\nA PERSON GROWS TOO \ud83e\uddd2\u27a1\ufe0f\ud83e\uddd1\n\nA baby grows into a child.\n\nA child grows into an adult.\n\nThe person remains the same living organism while growing and developing.",null,null,!1)
B.b_K=new A.a6("VISUAL 3 \u2014 PLANT GROWTH SEQUENCE","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-1-3","Living things grow and change during their lives.",!1)
B.aYL=new A.a6("SECTION 4 \u2014 LIVING THINGS RESPOND \ud83d\udc42\ud83d\udc40","",B.e,"cyan",'Living things can respond to changes around them.\n\nThis means they can react to their environment.\n\nANIMALS \ud83d\udc15\n\nA dog may turn its head when it hears its name.\n\nA bird may fly away when it senses danger.\n\nA person may pull a hand away from something very hot.\n\nThese are responses.\n\nPLANTS \ud83c\udf3b\n\nPlants respond too.\n\nTheir responses may be slower than an animal\'s movements.\n\nFor example, many plant shoots grow toward light.\n\nPlants do not need to walk or run to be alive.\n\n\ud83d\udca1 REMEMBER:\n\n"Not all living things move from place to place."',null,null,!1)
B.b_y=new A.a6("SECTION 5 \u2014 MOVEMENT DOES NOT ALWAYS MEAN LIFE \ud83d\ude97","",B.e,"cyan",'This is an important idea.\n\nA car can move.\n\nA fan can spin.\n\nA robot can walk.\n\nA cloud can move through the sky.\n\nBut these things are not living just because they move.\n\nA toy car moves because a motor or a person makes it move.\n\nA real dog moves because it is a living organism.\n\nSo this rule is wrong:\n\n\u274c "If it moves, it is alive."\n\nA better rule is:\n\n\u2705 "Look for several signs of life."',null,null,!1)
B.b_c=new A.a6("WORKED EXAMPLE \u2014 IS A TOY ROBOT ALIVE? \ud83e\udd16","",B.e,"cyan","A toy robot can walk and make sounds.\n\nDoes that make it alive?\n\nLet's check.\n\nSTEP 1:\nDoes the robot grow through biological growth?\n\nNo.\n\nSTEP 2:\nDoes the robot need food and water because it is alive?\n\nNo.\n\nSTEP 3:\nDoes it carry out life processes by itself?\n\nNo.\n\nSTEP 4:\nWhy does it move?\n\nIts machine parts, battery, or motor make it move.\n\nCONCLUSION:\n\nThe robot is nonliving.\n\n\u2b50 Movement alone does not prove that something is alive.",null,null,!1)
B.b_o=new A.a6("SECTION 6 \u2014 SOME LIVING THINGS LOOK STILL \ud83c\udf30","",B.e,"cyan",'Something does not have to be moving all the time to be alive.\n\nThink about a sleeping cat. \ud83d\udc08\ud83d\udca4\n\nThe cat may look very still.\n\nBut it is still breathing and using energy.\n\nIt is still alive.\n\nNow think about a seed. \ud83c\udf30\n\nA seed may look dry and still.\n\nBut under suitable conditions, it can germinate and grow into a plant.\n\nSo this rule is also wrong:\n\n\u274c "If it is not moving, it is not alive."',null,null,!1)
B.b_C=new A.a6("SECTION 7 \u2014 WHAT IS NONLIVING? \ud83e\udea8","",B.e,"cyan","A nonliving thing does not carry out the processes of life.\n\nExamples include:\n\n\ud83e\udea8 rocks\n\ud83d\udca7 water\n\ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f air\n\ud83e\ude91 chairs\n\u270f\ufe0f pencils\n\ud83e\uddf8 toys\n\ud83e\udd64 cups\n\ud83d\udeb2 bicycles\n\nNonliving things can still change.\n\nIce can melt. \ud83e\uddca\u27a1\ufe0f\ud83d\udca7\n\nA rock can break.\n\nMetal can rust.\n\nA balloon can get bigger when air is blown into it. \ud83c\udf88\n\nBut these changes are not biological growth.",null,null,!1)
B.b_O=new A.a6("SECTION 8 \u2014 THINGS THAT WERE ONCE LIVING \ud83c\udf33\u27a1\ufe0f\ud83e\ude91","",B.e,"cyan","Some nonliving things are made from materials that once came from living things.\n\nThink about wood.\n\nA tree is living. \ud83c\udf33\n\nA wooden table is not living. \ud83e\ude91\n\nThe wood came from a living tree, but the table does not carry out life processes.\n\nPaper is another example.\n\nPaper can be made from plant material.\n\nA sheet of paper is nonliving now. \ud83d\udcc4\n\nFor this lesson, remember:\n\nLIVING:\nalive now\n\nONCE LIVING:\ncame from something that was alive\n\nNEVER LIVING:\nwas never an organism\n\nBoth once-living and never-living objects are nonliving now.",null,null,!1)
B.b0w=new A.a6("GUIDED EXAMPLE \u2014 IS A TREE LIVING? \ud83c\udf33","",B.e,"cyan","Let's investigate.\n\nQUESTION 1:\nDoes a tree need resources?\n\nYes. \u2705\n\nIt needs water, gases from the air, light, and nutrients.\n\nQUESTION 2:\nDoes a tree grow?\n\nYes. \u2705\n\nA young tree can grow taller and develop more branches.\n\nQUESTION 3:\nDoes it respond to its surroundings?\n\nYes. \u2705\n\nPlants respond to environmental conditions such as light.\n\nQUESTION 4:\nDoes it have a life cycle?\n\nYes. \u2705\n\nNew plants can develop from seeds or through other reproductive processes.\n\nCONCLUSION:\n\n\ud83c\udf33 A tree is a living thing.",null,null,!1)
B.aZh=new A.a6("ACTIVITY \u2014 SORT THE OBJECTS \ud83e\udde0","",B.e,"cyan",'Look at these objects:\n\n\ud83d\udc08 Cat\n\ud83c\udf3b Sunflower\n\ud83e\udea8 Rock\n\ud83d\udeb2 Bicycle\n\ud83d\udc1f Fish\n\ud83e\uddf8 Teddy bear\n\nSort them.\n\nLIVING \ud83c\udf31\n\n\ud83d\udc08 Cat\n\ud83c\udf3b Sunflower\n\ud83d\udc1f Fish\n\nNONLIVING \ud83d\udce6\n\n\ud83e\udea8 Rock\n\ud83d\udeb2 Bicycle\n\ud83e\uddf8 Teddy bear\n\nNow ask:\n\n"What evidence helped you decide?"\n\nA strong answer should mention signs of life such as:\n\n\u2022 growth;\n\u2022 needing resources;\n\u2022 responding to surroundings;\n\u2022 having a life cycle.\n\nDo not use only:\n\n"It moves."',null,null,!1)
B.aZi=new A.a6("Picture guide","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-1-sort",null,!1)
B.aYX=new A.a6("Try it yourself: plant or toy?","",B.e,"cyan","Look at the sunflower and teddy bear in the picture guide.\n\nWhich is living? Tell someone two clues. You may point, talk, or draw.\n\nNow imagine a toy flower. It is green and a battery makes it turn. Is it living? Think before opening the answer.",null,null,!1)
B.b09=new A.a6("Reveal: use two clues","",B.e,"cyan","The sunflower is living. It needs water and grows into a larger plant. Those are two clues.\n\nThe teddy bear and toy flower are nonliving. They do not carry out life processes. A battery can make a toy move. Green color and movement do not prove life.",null,null,!0)
B.aZa=new A.a6("Check your explanation","",B.e,"cyan","Did you name the living thing?\n\nDid you give two signs of life?\n\nIf you only said, 'It moves,' try again. Look at the plant growth picture for another clue.",null,null,!1)
B.b_X=new A.a6("Remember tomorrow","",B.e,"cyan","Tomorrow, before opening the pictures, name one living thing and one nonliving thing. Tell two clues that helped you decide. Then open the picture guide to check.",null,null,!1)
B.b02=new A.a6("COMMON MISTAKES \u26a0\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan",'MISTAKE 1:\n\n"If something moves, it must be living."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nCars, fans, and robots can move but are nonliving.\n\n\nMISTAKE 2:\n\n"Plants are not living because they cannot walk."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nPlants grow, use resources, respond to their surroundings, and have life cycles.\n\n\nMISTAKE 3:\n\n"If something gets bigger, it must be growing."\n\n\u274c Not always.\n\nA balloon gets bigger when air is added.\n\nThat is not biological growth.\n\n\nMISTAKE 4:\n\n"If something is still, it is nonliving."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nA sleeping animal is still alive.\n\nA seed may also be alive even when it looks inactive.',null,null,!1)
B.b0h=new A.a6("QUICK CHECK \ud83e\udde0","",B.e,"cyan","Think about each item before revealing the answer.\n\n1. DOG \ud83d\udc15\nLiving or nonliving?\n\nAnswer:\nLiving\n\nWhy?\nIt grows, needs resources, responds, and has a life cycle.\n\n\n2. ROCK \ud83e\udea8\nLiving or nonliving?\n\nAnswer:\nNonliving\n\nWhy?\nIt does not carry out life processes.\n\n\n3. PLANT \ud83c\udf31\nLiving or nonliving?\n\nAnswer:\nLiving\n\nWhy?\nIt grows, needs resources, and responds to its environment.\n\n\n4. TOY CAR \ud83d\ude97\nLiving or nonliving?\n\nAnswer:\nNonliving\n\nWhy?\nMovement from a motor does not make it alive.",null,null,!0)
B.b_m=new A.a6("KEY CONCEPT \u2b50","",B.e,"cyan","TITLE:\n\nLiving things show signs of life.\n\nBODY:\n\nLiving things need resources, grow and develop, respond to their surroundings, and have life cycles.\n\nDo not decide whether something is living by using only one clue.\n\nLook for several signs of life together.",null,null,!1)
B.b_1=new A.a6("FINAL RECAP \ud83c\udf1f","",B.e,"cyan","Before leaving the lesson, remember:\n\n\ud83c\udf31 Plants and animals are living things.\n\n\ud83d\udca7 Living things need resources.\n\n\ud83d\udcc8 Living things grow and change.\n\n\ud83d\udc40 Living things respond to their surroundings.\n\n\ud83d\ude97 Something can move and still be nonliving.\n\n\ud83d\udca4 Something can look still and still be alive.\n\n\ud83e\udde0 Use several clues before deciding.",null,null,!1)
B.aqh=s([B.b0J,B.aZY,B.aZR,B.b0g,B.aZ8,B.aZP,B.aYS,B.b_K,B.aYL,B.b_y,B.b_c,B.b_o,B.b_C,B.b_O,B.b0w,B.aZh,B.aZi,B.aYX,B.b09,B.aZa,B.b_X,B.b02,B.b0h,B.b_m,B.b_1],t.u)
B.aYm=new A.hD("Living & Nonliving Things \ud83c\udf31","Learn how to tell whether something is living or nonliving by looking for signs of life.\n20\u201325 minutes",B.aqh,"","",50)
B.b0m=new A.a6("LEARNING GOALS \ud83c\udfaf","",B.e,"cyan","By the end of this lesson, the learner should be able to:\n\n\u2022 Name common materials such as wood, metal, plastic, glass, paper, fabric, and rubber.\n\u2022 Describe simple properties of materials.\n\u2022 Compare materials using words such as hard, soft, smooth, rough, flexible, and waterproof.\n\u2022 Explain that objects can be made from different materials.\n\u2022 Choose a suitable material for a simple purpose.\n\u2022 Understand that some materials can be reused or recycled.",null,null,!1)
B.b0F=new A.a6("OPENING \u2014 WHAT ARE THINGS MADE OF? \ud83d\udc40","",B.e,"cyan","Look around you.\n\nYou may see:\n\n\ud83e\ude91 a chair;\n\n\u270f\ufe0f a pencil;\n\n\ud83e\udd64 a cup;\n\n\ud83d\udcd8 a book;\n\n\ud83d\udc55 a shirt;\n\n\ud83e\ude9f a window;\n\n\ud83e\uddf8 a toy.\n\nThese objects are made from different materials.\n\nA material is what an object is made from.\n\nFor example:\n\nA wooden table may be made from wood. \ud83e\udeb5\n\nA spoon may be made from metal. \ud83e\udd44\n\nA raincoat may be made from waterproof fabric or plastic-like material. \ud83e\udde5\n\nA window may be made from glass. \ud83e\ude9f\n\nDifferent materials have different properties.\n\nThese properties help us decide which material is useful for a certain job.",null,null,!1)
B.b0n=new A.a6("SECTION 1 \u2014 WHAT IS A MATERIAL? \ud83e\uddf1","",B.e,"cyan",'A material is a substance used to make an object.\n\nOne object can be made from one material.\n\nAnother object can be made from several materials.\n\nFor example:\n\nA pencil may contain:\n\n\ud83e\udeb5 wood;\n\n\u270f\ufe0f graphite;\n\n\ud83c\udfa8 paint;\n\nand sometimes a rubber eraser.\n\nA shoe may contain:\n\n\ud83d\udc5f fabric;\n\nrubber;\n\nfoam;\n\nand other materials.\n\nSo when we study an object, we can ask:\n\n"What is it made of?"',null,null,!1)
B.b_l=new A.a6("VISUAL 1 \u2014 OBJECT AND MATERIAL","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-5-1","Objects are made from different materials.",!1)
B.aYD=new A.a6("SECTION 2 \u2014 WOOD \ud83e\udeb5","",B.e,"cyan","Wood comes from trees.\n\nWood can be used to make many objects.\n\nExamples:\n\n\ud83e\ude91 chairs;\n\n\ud83d\udeaa doors;\n\n\ud83d\udcda shelves;\n\n\u270f\ufe0f pencils;\n\n\ud83e\uddf8 some toys.\n\nWood is often:\n\n\u2022 hard;\n\u2022 strong;\n\u2022 easy to shape into useful objects.\n\nDifferent kinds of wood can feel and look different.\n\nSome wood is smooth.\n\nSome wood feels rough.",null,null,!1)
B.aZQ=new A.a6("SECTION 3 \u2014 METAL \ud83d\udd29","",B.e,"cyan","Metal is used in many everyday objects.\n\nExamples:\n\n\ud83e\udd44 spoons;\n\n\ud83c\udf74 forks;\n\n\ud83d\udd11 keys;\n\n\ud83d\udeb2 bicycles;\n\n\ud83d\udd27 tools.\n\nMany metals are:\n\n\u2022 hard;\n\u2022 strong;\n\u2022 durable.\n\nSome metals can also be shiny.\n\nSome metals can bend when enough force is used.\n\n\ud83d\udca1 REMEMBER:\n\nNot every metal object looks the same.\n\nDifferent metals can have different properties.",null,null,!1)
B.b_E=new A.a6("SECTION 4 \u2014 PLASTIC \ud83e\uddf4","",B.e,"cyan","Plastic is used to make many objects.\n\nExamples:\n\n\ud83e\uddf4 bottles;\n\n\ud83e\uddf8 toys;\n\n\ud83d\udce6 containers;\n\n\ud83e\udea3 buckets.\n\nPlastic can be made in many forms.\n\nSome plastic is:\n\n\u2022 hard;\n\nwhile other plastic is:\n\n\u2022 flexible.\n\nPlastic can also be:\n\n\u2022 lightweight;\n\u2022 waterproof;\n\u2022 easy to shape.\n\nBecause plastic lasts a long time, we should use and dispose of it carefully.",null,null,!1)
B.aZj=new A.a6("SECTION 5 \u2014 GLASS \ud83e\ude9f","",B.e,"cyan","Glass is often used when we want light to pass through an object.\n\nExamples:\n\n\ud83e\ude9f windows;\n\n\ud83e\udd5b drinking glasses;\n\n\ud83e\uded9 jars.\n\nMany types of glass are:\n\n\u2022 hard;\n\u2022 smooth;\n\u2022 transparent.\n\nTransparent means light can pass through so we can see through it.\n\nBut glass can break.\n\nBroken glass can be sharp and dangerous.\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f SAFETY:\n\nDo not pick up broken glass.\n\nTell a trusted adult.",null,null,!1)
B.aYK=new A.a6("SECTION 6 \u2014 PAPER \ud83d\udcc4","",B.e,"cyan","Paper is used for:\n\n\ud83d\udcda books;\n\n\ud83d\udcdd notebooks;\n\n\ud83d\udcf0 newspapers;\n\n\ud83c\udf81 wrapping;\n\n\ud83c\udfa8 artwork.\n\nPaper is often:\n\n\u2022 light;\n\u2022 thin;\n\u2022 easy to fold;\n\u2022 easy to write or draw on.\n\nPaper is not usually a good material for holding water because it can become wet and weak.",null,null,!1)
B.b_p=new A.a6("SECTION 7 \u2014 FABRIC \ud83d\udc55","",B.e,"cyan","Fabric is used to make many things we wear and use.\n\nExamples:\n\n\ud83d\udc55 shirts;\n\n\ud83d\udc56 pants;\n\n\ud83d\udecf\ufe0f blankets;\n\n\ud83e\udde6 socks;\n\n\ud83c\udf92 some bags.\n\nDifferent fabrics can have different properties.\n\nSome are:\n\n\u2022 soft;\n\u2022 smooth;\n\u2022 thick;\n\u2022 thin;\n\u2022 stretchy.\n\nThe type of fabric depends on what the object needs to do.",null,null,!1)
B.b0I=new A.a6("SECTION 8 \u2014 RUBBER \ud83d\udfe0","",B.e,"cyan","Rubber is used in objects that may need to bend, stretch, grip, or bounce.\n\nExamples:\n\n\ud83c\udfc0 balls;\n\n\ud83d\udeb2 tires;\n\n\u270f\ufe0f erasers;\n\n\ud83e\udde4 rubber gloves;\n\n\ud83d\udc62 some boots.\n\nRubber can be:\n\n\u2022 flexible;\n\u2022 stretchy;\n\u2022 waterproof;\n\u2022 grippy.\n\nThese properties make rubber useful for many jobs.",null,null,!1)
B.b04=new A.a6("VISUAL 2 \u2014 COMMON MATERIALS","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-5-2","Different materials are useful for different objects.",!1)
B.b0q=new A.a6("SECTION 9 \u2014 WHAT IS A PROPERTY? \ud83d\udd0d","",B.e,"cyan","A property is something we can observe or describe about a material.\n\nWe can use our senses carefully to learn about materials.\n\nWe might ask:\n\nIs it hard or soft?\n\nIs it smooth or rough?\n\nIs it stiff or flexible?\n\nCan we see through it?\n\nDoes it soak up water?\n\nCan it stretch?\n\nThese observations help us compare materials.",null,null,!1)
B.b_J=new A.a6("SECTION 10 \u2014 HARD AND SOFT \ud83e\uddf1\ud83e\uddf8","",B.e,"cyan","Some materials feel hard.\n\nExamples:\n\n\ud83d\udd29 metal;\n\n\ud83e\udeb5 wood;\n\n\ud83e\ude9f glass.\n\nOther materials can feel soft.\n\nExamples:\n\n\ud83e\uddf8 soft fabric;\n\n\ud83d\udecf\ufe0f a blanket;\n\n\ud83e\uddfd foam.\n\nEXAMPLE\n\nA metal spoon is hard.\n\nA soft blanket is soft.\n\nHard and soft are properties we can use to describe materials.",null,null,!1)
B.b05=new A.a6("SECTION 11 \u2014 SMOOTH AND ROUGH \u270b","",B.e,"cyan","Some surfaces feel smooth.\n\nOthers feel rough.\n\nA glass window may feel smooth.\n\nA rough piece of wood may feel uneven.\n\nTouch can help us notice texture.\n\nTexture means how a surface feels.\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f SAFETY:\n\nOnly touch objects that are safe to handle.\n\nDo not touch sharp, hot, or broken materials.",null,null,!1)
B.b0C=new A.a6("SECTION 12 \u2014 FLEXIBLE AND STIFF \u21aa\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan","A flexible material can bend without breaking easily.\n\nA stiff material does not bend easily.\n\nExamples:\n\nA rubber band is flexible.\n\nA metal spoon is usually much stiffer.\n\nFabric can also be flexible.\n\nSome plastic can bend too.\n\nDifferent objects need different amounts of flexibility.",null,null,!1)
B.aYG=new A.a6("SECTION 13 \u2014 WATERPROOF AND ABSORBENT \ud83d\udca7","",B.e,"cyan","Some materials resist water.\n\nWe call these waterproof or water-resistant materials.\n\nExamples can include:\n\n\ud83e\uddf4 some plastics;\n\n\ud83d\udfe0 rubber;\n\n\ud83e\udde5 waterproof fabric.\n\nOther materials absorb water.\n\nAbsorb means to take in liquid.\n\nExamples:\n\n\ud83e\uddfd sponge;\n\n\ud83e\uddfb paper towel;\n\nsome fabrics.\n\nEXAMPLE\n\nImagine making an umbrella.\n\nWould paper be a good material?\n\nProbably not.\n\nPaper can absorb water and become weak.\n\nA waterproof material is a better choice.",null,null,!1)
B.aZu=new A.a6("VISUAL 3 \u2014 MATERIAL PROPERTIES","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-5-3","Properties help us describe and compare materials.",!1)
B.b0A=new A.a6("SECTION 14 \u2014 TRANSPARENT AND OPAQUE \ud83d\udc40","",B.e,"cyan","Some materials let light pass through.\n\nIf we can see through a material clearly, we call it transparent.\n\nGlass used in many windows is transparent.\n\nOther materials block our view.\n\nWe can call these opaque.\n\nWood is usually opaque.\n\nMetal is usually opaque.\n\nPaper is usually opaque.\n\nEXAMPLE\n\nWhy is clear glass useful for a window?\n\nBecause we can see through it and light can pass through.",null,null,!1)
B.aZl=new A.a6("TRANSPARENT AND OPAQUE \u2014 LIGHT AND VIEW","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-5-5","Clear glass lets light and our view pass through. Wood blocks our view.",!1)
B.b_F=new A.a6("SECTION 15 \u2014 ONE OBJECT, DIFFERENT MATERIALS \ud83d\udd04","",B.e,"cyan","The same type of object can sometimes be made from different materials.\n\nThink about a chair.\n\nA chair can be made from:\n\n\ud83e\udeb5 wood;\n\n\ud83d\udd29 metal;\n\n\ud83e\uddf4 plastic.\n\nThe material may change how the chair feels, looks, weighs, or how long it lasts.\n\nThink about a cup.\n\nA cup may be made from:\n\n\ud83e\ude9f glass;\n\n\ud83e\uddf4 plastic;\n\n\ud83d\udd29 metal;\n\nor other safe materials.\n\nThis shows us that there can be more than one suitable material for an object.",null,null,!1)
B.b0_=new A.a6("SECTION 16 \u2014 ONE OBJECT CAN USE MANY MATERIALS \ud83e\udde9","",B.e,"cyan","Some objects are made from several materials.\n\nThink about a bicycle. \ud83d\udeb2\n\nIt may have:\n\n\ud83d\udd29 a metal frame;\n\n\ud83d\udfe0 rubber tires;\n\n\ud83e\uddf4 plastic parts;\n\n\ud83e\ude91 fabric or foam on the seat.\n\nEach material is chosen because it has useful properties.\n\nThis is called choosing materials for a purpose.",null,null,!1)
B.aZH=new A.a6("ONE BICYCLE, MANY MATERIALS","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-5-6","Each part uses materials with useful properties.",!1)
B.b_U=new A.a6("WORKED EXAMPLE \u2014 WHAT SHOULD A RAINCOAT BE MADE FROM? \ud83c\udf27\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan","Imagine we want to make a raincoat.\n\nWhat property do we need most?\n\nSTEP 1:\n\nThink about what a raincoat must do.\n\nIt should help keep water away from the person wearing it.\n\nSTEP 2:\n\nCompare materials.\n\nPaper:\ncan absorb water and become weak.\n\nWood:\nhard and uncomfortable to wear.\n\nGlass:\nhard and breakable.\n\nWaterproof fabric:\nflexible and resists water.\n\nSTEP 3:\n\nChoose the material that fits the job.\n\nCONCLUSION:\n\n\ud83e\udde5 A flexible waterproof material is a good choice for a raincoat.",null,null,!1)
B.aZ9=new A.a6("GUIDED EXAMPLE \u2014 WHAT SHOULD A WINDOW BE MADE FROM? \ud83e\ude9f","",B.e,"cyan","A window should let us see outside and allow light to pass through.\n\nWhich property is useful?\n\nTransparent.\n\nWhich material is commonly transparent and suitable for windows?\n\nGlass.\n\nSo:\n\n\ud83e\ude9f Glass is useful for many windows because it can be transparent.",null,null,!1)
B.aZz=new A.a6("SECTION 17 \u2014 CHOOSING THE RIGHT MATERIAL \ud83c\udfaf","",B.e,"cyan",'We choose materials based on what an object needs to do.\n\nUMBRELLA \u2614\n\nUseful property:\n\nWaterproof\n\nBLANKET \ud83d\udecf\ufe0f\n\nUseful property:\n\nSoft\n\nWINDOW \ud83e\ude9f\n\nUseful property:\n\nTransparent\n\nBICYCLE TIRE \ud83d\udeb2\n\nUseful properties:\n\nFlexible and grippy\n\nSPOON \ud83e\udd44\n\nUseful properties:\n\nHard and strong\n\nThe "best" material depends on the job.',null,null,!1)
B.b0G=new A.a6("CHOOSING A MATERIAL FOR A PURPOSE","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-5-7","Choose a material whose properties fit the job.",!1)
B.b_w=new A.a6("SECTION 18 \u2014 CHANGING MATERIALS \u2702\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan","Materials can sometimes be changed.\n\nPaper can be:\n\nfolded;\n\ncut;\n\ntorn.\n\nClay can be:\n\npressed;\n\nrolled;\n\nshaped.\n\nFabric can be:\n\ncut;\n\nfolded;\n\nsewn.\n\nMetal can be shaped using special tools.\n\nChanging the shape of a material does not always create a new material.\n\nA folded sheet of paper is still paper.",null,null,!1)
B.b_h=new A.a6("SECTION 19 \u2014 REUSE \u267b\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan","Some objects can be used again instead of being thrown away.\n\nThis is called reuse.\n\nExamples:\n\n\ud83e\uded9 A clean jar may be reused for storage.\n\n\ud83d\udce6 A box may be reused to hold objects.\n\n\ud83c\udfa8 Clean safe materials may be reused for art projects.\n\nReusing objects can help reduce waste.\n\nAlways make sure reused objects are clean and safe.",null,null,!1)
B.b0s=new A.a6("SECTION 20 \u2014 RECYCLING \u267b\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan","Some materials can be collected and processed so they can be used again.\n\nThis is called recycling.\n\nMaterials that may be recyclable in some places include:\n\n\ud83d\udcc4 paper;\n\n\ud83e\uddf4 some plastics;\n\n\ud83d\udd29 some metals;\n\n\ud83e\ude9f some glass.\n\nRecycling rules are different in different places.\n\nAlways follow local recycling instructions and adult guidance.\n\nFor Grade 1, remember:\n\n\u267b\ufe0f Recycling can help turn some used materials into useful materials again.",null,null,!1)
B.aZo=new A.a6("VISUAL 4 \u2014 REDUCE, REUSE, RECYCLE","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-5-4","We can make careful choices about the materials we use.",!1)
B.aZI=new A.a6("SECTION 21 \u2014 MATERIAL SAFETY \u26a0\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan","Science means observing carefully.\n\nIt also means staying safe.\n\nDo not:\n\n\u274c touch broken glass;\n\n\u274c touch sharp metal;\n\n\u274c touch very hot objects;\n\n\u274c taste unknown materials;\n\n\u274c put unknown materials in your mouth.\n\nIf an object looks dangerous, ask a trusted adult for help.",null,null,!1)
B.aZD=new A.a6("ACTIVITY \u2014 WHAT MATERIAL IS IT? \ud83d\udd0e","",B.e,"cyan","Look at these objects.\n\n\nWOODEN TABLE \ud83e\udeb5\n\nMaterial:\nWood\n\n\nMETAL SPOON \ud83e\udd44\n\nMaterial:\nMetal\n\n\nGLASS WINDOW \ud83e\ude9f\n\nMaterial:\nGlass\n\n\nPAPER NOTEBOOK \ud83d\udcd8\n\nMaterial:\nPaper\n\n\nCOTTON SHIRT \ud83d\udc55\n\nMaterial:\nFabric\n\n\nRUBBER BALL \ud83c\udfc0\n\nMaterial:\nRubber",null,null,!1)
B.aYx=new A.a6("MATERIAL SORTING ACTIVITY","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-5-8","Match each pictured object to its material.",!1)
B.b_H=new A.a6("ACTIVITY \u2014 WHICH PROPERTY? \ud83e\udde0","",B.e,"cyan","A BLANKET\n\nUseful property:\nSoft \ud83e\uddf8\n\n\nA WINDOW\n\nUseful property:\nTransparent \ud83d\udc40\n\n\nA RAINCOAT\n\nUseful property:\nWaterproof \ud83d\udca7\n\n\nA RUBBER BAND\n\nUseful property:\nFlexible \u21aa\ufe0f\n\n\nA METAL TOOL\n\nUseful property:\nStrong \ud83d\udd29",null,null,!1)
B.b_v=new A.a6("ACTIVITY \u2014 CHOOSE THE BETTER MATERIAL \ud83c\udfaf","",B.e,"cyan","You want to make something that keeps rain off your head.\n\nWhich is better?\n\nPaper or waterproof material?\n\nBetter choice:\nWaterproof material \ud83d\udca7\n\n\nYou want to make a clear window.\n\nWhich is better?\n\nTransparent glass or wood?\n\nBetter choice:\nTransparent glass \ud83e\ude9f\n\n\nYou want a soft blanket.\n\nWhich is better?\n\nSoft fabric or hard metal?\n\nBetter choice:\nSoft fabric \ud83d\udecf\ufe0f",null,null,!1)
B.b_D=new A.a6("COMMON MISTAKES \u26a0\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan",'MISTAKE 1:\n\n"Every object is made from only one material."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nMany objects contain several materials.\n\n\nMISTAKE 2:\n\n"Every plastic object has the same properties."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nSome plastics are hard.\n\nOthers are flexible.\n\n\nMISTAKE 3:\n\n"Glass is always the best material for everything."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nGlass is useful for some purposes, but it can break.\n\nDifferent jobs need different materials.\n\n\nMISTAKE 4:\n\n"Soft materials are weak and useless."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nSoft materials are useful for things such as clothing, blankets, cushions, and other objects.\n\n\nMISTAKE 5:\n\n"Any material can be used safely for any job."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nMaterials are chosen because their properties fit the purpose.',null,null,!1)
B.b_j=new A.a6("QUICK CHECK \ud83e\udde0","",B.e,"cyan","1. WHAT MATERIAL IS A METAL SPOON MADE FROM?\n\nAnswer:\nMetal \ud83d\udd29\n\nWhy?\nMetal is hard and strong, making it useful for many utensils.\n\n\n2. WHICH PROPERTY IS USEFUL FOR A WINDOW?\n\nAnswer:\nTransparent \ud83d\udc40\n\nWhy?\nA transparent material lets us see through it.\n\n\n3. WHICH MATERIAL IS OFTEN FLEXIBLE AND USED FOR TIRES?\n\nAnswer:\nRubber \ud83d\udfe0\n\nWhy?\nRubber can bend and provide grip.\n\n\n4. WHY IS PAPER NOT A GOOD MATERIAL FOR AN UMBRELLA?\n\nAnswer:\nIt can absorb water. \ud83d\udca7\n\nWhy?\nWet paper can become weak.\n\n\n5. CAN ONE OBJECT BE MADE FROM MORE THAN ONE MATERIAL?\n\nAnswer:\nYes \u2705\n\nWhy?\nMany objects combine different materials for different jobs.",null,null,!0)
B.b_u=new A.a6("KEY CONCEPT \u2b50","",B.e,"cyan","TITLE:\n\nDifferent materials have different properties.\n\nBODY:\n\nObjects are made from materials such as wood, metal, plastic, glass, paper, fabric, and rubber.\n\nMaterials can be hard, soft, smooth, rough, flexible, waterproof, absorbent, transparent, or opaque.\n\nWe choose materials based on what we need an object to do.",null,null,!1)
B.b_Q=new A.a6("FINAL RECAP \ud83c\udf1f","",B.e,"cyan","Remember:\n\n\ud83e\udeb5 Wood is used for many strong objects.\n\n\ud83d\udd29 Metal is often hard and strong.\n\n\ud83e\uddf4 Plastic can be light, waterproof, hard, or flexible.\n\n\ud83e\ude9f Glass can be transparent but can break.\n\n\ud83d\udcc4 Paper is light and easy to fold.\n\n\ud83d\udc55 Fabric can be soft and flexible.\n\n\ud83d\udfe0 Rubber can be flexible and grippy.\n\n\ud83d\udd0e Properties help us compare materials.\n\n\ud83c\udfaf We choose materials based on their purpose.\n\n\u267b\ufe0f Some objects and materials can be reused or recycled.\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f Always handle materials safely.",null,null,!1)
B.ayY=s([B.b0m,B.b0F,B.b0n,B.b_l,B.aYD,B.aZQ,B.b_E,B.aZj,B.aYK,B.b_p,B.b0I,B.b04,B.b0q,B.b_J,B.b05,B.b0C,B.aYG,B.aZu,B.b0A,B.aZl,B.b_F,B.b0_,B.aZH,B.b_U,B.aZ9,B.aZz,B.b0G,B.b_w,B.b_h,B.b0s,B.aZo,B.aZI,B.aZD,B.aYx,B.b_H,B.b_v,B.b_D,B.b_j,B.b_u,B.b_Q],t.u)
B.aYn=new A.hD("Materials Around Us \ud83e\udeb5\ud83e\uddf1","Learn about common materials, their properties, and why different materials are used to make different objects.\n20\u201325 minutes",B.ayY,"","",50)
B.aYU=new A.a6("LEARNING GOALS \ud83c\udfaf","",B.e,"cyan","By the end of this lesson, the learner should be able to:\n\n\u2022 Describe common kinds of weather.\n\u2022 Identify sunny, cloudy, rainy, windy, and stormy conditions.\n\u2022 Recognize that weather can change from day to day.\n\u2022 Use simple observations to describe the weather.\n\u2022 Choose suitable clothing or activities for different weather.\n\u2022 Follow basic weather safety rules.",null,null,!1)
B.aZf=new A.a6("OPENING \u2014 WHAT IS THE WEATHER LIKE TODAY? \ud83d\udc40","",B.e,"cyan","Look outside.\n\nWhat do you notice?\n\nIs the Sun shining brightly? \u2600\ufe0f\n\nAre there many clouds? \u2601\ufe0f\n\nIs rain falling? \ud83c\udf27\ufe0f\n\nAre tree branches moving in the wind? \ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f\n\nWeather describes what the air and sky are like in a place at a certain time.\n\nWeather can be:\n\n\u2600\ufe0f sunny;\n\n\u2601\ufe0f cloudy;\n\n\ud83c\udf27\ufe0f rainy;\n\n\ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f windy;\n\n\u26c8\ufe0f stormy;\n\nor a mixture of different conditions.\n\nWeather can change.\n\nA morning may begin sunny and later become cloudy or rainy.\n\nThat is why we observe the weather regularly.",null,null,!1)
B.b0v=new A.a6("SECTION 1 \u2014 SUNNY WEATHER \u2600\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan","A sunny day usually has plenty of sunlight.\n\nThe sky may look bright and mostly clear.\n\nSunlight can make the day feel warm.\n\nOn a sunny day, people may:\n\n\ud83e\udde2 wear a hat;\n\n\ud83d\udca7 drink water;\n\n\ud83c\udf33 spend time in shaded areas;\n\n\ud83e\uddf4 use sun protection with adult guidance.\n\nEXAMPLE\n\nYou look outside and see:\n\n\u2022 a bright sky;\n\u2022 strong sunlight;\n\u2022 only a few clouds.\n\nYou could describe the weather as:\n\n\u2600\ufe0f Sunny",null,null,!1)
B.aZ3=new A.a6("VISUAL 1 \u2014 SUNNY WEATHER","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-4-1","Sunny weather has plenty of sunlight.",!1)
B.b0t=new A.a6("SECTION 2 \u2014 CLOUDY WEATHER \u2601\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan","Cloudy weather happens when many clouds cover part or much of the sky.\n\nClouds are made of tiny drops of water or ice high in the atmosphere.\n\nSome clouds are thin and white.\n\nOthers can look thick and gray.\n\nA cloudy day does not always mean it will rain.\n\nBut some clouds can bring rain.\n\nEXAMPLE\n\nYou look outside.\n\nThe Sun is hard to see because many clouds cover the sky.\n\nYou could describe the weather as:\n\n\u2601\ufe0f Cloudy",null,null,!1)
B.b0D=new A.a6("SECTION 3 \u2014 RAINY WEATHER \ud83c\udf27\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan","Rain happens when drops of water fall from clouds.\n\nRain gives water to plants and helps fill rivers, ponds, and other water sources.\n\nWhen it rains, people may use:\n\n\u2614 umbrellas;\n\n\ud83e\udde5 raincoats;\n\n\ud83e\udd7e boots.\n\nRoads and paths can become wet and slippery.\n\nWe should walk carefully.\n\nEXAMPLE\n\nYou hear drops hitting the roof.\n\nWater is falling from the sky.\n\nPeople are carrying umbrellas.\n\nThe weather is:\n\n\ud83c\udf27\ufe0f Rainy",null,null,!1)
B.aYE=new A.a6("VISUAL 2 \u2014 RAINY WEATHER","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-4-2","Rain is water that falls from clouds.",!1)
B.b_x=new A.a6("SECTION 4 \u2014 WINDY WEATHER \ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan","Wind is moving air.\n\nWe cannot usually see the air itself.\n\nBut we can see what the wind moves.\n\nOn a windy day, you might notice:\n\n\ud83c\udf43 leaves moving;\n\n\ud83c\udf33 branches swaying;\n\n\ud83d\udea9 flags waving;\n\n\ud83e\ude81 kites flying.\n\nA little wind can feel pleasant.\n\nVery strong winds can be dangerous.\n\nHOW CAN WE TELL IT IS WINDY?\n\nLook for things that are moving because of the air.\n\nFor example:\n\nA flag is flapping.\n\nLeaves are blowing across the ground.\n\nTree branches are moving.\n\nThese are clues that the weather is windy.",null,null,!1)
B.aYW=new A.a6("VISUAL 3 \u2014 HOW WE NOTICE WIND","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-4-3","We cannot see wind itself, but we can see what it moves.",!1)
B.aZ4=new A.a6("SECTION 5 \u2014 STORMY WEATHER \u26c8\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan","A storm may bring:\n\n\ud83c\udf27\ufe0f heavy rain;\n\n\ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f strong wind;\n\n\u26a1 lightning;\n\n\ud83c\udf29\ufe0f thunder;\n\ndark clouds.\n\nStormy weather can be dangerous.\n\nWhen there is thunder or lightning, children should follow instructions from trusted adults and stay in a safe place.\n\nIMPORTANT SAFETY RULE \u26a0\ufe0f\n\nIf you hear thunder:\n\nGo indoors or stay inside a safe building.\n\nDo not stay in an open field.\n\nDo not shelter under a lone tree.\n\nFollow adult instructions.",null,null,!1)
B.aZT=new A.a6("SECTION 6 \u2014 WEATHER CAN CHANGE \ud83d\udd04","",B.e,"cyan","Weather does not stay exactly the same every day.\n\nMonday may be sunny.\n\nTuesday may be rainy.\n\nWednesday may be cloudy.\n\nWeather can even change during the same day.\n\nFor example:\n\nMorning:\n\u2600\ufe0f Sunny\n\nAfternoon:\n\u2601\ufe0f Cloudy\n\nLater:\n\ud83c\udf27\ufe0f Rainy\n\nBecause weather can change, people observe it often.",null,null,!1)
B.b_I=new A.a6("VISUAL 4 \u2014 WEATHER CAN CHANGE","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-4-4","Weather can change during the day.",!1)
B.aZ2=new A.a6("SECTION 7 \u2014 HOW DO WE DESCRIBE WEATHER? \ud83d\udd0e","",B.e,"cyan","We can describe weather by observing different things.\n\nAsk:\n\nWhat does the sky look like?\n\nIs the Sun easy to see?\n\nAre clouds covering the sky?\n\nIs rain falling?\n\nIs the wind moving leaves or flags?\n\nDoes the air feel warm or cool?\n\nThese observations help us describe the weather.",null,null,!1)
B.b03=new A.a6("SECTION 8 \u2014 TEMPERATURE \ud83c\udf21\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan","Temperature tells us how warm or cool something is.\n\nWe often describe the weather as:\n\n\ud83d\udd25 hot;\n\n\ud83d\ude42 warm;\n\n\u2744\ufe0f cool;\n\n\ud83e\udd76 cold.\n\nA thermometer is a tool used to measure temperature.\n\nFor Grade 1, the important idea is:\n\n\ud83c\udf21\ufe0f A thermometer helps us measure how warm or cold the air is.",null,null,!1)
B.aYR=new A.a6("VISUAL 5 \u2014 HOT, WARM, COOL, COLD","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-4-5","Temperature tells us how warm or cold the air is.",!1)
B.b0d=new A.a6("SECTION 9 \u2014 WEATHER TOOLS \ud83e\uddf0","",B.e,"cyan",'People use tools to observe and measure weather.\n\nFor Grade 1, introduce these simple tools:\n\nTHERMOMETER \ud83c\udf21\ufe0f\n\nMeasures temperature.\n\nRAIN GAUGE \ud83c\udf27\ufe0f\n\nMeasures how much rain falls.\n\nWIND VANE \ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f\n\nShows the direction the wind is coming from.\n\nANEMOMETER \ud83d\udca8\n\nMeasures wind speed.\n\nDo not expect Grade 1 students to memorize every tool perfectly.\n\nThe main idea is:\n\n"Scientists use tools to measure weather."',null,null,!1)
B.b0r=new A.a6("VISUAL 6 \u2014 SIMPLE WEATHER TOOLS","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-4-6","",!1)
B.aZg=new A.a6("SECTION 10 \u2014 CLOUDS CAN GIVE US CLUES \u2601\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan","Clouds are an important part of weather.\n\nA few small white clouds may appear on a fair day.\n\nThicker gray clouds may bring rain.\n\nBut looking at clouds alone does not always tell us exactly what will happen.\n\nWeather can change.\n\nWe observe several clues together.\n\n\ud83d\udca1 REMEMBER:\n\nOne cloud does not always mean rain is coming.",null,null,!1)
B.aYY=new A.a6("SECTION 11 \u2014 WEATHER AND CLOTHING \ud83d\udc55","",B.e,"cyan","Weather can help us decide what clothing may be useful.\n\nSUNNY AND HOT \u2600\ufe0f\n\nYou might choose:\n\n\ud83d\udc55 light clothing;\n\n\ud83e\udde2 a hat;\n\n\ud83d\udca7 water.\n\nRAINY \ud83c\udf27\ufe0f\n\nYou might choose:\n\n\ud83e\udde5 raincoat;\n\n\u2614 umbrella;\n\n\ud83e\udd7e boots.\n\nCOOL OR COLD \u2744\ufe0f\n\nYou might choose:\n\n\ud83e\udde5 jacket;\n\n\ud83e\udde3 warm clothing.\n\nThe goal is to dress in a way that helps you stay comfortable and safe.",null,null,!1)
B.aYH=new A.a6("Picture guide","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-4-clothing",null,!1)
B.b_i=new A.a6("SECTION 12 \u2014 WEATHER AND ACTIVITIES \u26bd","",B.e,"cyan","Weather can affect what activities are suitable.\n\nA calm sunny day might be good for:\n\n\u26bd outdoor games;\n\n\ud83d\udeb2 cycling with proper safety equipment;\n\n\ud83c\udf33 visiting a park.\n\nA rainy day may be better for:\n\n\ud83d\udcda reading indoors;\n\n\ud83c\udfa8 drawing;\n\n\ud83e\udde9 puzzles.\n\nStormy weather may mean:\n\n\ud83c\udfe0 staying safely indoors.\n\nWe should always choose activities that fit the weather and follow adult safety instructions.",null,null,!1)
B.b_k=new A.a6("WORKED EXAMPLE \u2014 WHAT IS THE WEATHER? \ud83d\udd0e","",B.e,"cyan","Imagine you look outside.\n\nYou observe:\n\n\u2022 dark clouds;\n\u2022 water falling from the sky;\n\u2022 people using umbrellas;\n\u2022 puddles forming on the ground.\n\nLet's decide what kind of weather this is.\n\nSTEP 1:\nLook at the sky.\n\nThere are dark clouds.\n\nSTEP 2:\nLook for precipitation.\n\nWater is falling from the sky.\n\nSTEP 3:\nLook at what people are doing.\n\nPeople are using umbrellas.\n\nCONCLUSION:\n\n\ud83c\udf27\ufe0f The weather is rainy.",null,null,!1)
B.aZ7=new A.a6("GUIDED EXAMPLE \u2014 IS IT WINDY? \ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan","You cannot see air.\n\nBut you notice:\n\n\ud83c\udf43 leaves blowing;\n\n\ud83d\udea9 a flag waving quickly;\n\n\ud83c\udf33 small branches moving.\n\nWhat does this tell you?\n\nThe air is moving.\n\nSo the weather is:\n\n\ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f Windy",null,null,!1)
B.aZF=new A.a6("SECTION 13 \u2014 WEATHER OR SEASON? \ud83e\udd14","",B.e,"cyan",'Weather describes conditions over a short time.\n\nFor example:\n\n"Today is rainy."\n\n"Tomorrow may be sunny."\n\nA season lasts much longer.\n\nDifferent places have different seasonal patterns.\n\nFor Grade 1, remember:\n\nWEATHER:\nwhat the air and sky are like now or over a short time.\n\nSEASON:\na longer part of the year with common weather patterns.\n\nDo not mix up weather with seasons.',null,null,!1)
B.b0B=new A.a6("SECTION 14 \u2014 WEATHER SAFETY \ud83d\udee1\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan","Weather can be fun to observe, but safety is important.\n\nHOT AND SUNNY WEATHER \u2600\ufe0f\n\nDrink water.\n\nTake breaks in shade.\n\nFollow adult guidance for sun protection.\n\nRAINY WEATHER \ud83c\udf27\ufe0f\n\nWalk carefully on wet surfaces.\n\nStay away from fast-moving floodwater.\n\nNever play in flooded areas.\n\nTHUNDERSTORMS \u26c8\ufe0f\n\nGo inside a safe building.\n\nStay away from open areas.\n\nFollow adult instructions.\n\nSTRONG WIND \ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f\n\nStay away from loose objects or branches that may fall.\n\nMove to a safe place if adults tell you to.",null,null,!1)
B.aZA=new A.a6("Picture guide","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-4-safety",null,!1)
B.aYT=new A.a6("SECTION 15 \u2014 WEATHER CAN AFFECT LIVING THINGS \ud83c\udf31\ud83d\udc3e","",B.e,"cyan","Weather affects plants, animals, and people.\n\nPlants may receive water from rain. \ud83c\udf31\ud83d\udca7\n\nAnimals may look for shade when it is very hot. \ud83d\udc15\ud83c\udf33\n\nBirds may seek shelter during strong rain. \ud83d\udc26\n\nPeople choose clothing and activities based on weather.\n\nWeather is part of the environment around living things.",null,null,!1)
B.b_V=new A.a6("ACTIVITY \u2014 WHAT WEATHER DO YOU SEE? \ud83d\udc40","",B.e,"cyan","Imagine these scenes.\n\n\nSCENE 1\n\nBright Sun\nBlue sky\nVery few clouds\n\nWeather:\n\u2600\ufe0f Sunny\n\n\nSCENE 2\n\nMany gray clouds\nWater falling\nUmbrellas open\n\nWeather:\n\ud83c\udf27\ufe0f Rainy\n\n\nSCENE 3\n\nFlag waving\nLeaves blowing\nBranches moving\n\nWeather:\n\ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f Windy\n\n\nSCENE 4\n\nDark clouds\nHeavy rain\nThunder and lightning\n\nWeather:\n\u26c8\ufe0f Stormy",null,null,!1)
B.aYv=new A.a6("Picture guide","",B.e,"cyan",null,"science-4-sort",null,!1)
B.aZB=new A.a6("ACTIVITY \u2014 WHAT SHOULD YOU BRING? \ud83c\udf92","",B.e,"cyan","RAINY DAY \ud83c\udf27\ufe0f\n\nUseful:\n\u2614 Umbrella\n\n\nSUNNY HOT DAY \u2600\ufe0f\n\nUseful:\n\ud83e\udde2 Hat\n\ud83d\udca7 Water\n\n\nCOOL DAY \ud83c\udf43\n\nUseful:\n\ud83e\udde5 Jacket\n\n\nSTORMY DAY \u26c8\ufe0f\n\nBest choice:\n\ud83c\udfe0 Stay safely indoors and follow adult instructions.",null,null,!1)
B.aZ6=new A.a6("COMMON MISTAKES \u26a0\ufe0f","",B.e,"cyan",'MISTAKE 1:\n\n"Cloudy always means it is raining."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nA day can be cloudy without rain.\n\n\nMISTAKE 2:\n\n"If I cannot see the wind, it is not there."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nWind is moving air.\n\nWe can observe what it moves.\n\n\nMISTAKE 3:\n\n"Weather stays the same all day."\n\n\u274c Not always.\n\nWeather can change during the day.\n\n\nMISTAKE 4:\n\n"Every sunny day has no clouds."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nA sunny day can still have some clouds.\n\n\nMISTAKE 5:\n\n"Thunderstorms are safe to play outside in."\n\n\u274c Not true.\n\nThunder and lightning can be dangerous.\n\nWe should go to a safe indoor place and follow adult instructions.',null,null,!1)
B.b_W=new A.a6("QUICK CHECK \ud83e\udde0","",B.e,"cyan","1. THE SUN IS BRIGHT AND THE SKY IS MOSTLY CLEAR.\n\nWeather:\nSunny \u2600\ufe0f\n\nWhy?\nThere is plenty of sunlight and few clouds.\n\n\n2. WATER IS FALLING FROM CLOUDS.\n\nWeather:\nRainy \ud83c\udf27\ufe0f\n\nWhy?\nRain is falling from the sky.\n\n\n3. LEAVES AND FLAGS ARE MOVING QUICKLY.\n\nWeather:\nWindy \ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f\n\nWhy?\nMoving air is pushing them.\n\n\n4. WHICH TOOL MEASURES TEMPERATURE?\n\nAnswer:\nThermometer \ud83c\udf21\ufe0f\n\nWhy?\nA thermometer measures how warm or cold the air is.\n\n\n5. YOU HEAR THUNDER OUTSIDE.\n\nWhat should you do?\n\nAnswer:\nGo to a safe indoor place. \ud83c\udfe0\n\nWhy?\nThunderstorms can be dangerous.",null,null,!0)
B.aZr=new A.a6("KEY CONCEPT \u2b50","",B.e,"cyan","TITLE:\n\nWeather tells us what the air and sky are like.\n\nBODY:\n\nWeather can be sunny, cloudy, rainy, windy, stormy, warm, cool, and more.\n\nWe can observe the sky, rain, wind, and temperature to describe weather.\n\nWeather can change, so we observe it regularly.",null,null,!1)
B.aZm=new A.a6("FINAL RECAP \ud83c\udf1f","",B.e,"cyan","Remember:\n\n\u2600\ufe0f Sunny weather has plenty of sunlight.\n\n\u2601\ufe0f Cloudy weather has many clouds.\n\n\ud83c\udf27\ufe0f Rainy weather has water falling from clouds.\n\n\ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f Windy weather has moving air.\n\n\u26c8\ufe0f Stormy weather may bring heavy rain, strong wind, thunder, or lightning.\n\n\ud83c\udf21\ufe0f Temperature tells us how warm or cold the air is.\n\n\ud83d\udd04 Weather can change during the day.\n\n\ud83d\udc55 Weather helps us choose suitable clothing.\n\n\ud83d\udee1\ufe0f We should follow safety rules during dangerous weather.",null,null,!1)
